tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:5719:     "note": "*** RULED `#524` NOT \"AWAITING COMMIT ONLY\", WHICH IS WHAT THE MAIN LOOP HAD RECORDED AND TOLD THE OWNER. ***  Three grounds, each checked against the tree rather than argued: (1) codex's 12:44 repairpass says *\"FLOOR BREACH FOUND; the repair correctly detects M_MASK1 but does not close the repair chain\"*, the 13:06 class-closure repair followed, and **NO PASS HAS BEEN FIRED ON THAT DIFF** -- pass A found M_MASK1, pass B found M_MASK7/M_PBITCOND, and the chain closes only on the first VALID pass finding ZERO breaches, so pass C is owed.  (2) This row carried **no `witness` field and no `sev`** in the working tree AND at HEAD, newest entry 2026-08-29, and `check_witness.py:162` bites at `closed` -- it would have bitten AT COMMIT.  (3) Today's panel was cited only in row-level `note` fields and `check_seats_read.py:118` scans `entries`, so **section G could not see it at all** -- the 2026-08-24 failure repeating.  Also found both uncommitted CHANGELOG blocks still carrying a discharged *\"STILL OWED: promote the probe\"* (L4, both corrected) and noted `RESTORED 17/32` equals HEAD by the pinned-baseline design, which a reader will misread as a broken clearance.  ORDER RULED: `#524` first, then `#525` -- flipping the earlier ruling, whose warrant expired when `#524` went gate-green."
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:5894:   "note": "*** COMMITTED 2026-09-12 as 4dfc716 (local only).  gate 14 PASS 910 checks at the 43 pin (regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/gate_arm_43rows.log); all four trees cleared both parts after cpu_arm_instr.c was propagated (three trees held an older comment).  Pass-2 receipt in regress/pass2_ledger.tsv.  State stays `held`: the closure records are owed. ***\n\n*** GATE PIN RAISED 39 -> 43 (2026-09-12).  THE GATE MUST BE RE-RUN; ITS LAST GREEN WAS AT 39. ***\n\nTable 7 added four rows to close the floor -- the three store-side modes that had NO ROW AT ALL\n(`ARM_STM_IB_R7`, `ARM_STM_DA_R7`, `ARM_STM_S_R7`) and the tenth cell (`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7`) -- so\nthe probe is 43 rows and the literal moved by hand with its reason.  **A pin that follows the probe\ncannot tell a probe that GAINED rows from one that LOST them**, which is why it is a literal.\n\nOWED FOR `#524`, IN ORDER:\n  1. re-run `gate_arm.sh` at the 43 pin;\n  2. re-add the `arm_bdt_probe.py` MANIFEST entry to `check_probe_wiring.py` **in the same commit\n     as the probe** -- the exact line is preserved in a comment there.  The entry without the file\n     is a GHOST; the file without the entry is UNCLASSIFIED.  Both are red, in opposite\n     directions, and I hit each in turn today;\n  3. restore the `#524` CHANGELOG block (`python _scratchpad/land_shared.py round524`) and record\n     table 7 in it -- the block currently describes the closure at 39 rows;\n  4. commit, AFTER `#525`.\n\n---\n\n*** TABLE 7 CLOSES THE CHAIN: ALL TEN FLOOR CELLS KILL, AND THE ONLY ESCAPE IS ONE THE CONTRACT\nNAMED IN ADVANCE.  2026-09-12 02:32. ***  Cited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911h_floor/SCORES.txt\n\n    BASE            43/43  CONTROL=OK  FAILING none\n    -- regression, the six from table g: all still die --\n    M_POPP_NONE     41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_POPP_WEAK  41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2\n    M_NPMASK        41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2         M_SBIT_NONE  (dies)\n    M_POPW_WEAK     40/43  DB_P2, S_P2, MULTI_P2_R1          M_POPU_WEAK  (dies)\n    -- the six NEW floor mutants: ALL DIE --\n    M_PUSHS_NONE    42/43  ARM_STM_S_R7          <- E1, the MEASURED L2 breach\n    M_POPP_MASK2    41/43  IB_P2, DB_P2          <- the ~2 gap, live until residue 7\n    M_PUSH_IB_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7         <- a mode with NO ROW AT ALL\n    M_PUSH_DA_WEAK  42/43  ARM_STM_DA_R7         <- likewise\n    M_PUSHPC_NONE   42/43  ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7      <- the tenth cell\n    M_PUSHP_OVER7   41/43  ARM_STM_IB_R7, ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7   <- E4\n    -- disclosed under clause (e), EXPECTED to escape --\n    D_PUW_WEAK      43/43  FAILING none          <- ESCAPED, exactly as disclosed\n    D_POPSW_WEAK    42/43  ARM_LDM_S_P2          <- DIED, better than disclosed\n    RESTORE         cmp-vs-SEC CLEAN, binary FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED (both parts)\n\n**`D_POPSW_WEAK` dying is the contract being CONSERVATIVE rather than wrong**: upgrading `S_P2` to\n`LDMIA r0!,{r1}^` at residue 7 closed the (s&&w) joint for free, which the PRETABLE predicted.\n\n*** WHAT MAKES THIS TABLE DIFFERENT FROM THE SIX BEFORE IT IS WHERE THE ROWS CAME FROM. ***  It is\nthe first whose row set is enumerated from the HELPERS' OWN INPUTS rather than from the mutant\nlist -- which is what the class-closure rule always said, and what pass C invoked but did not do.\nEvery earlier repair rowed at the residue of the mutant it had just found, so each pass\nnecessarily discovered the next strength.  **Residue 7 (mod 8) discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` AND\n`~7` from ONE access**, so no future strength needs a new residue.\n\nThe `pc` row also settled a question its designing seat left open: it could not establish\nunconditional cold-path reachability from source (it depends on `host_store[0x21] == NULL` and on\nthe decoder not substituting an optimised handler).  **`ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7` is GREEN on BASE, so the\ncold path was taken** -- the measurement answered what the reading could not.\n\n---\n\n*** THE FLOOR IS TEN CELLS, DERIVED FROM THE HELPERS' INPUTS -- NOT FROM THE MUTANT LIST. ***\nAdjudicated 2026-09-11; full text `regress/artifacts/armbdt/passD/fable_floor_20260911.md`.\n\n    FLOOR = {cold first access} x {pop, push} x {IA, IB, DA, DB}\n          + {push} x {s = 1}\n          + {push} x {pc in list}          = TEN CELLS, SEVEN OPEN\n    one cold DATA row per cell, access address = 7 (mod 8)\n\n**RESIDUE 7 MOD 8 IS THE WHOLE GEOMETRIC FIX**: it discriminates `~1`, `~2`, `~3` and `~7` at once,\nso no future strength needs a new residue.  Every previous repair placed its rows at the FOUND\nMUTANT'S residue, which is why each pass found the next one.\n\nOPEN: pop IB / DA / DB for the `~2` strength (all three closure rows sit at `0x20006`, bit 0 CLEAR);\npush IB and push DA (NO ROWS AT ALL -- STM is IA, Thumb PUSH is DB); push s=1 (**E1, MEASURED,\nL2**); push pc-in-list (which also closes E4 for free).\n\n---\n\n*** PASS D DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN EITHER. FIVE MORE ESCAPES, MEASURED AT 39/39. THIS IS THE\nFOURTH CONSECUTIVE PASS TO FIND A BREACH, AND THE COUNT IS RISING: 1, 2, 5, 5. ***\n\n    E1 M_PUSHS_NONE   s_bit ? addr : (addr & ~3)               PUSH only   39/39\n    E2 M_POPSW_WEAK   addr & ((s_bit && w_bit) ? ~1 : ~3)      POP         39/39\n    E3 M_POPS_OVER7   addr & (s_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 POP         39/39\n    E4 M_PUSHP_OVER7  addr & (p_bit ? ~7 : ~3)                 PUSH only   39/39\n    E5 M_PUW_WEAK     addr & ((p&&u&&w) ? ~1 : ~3)             BOTH        39/39\n\n**THE SHAPE IS THE SAME EVERY TIME AND IT IS MY CLOSURE'S OWN SHAPE: I CLOSED THE LOAD SIDE, AT ONE\nRESIDUE, AT ONE STRENGTH.**  E1 and E4 are the PUSH-side halves; E3 is the same axis at a\nBIT2-SET residue; E2 and E5 are joints.  Pass C closed `{site}` for the p axis and left `s`; pass D\nfinds `s` at the other site, and `p` at the other strength.\n\nGOVERNING FACT, CHECKED NOT ASSUMED: **all four bits are used in CONDITIONS by BOTH bodies** --\n`arm_pop` has `if (s_bit)`, `if (p_bit)`, `if (!p_bit)`, `u_bit? :` and `w_bit`; `arm_push`\nlikewise.  So they are BODY-TESTED PARTITIONS and the flagship's `xscaleinten` ruling today (a\nparameter that touches nothing the body reads is clause (e)) does NOT rescue them.\n\n**PROPOSED SPLIT, PUT TO THE ADJUDICATOR RATHER THAN DECIDED HERE:** single-axis cells (E1, E3, E4)\nare FLOOR, L2, close in-round; multi-axis JOINTS (E2, E5) are clause-(e) DEBT on the pass-11\nprecedent.  If that holds, the remaining floor is `{p,u,s,w} x {pop,push} x {none, ~1, ~7}` minus\nwhat is rowed -- finite.  **The second question asked of the adjudicator is whether this chain is\nCONVERGING AT ALL, or has become the `#392` shape wearing the class-closure rule's clothes.**\n\n*** ONE OF MY SEVEN CLOSURE ROWS IS VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, and the reason is sharper than the\n'kills nothing' I disclosed: `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2` CANNOT fail under a slow-path mutant, because\nTHE FIRST COLD ACCESS WARMS THE PAGE and the second register's access takes the FAST PATH. ***\nMeasured by kimi; glm independently called the table *\"internally impossible as stated\"* from the\nsame evidence without knowing why.  **A reading seat and a measuring seat converging on one defect\nfrom opposite ends is the panel working as designed.**\n\n---\n\n*** GATE GREEN AT THE NEW 39-ROW PIN, 2026-09-11 19:4x. ***  `/tmp/gate_arm_524g.log` (100,681 b),\nrun UNPIPED with no timeout wrapper (a pipe makes the exit status the filter's; a timeout kills the\ngate while reporting a clean completion -- both are documented traps here).\n\n    GATE_EXIT=0\n    ok   bdt rows run       39      <- the class-closure pin, moved 32 -> 39 by hand\n    ok   bdt rows correct   39\n    ^FAIL rows: 0     SKIP occurrences: 0     <- no silent coverage loss\n    gate 14: ARM/Thumb flags (#311/#312/#319-#322/#328/#329): PASS (910 checks)\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE COMMIT: **PASS D on the closure diff.**  Passes A, B and C each found a breach\n(one, then two, then five), so the class closure owes its own pass -- and the chain closes only on\nthe first VALID pass finding ZERO breaches **with its denominator**.  A bare zero does not close\nit.\n\n---\n\n*** THE CLASS CLOSURE IS MEASURED AND ALL FIVE PASS-C ESCAPES ARE DEAD. 2026-09-11 23:37. ***\nCited: regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911g_closure/SCORES.txt  (frozen 39-row probe, one instrument for every leg)\n\n    LEG            was      now      killed by\n    BASE                    39/39    -- CONTROL=OK, FAILING none\n    M_POPP_NONE    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPP_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_POPW_WEAK    32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_DB_P2, ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R1\n    M_NPMASK       32/32    37/39    ARM_LDM_IB_P2, ARM_LDM_DB_P2\n    M_SBIT_NONE    32/32    38/39    ARM_LDM_S_P2\n    RESTORE                          cmp-vs-SEC=CLEAN, binary=FRESH, SENTINEL CLEARED\n\n**Every mutant that was a TOTAL ESCAPE now reds with NAMED rows, and BASE is clean at 39/39.**\nSeven rows closed the load side of the p/u/s/w x site product: `ARM_LDM_IB_P2` (p=1,u=1),\n`ARM_LDM_DA_P2` (u=0), `ARM_LDM_DB_P2` (p=1,u=0), `ARM_LDM_S_P2` (s=1), and the three-assertion\n`ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_*` leg.  **The multi leg is the bargain: ONE session, THREE assertions, closing\nw_bit x residue-2 AND register-list-length together.**  It had to sit at residue 2 -- at the\nexisting `WB_UNAL2` base (0x20001/0x20005) the low bits are `01`, where `~1` and `~3` AGREE, so\nthat row is structurally blind to a w_bit-conditioned weakening.\n\n*** THREE OF THE SEVEN NEW ROWS KILL NOTHING IN THIS TABLE, AND THAT IS RECORDED RATHER THAN\nGLOSSED: `ARM_LDM_DA_P2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2`, `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_WB`. ***  Their channels are\nreal but no BUILT mutant varies them -- `DA_P2` exists for the `u_bit=0` cell, which the pass-C\nseat ENUMERATED and explicitly did NOT build (*\"derived (same legs)\"*).  A condemning prediction\ngets built, so `M_POPU_WEAK` -- `addr & (u_bit ? 0xfffffffc : 0xfffffffe)` -- WAS BUILT AND\nMEASURED: **37/39, FAILING ARM_LDM_DA_P2 and ARM_LDM_DB_P2.**  `DA_P2` is therefore\nDISCHARGED BY A MEASURED KILL and the u_bit cell is closed rather than derived.\n*Six mutants built, six dead.*  The other two are secondary assertions on a leg whose primary assertion\nalready kills; they are disclosed under clause (e), not claimed as coverage.\n\nTHE REPAIR-CHAIN STATUS: passes A, B and C each found a breach (M_MASK1; M_MASK7 + M_PBITCOND;\nthen five at once).  **This diff is the CLASS CLOSURE the rule demands after two consecutive\npasses of one class -- the full enumeration in ONE diff rather than another point row -- and it\nowes its own pass.  A zero does not close the chain unless it carries its denominator; the\ndenominator is the table above plus the enumeration the pass-C seat swept (mask value, placement,\nsite, predicate x 2 sites x 4 bits x 2 strengths = 16 cells, operand, joint (p,u), reg-list\nlength, byte order, null medium).**\n\n---\n\n*** PASS C DID NOT CLOSE THE CHAIN. 2026-09-11 17:52. FOUR SEATS OF SIX FOUND A CLAUSE-(d)\nESCAPE; TWO DID NOT.  `#524` DOES NOT COMMIT. ***\n\n**`M_POP_PBITCOND` -- found INDEPENDENTLY by codex and grok**, the read-side counterpart of the\nmeasured `M_PBITCOND`:\n    /*  at the arm_pop() read call, fix present at BOTH sites, then:  */\n    addr & (p_bit ? 0xfffffffe : 0xfffffffc)\n\n**Predicted 32/32, a TOTAL SWEEP**, because every tested load carries p_bit=0 -- established by\nDECODING the probe's literal instruction words (`0xe8900002`, `0xe8b00002`, `0xe8b00006`, all IA;\nThumb `0xbc02`), not by assertion.\n\n*** THIS IS THE `#499` SHAPE FOR THE THIRD TIME IN ONE DAY, AND THE FLAGSHIP ADJUDICATION PREDICTED\nIT HOURS EARLIER: *\"Each pass today found a new cell; extrapolate.\"* ***  Pass A found `M_MASK1`;\npass B found `M_MASK7`/`M_PBITCOND`; pass C finds the SYMMETRIC CELL OF PASS B'S OWN REPAIR -- the\nclass closure repaired the store-side cell `M_PBITCOND` used and left the load-side open.\n\n**grok named the enumeration that was missing, in one sentence:** *\"Mask-width (~1 / ~7 / ~15) is\nCLOSED; the predicate x site product is NOT.\"*  So the owed closure is\n**{unconditional, p_bit-conditioned} x {arm_pop, arm_push}** in ONE diff, not another single cell --\nthe class-closure rule, which pass B already invoked and applied to only one axis.\n\n**AN EQUIVALENT-MUTANT TRAP INSIDE THE MUTANT, from grok:** the form must be truthy `p_bit ?`, NOT\n`p_bit == 1`.  `bdt_load` passes `iw & 0x01000000`, so `== 1` is DEAD on every ARM encoding and\nwould measure EQUIVALENT for the wrong reason.\n\n*** IT IS A PREDICTION AND IS RECORDED AS OWED, NOT AS A SCORE. ***  Both seats said so plainly --\ngrok: *\"I could not exec a scratch rebuild (WSL was blocked in this seat)... treat the 32/32 as a\npredicted sweep that still owes a clone run.\"*  Per the flagship ruling on predictions: **a\nCONDEMNING prediction gets BUILT FIRST** (a wrong condemnation costs one build; a wrong excuse ships\na hole).  Build `M_POP_PBITCOND`, score it, and if it sweeps that is **L2 -- repair in-round**.\n\nOpus 5 was fired on the same brief and is still running; agy deliberately not fired.\n\n---\n\n*** 2026-09-11 INVESTIGATION -- **CONFIRMED AT HEAD, AND THE SPLICE IS TRACED TO THE memcpy, NOT ASSERTED.  The row is right in every particular and the artifact genuinely does not exist.** ***\n\n**THE MECHANISM, read at HEAD (`cpu_arm_instr.c`, unmodified in `git status`):**\n  * `arm_pop()` `:1602-1764` and `arm_push()` `:1770-1859` each hold one running `addr = *np`, the RAW base-register value, and branch on host page residency per register.\n  * **FAST path** (page resident): `p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]` -- the `>>2` truncates the low two bits, so it is **architecturally correct** for the implemented A=0/U=0 state (DDI0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190: *ignore the least significant two bits of the address*).\n  * **SLOW path** (cold): `cpu->memory_rw(..., addr, data, 4, ...)` with the **RAW, UNMASKED addr** (`:1653-1654`, `:1842-1843`).  **Traced into `memory_rw.c:728-782`: `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity, NOT word-rounded, and the transfer is a raw `memcpy` at `:770-772`/`:780-782`.  So an unaligned addr genuinely SPLICES BYTES ACROSS AN ARCHITECTURAL WORD BOUNDARY** -- confirmed at the lowest level rather than inferred.\n\n> **The same instruction, the same unaligned base, produces two different byte patterns purely according to HOST PAGE-CACHE WARMTH -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n*** AND THE WRITEBACK IS CORRECT AS IT STANDS, WHICH IS WHY THE FIX SHAPE MATTERS: *** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` uses the unmasked running addr, and **Mode 4 writeback DERIVES from unmasked Rn**.  **So the repair must mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites and NEVER at entry** -- masking at entry corrupts writeback.\n\n**THE IN-TREE PRECEDENT FOR EXACTLY THAT SHAPE ALREADY EXISTS:** `src/cpus/generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;` -- the code-generated fast dispatch already masks the ACCESS while leaving writeback unmasked.  *A transferred warrant, verified at the cited line.*\n\n**FOUR CALLERS, all confirmed:** `X(bdt_load)` `:1889`, `X(bdt_store)` `:1921`, and Thumb PUSH/POP at `cpu_arm.c:1670`/`:1677`.  **Thumb inherits the defect and has ZERO probe coverage of any kind.**\n\n=== NO ARTIFACT EXISTS.  SEARCHED BY THE DEFECT'S VOCABULARY, NEVER BY GUESSING A FILENAME ===\n*(the brief warned against filename invention because I did it twice today; the search was done by function names and mnemonics instead)*\n  * filename patterns `*armbdt*`, `*bdt*`, `*block*transfer*`, `*blockdata*`: **0 hits**\n  * **127** artifact directories: none for this item\n  * content-grep of all **93** `regress/*.py` for LDM/STM mnemonics: **2 files**, both testing something else\n  * `bdt_load|bdt_store|arm_pop|arm_push`: **1 hit**, a comment\n  * `CHANGELOG.md`: **0** occurrences -- **no round has ever shipped against it**\n\n**GATE 14's 358 IS CORRECT AND CURRENT**, confirmed two independent ways -- `gate_arm.sh:561-563`'s comment AND commit `6014b7e`'s message recording the gate's own run output.  *(The carrier records this figure was stale once at 261, so both were treated as claims.)*  **And it does not cover this defect under any name:**\n  * `arm_writeback_probe.py` scopes itself explicitly to *the single-register path* -- a different generator entirely;\n  * `arm_endian_probe.py` DOES reach `arm_pop`/`arm_push`, but only on byte order and **with a word-aligned base (`0x10000`), where masking is a no-op** -- so it cannot discriminate this defect whichever path a row takes;\n  * measured corroboration: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 identical** on `arm_writeback_probe.py`, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback**;\n  * and the regress adjudication of `6014b7e` states it plainly -- *gate_arm passed 16/16 WITH armbdt PRESENT.*\n\n*** THE MANDATORY ROW: a WRITEBACK row threading an unaligned base through a W=1 transfer and asserting the base register equals the UNMASKED arithmetic result. ***  It is the row `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to, and **the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant.**\n\n**CITATION DRIFT, noted rather than silently fixed:** the 2026-08-19 entries cite `:1563`/`:1752`; HEAD has the same code at `:1653`/`:1842` -- about 90 lines, from unrelated intervening edits.  The mechanism is unchanged; only the numbers moved.\n\n**STILL LIVE AT HEAD**, checked from source rather than from the row: `git log -10` on that file shows the recent touches are all unrelated to the `memory_rw` calls.\n\n*Investigated by a SONNET subagent while both Anthropic seats were walled -- see [[agentmodelwall]].  The work is of the right standard for a READING task, which is the half of that row's claim this supports; it says nothing about measuring equivalence.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 WITNESS + KILL TABLE (agy 3.8, measuring seat, independent transport).  **THE WITNESS EXISTS.  RUNG 3, UNMODIFIED testarm, 17/25 PRE-FIX AND 25/25 POST-FIX -- after three weeks in which this row's own record said the artifact genuinely did not exist.** ***\n\n**THE DEFECT IS UNCHANGED AND IS NOW MEASURED RATHER THAN DERIVED.**  The fast path (`p32[(addr & 0xfff) >> 2]`) truncates the low two bits and is architecturally CORRECT per DDI 0100I LDM(1) p.A4-37 / STM(1) p.A4-190; the slow path passes the **RAW, UNMASKED `addr`** to `memory_rw`, whose `offset = paddr & offset_mask` is PAGE-granularity and whose transfer is a raw `memcpy`.  **So the identical instruction at the identical unaligned base yields DIFFERENT BYTES according to host page-cache warmth -- a condition invisible to the guest architecture.**\n\n=== THE WITNESS, AND IT CLEARS EVERY LADDER CLAUSE ===\n**Rung 3, cold interactive debugger, on the UNMODIFIED in-tree `testarm`** (`gxemul -V -A -T -E testarm -M 64`) driven through a pty.  **No `device_add`, no custom machine -- which is the laundering clause, and it is met by construction rather than by assertion.**\n\n*** THE PAGE-CACHE CONDITIONING IS THE PART THAT MAKES IT A WITNESS AT ALL, and it turns on a fact this project got BACKWARDS once and had two shipped rounds leaning on the wrong version: *** `put b` writes byte-wise with `CACHE_NONE | NO_EXCEPTIONS`, populating physical RAM **without** updating the translation tables, so `host_load`/`host_store` stay NULL -- the COLD seed; `put w` writes words with `CACHE_DATA` and **WARMS** the host page -- the WARM seed.  *The carrier records `put w` WARMS and `put b` does not; the witness uses exactly that asymmetry as its instrument.*\n\n**AND IT VERIFIES THE PATH RATHER THAN ASSUMING IT:** before every transfer the probe runs `tlbdump 0,r` to confirm the target is genuinely ABSENT from the host cache on a cold run and RESIDENT on a warm one.  **That is the row establishing which path each run took -- without it the core comparison proves nothing**, which is the one thing the brief insisted on.\n\n=== SCORES, and the artifact for every one of them is named ===\n*(`regress/artifacts/armbdt/killtable_20260911/`, `KILLTABLE_DONE` sentinel present, each score read from its own `probe_<name>.raw`)*\n\n| leg | score | reading |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (fix applied) | **25/25** | clean |\n| `HEAD` (committed, pre-fix) | **17/25** | all four cold-data rows AND all four divergence checks red -- *test-first satisfied: the symptom is shown on the COMMITTED build and goes green post-fix with the same script* |\n| `M_ENTRY` (mask at entry) | **20/25** | *** KILLED, AND KILLED BY THE WRITEBACK ROWS SPECIFICALLY *** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` (one site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` (other site masked) | 21/25 | killed |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` (identity) | **6/25** | 19 rows red -- the rows genuinely observe block transfer, discharging clause (c) |\n| `RESTORED` | **17/25** | *** IDENTICAL TO `HEAD`, which is the clean-restore proof: the clone came back to the committed baseline exactly. *** |\n\n*** `M_ENTRY` IS THE VINDICATION OF THE MANDATORY ROW. ***  The brief said the writeback row *'is the only one that discriminates the correct fix from the plausible-but-wrong mask-at-entry mutant'*, and predicted `arm_writeback_probe.py` is structurally blind to it -- which had already been measured: three binaries (HEAD / mask-at-access / mask-at-entry) all scored **22/22 IDENTICAL** on that probe, **including the entry-mask mutant that corrupts writeback.**  The new instrument kills it 20/25.  *A row specified from the defect's own control flow, before any table ran, and it is the row that carries the whole discrimination.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `PRETABLE.out` written **06:46:22**, first table run `probe_BASE.raw` **06:50:06** -- 3m44s earlier, so the pre-table block cannot have been fitted to the answers.\n\n=== THE FIX SHAPE IS CONFIRMED, AND THE TRAP WITH IT ===\n**Mask ONLY at the two `memory_rw` call sites** -- `addr & ~3` at `cpu_arm_instr.c:1653` and `:1842`.  **NEVER at entry:** `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` (`:1715`, `:1858`) uses the unmasked running `addr`, and Mode 4 writeback derives from unmasked `Rn`, so an entry mask corrupts writeback -- which is precisely what `M_ENTRY` measures.  In-tree precedent verified at the cited line: `generate_arm_multi.c:117` emits `addr &= 0xffc;`, masking the ACCESS while leaving writeback alone.\n\n*** HOUSE RULES OBSERVED, VERIFIED BY ME RATHER THAN TAKEN FROM THE REPORT: `git status` shows `src/cpus/cpu_arm_instr.c` UNMODIFIED in GXEMUL-SEC. ***  The seat worked in `/tmp/gxarmbdt_clone` and wrote only into `regress/artifacts/armbdt/`, which is the measuring-seat rule met exactly.  The live `/tmp/gxop519p2` tail was not touched.\n\n**STATUS: the fix is SPECIFIED AND MEASURED BUT NOT APPLIED TO SEC.**  What remains is the edit itself, propagation into the build trees, a pass-2 panel on the real diff, and the gates -- none of which could be done in this window, because the `#519` regression tail holds the machine and this project's gates carry load-sensitive wall-clock oracles.  *Deliberately left rather than rushed: a measurement taken against a loaded host is the false-FAIL this project has already paid for once.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 IMPLEMENTED.  **FIX APPLIED TO SEC AS `#524`, MASKING THE ACCESS AND NOT THE RUNNING addr** ***\n\n**Round number `#524`, chosen after checking: `#523` is STAKED by `regress/artifacts/wdcerrfamily/codex_design523_20260911.txt` even though it appears in no CHANGELOG entry, source file or commit.**  *An artifact filename is a claim on a number, and taking a staked one would have collided with a round already in design.*\n\n**THE EDIT, both sites, `uint32_t addr` masked with a hex literal to match the file's own idiom (`addr & 0xfff`, `addr &= 0xffc`):**\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_pop()  : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_READ, ...)\n    cpu_arm_instr.c  arm_push() : cpu->memory_rw(..., addr & 0xfffffffc, data, ..., MEM_WRITE, ...)\n\n**THE TWO WRITEBACK SITES WERE VERIFIED UNTOUCHED AFTER THE EDIT** -- `if (w_bit) *np = addr;` still reads the bare unmasked running value at both.  *That is the whole discrimination: an entry-mask scores 20/25 on this round's detector and **22/22 -- identical to a correct build -- on the pre-existing `arm_writeback_probe.py`.*  The comment records that measurement at the site, so a future edit tempted to hoist the mask meets the evidence rather than the rule.\n\n*Other `memory_rw(..., addr, ...)` calls in this file (the single-register load/store paths and the instruction fetch) were left alone -- checked by grep after the edit: 7 remain bare and none is on the block-data-transfer path.*\n\n**PROPAGATION, and it is deliberately PARTIAL:**\n  * `est/` -- **DONE and verified BYTE-IDENTICAL by `cmp`.**  It was confirmed equal to SEC@HEAD BEFORE the copy, so it is genuinely a shared file rather than a silent divergence being overwritten.  *est/ is a SOURCE tree with no binary, so propagating without rebuilding creates no stale-binary trap.*\n  * `build/`, `build-asan/`, `/tmp/gxsec-build` -- **NOT propagated.**  Three codex jobs are in flight and a rebuild is CPU-heavy; **propagating into a build tree without rebuilding is exactly the trap recorded on [[xscaleinten]] this morning**, where all three trees report `FRESH` over a source that is simply the wrong source.  Recorded in `_scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md` so a fresh session finds the debt.\n\n**STILL OWED BEFORE THIS COMMITS: propagate+rebuild the three build trees together, a pass 2 on the real diff, and the targeted gate.**  The witness and the kill table are already done (HEAD 17/25, fix 25/25, `M_ENTRY` 20/25, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 6/25, `RESTORED` = HEAD).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 on the real `#524` diff (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **PASS 2: NOT RATIFIED -- THE MASKS ARE RIGHT AND 'ONLY TWO SITES' WAS WRONG.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: NOT RATIFIED -- the two masks are locally correct, but other BDT implementations still use unrounded addresses, and the architectural justification needs qualification.*\n\n**THE MASK AND THE LITERAL ARE CORRECT.**  `addr & 0xfffffffc` clears exactly bits 1 and 0; the unsuffixed hex literal has type `unsigned int` on ordinary implementations (its value does not fit signed `int`), so the result stays unsigned and widens correctly into `memory_rw`'s `uint64_t` parameter.  *A wider host pointer does not change this.*\n\n**FOR AN ALIGNED BASE BOTH CHANGES ARE STRICT NO-OPS** -- every running-address adjustment is a multiple of four, so an initially aligned `addr` stays aligned **including across 32-bit wraparound**, and old and new arguments are identical at both call sites.\n\n=== *** MY 'ONLY TWO SITES ON THE BDT PATH' PREMISE WAS WRONG, AND THE COUNTEREXAMPLES ARE TWO HANDLERS THAT REPLACE LDM/STM OUTRIGHT *** ===\n*'yes for the generic helpers' `memory_rw` calls; no for all implementations of the BDT access rule.'*  **Verified here before filing, all three quotes read from the file:**\n  * **`netbsd_memset`** replaces STM instructions and does **`memset(page + (addr & 0xfff), 0, 128);`** at `:2127` -- raw page offset, no word rounding.  Its runtime guards check page bounds, zero source values and page availability; **none rejects an unaligned address.**\n  * **`netbsd_memcpy`** replaces LDM/STM and copies through raw `& 0xfff` source AND destination offsets (`:2274-2275`), **while its register-publication reads use `& 0xffc`** -- *two different address rules inside one handler.*  Its own comment at `:2252` already acknowledges *'the overlap and unaligned-copy divergences'*.\n  * Their matchers recognise instruction patterns **without establishing address alignment**, and the combined bodies **bypass both edited calls entirely.**\n\n**These predate the `#524` hunks, so they are FILED rather than fixed in-round** per the stopping rule -- [[armbdtcombined]].  *But they mean the round may not claim to have closed the class, only the generic helpers.*\n\n**AND MY CLASSIFICATION OF THE SEVEN REMAINING CALLS WAS WRONG IN DETAIL WHILE RIGHT IN CONCLUSION** -- the count is correct and *'none is a missed generic LDM/STM call'*, but they are not 'generated single-register' calls: `1498/1521` are SWP (already word-rounded at `:1497`), `1540/1547` are SWPB (one-byte, **low bits legitimately select the byte**), `1982/2042` are LDREX/STREX (unaligned rejected before the call), `4009` is the instruction fetch.\n\n=== THUMB: THE FIX REACHES IT, AND A DIFFERENT DEFECT DOES NOT ===\n*'Every transferred register in either helper goes through the same aligned host-word access or the newly masked slow call.  There is no Thumb-specific raw-address branch inside either transfer loop.'*  **So Thumb callers that enter these helpers receive the access fix automatically.**\n\n*Coverage unverified because `cpu_arm.c` was outside the permitted set -- my omission again, the second brief today to exclude the file its own question needed.*  The seat declined to certify the PUSH/POP call sites, whether Thumb LDMIA/STMIA use these helpers, or the reported absence of Thumb probes.\n\n> *** AND IT NAMED A SEPARATE THUMB DEFECT THAT MY PATCH DOES NOT TOUCH: the helper's PC handling CLEARS BOTH LOW PC BITS and calls the ARM pointer-update routine, while DDI0100I's Thumb POP (`ddi0100i.txt:24256`) PRESERVES THE HALFWORD ADDRESS and specifies interworking. ***  An external caller might compensate; that is not establishable from these files.  **Unchanged by `#524` and not claimed by it.**\n\n=== TWO QUALIFICATIONS TO THE ARCHITECTURAL WARRANT, both of which I had not considered ===\n  * **THE HELPERS ARE NOT RESTRICTED TO RAM.**  An unaligned address can reach DEVICE DISPATCH, and *'rounding can change a device-relative offset or even the selected mapping near a device boundary'* -- device selection uses the translated address before the RAM-copy path.\n  * **`ddi0100i.txt:3256` (A2-42) specifies additional UNPREDICTABLE behaviour for ARMv6 unaligned multiword accesses to Device or Strongly Ordered memory**, so *'the unconditional claim that all callers simply ignore those bits is too broad.'*\n\n**AND THE EDIT ROUNDS THE VIRTUAL ADDRESS, BEFORE TRANSLATION** -- `memory_rw` splits translated accesses at **1 KB** (`MRW_SPLIT_MASK == 0x3ff`) while its RAM worker uses a 4 KB offset mask for ARM -- *'the old call can touch, and fault on, a page that the corrected call never accesses.'*  **That is a real behavioural difference beyond the fix**, and it is the intended one (the architecture says ignore the low bits), but it was not in the round's stated reasoning and is recorded now rather than discovered later.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL CONVENED on the combined `#524`/`#525` packet, and **SEAT LEDGER FOR THE #524/#525 PANEL -- THREE SEATS DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, BY NAME** ***\n\n**FIRED: the eight scriptable transports** via `bash GXEMUL-SEC/tools/panel.sh <absolute brief>` -- codex GPT-6 Astra (max), grok 4.6 (xhigh), kimi 3, and the Ollama cloud seats glm-5.3, deepseek-v4-pro, minimax-m3 (+ the kimi-k3 substitute if configured).  **All four Ollama transports were confirmed REACHABLE by `api/tags` beforehand**, so a non-answer from any of them is a seat failure rather than a dead daemon.\n\n=== *** DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED -- NAMED, BECAUSE A SEAT HELD BACK AND A SEAT THAT FAILED ARE DIFFERENT THINGS AND CONFLATING THEM IS HOW A TWO-SEAT REVIEW GETS REPORTED AS A PANEL *** ===\n  * **agy 3.8** -- RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by standing owner ruling, and it is the reason both of these rounds have witnesses at all: it built the `armbdt` witness + kill table (25/25) and the `wdcaltdigin` witness + kill table (16/16) earlier today.  *Spending its bounded budget on a reading seven other seats can do is spending the roster's only independent-transport measuring capability on a fungible job.*\n  * **Opus 5** -- HELD.  It answers through the Agent tool and consumes the same Anthropic quota the owner wants preserved for the adjudication.  *Not a failure, not agreement: a budget decision.*\n  * **Fable 5** -- HELD BY EXPLICIT OWNER SEQUENCING: *'don't run fable until all other panel seats have had a say.'*  **This panel is the 'all other seats' step.**  Its availability is to be checked by a plain subagent rather than by Opus when the time comes.\n\n**WHY THE PACKET IS SHAPED AS IT IS:** every fact is INLINED -- both diffs, both full enclosing contexts, both measured kill tables, and **both first-pass NOT-RATIFIED findings** -- because the Ollama transports cannot read files and a brief containing *'check the source'* is a documented way to turn a live seat into a stall that looks exactly like a quota wall.  *The packet also asks the seats to attack the FIRST PASS'S FINDINGS, not only the code, so the corrections get adversarial scrutiny too.*\n\n*** THE LOAD-BEARING QUESTION PUT TO THEM IS THE SELECTOR ONE, and it is imported from a sibling round rather than invented: *** `sgirandomcolour` had **six mutants score a TOTAL ESCAPE** because every detector row probed a single position and each mutant conditioned the repair's effect on a tile or coordinate selector.  **`#524` has 25 rows and `#525` has 16, and neither has had a selector audit.**  The panel is asked to name a conditioning that a plausible edit could produce and the rows could not see.\n\n*Landing remains HELD by owner ruling until the full panel reports -- nothing is committed and no gate has run.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PANEL (`_scratchpad/panel_20260911_103402`).  **SIX of seven scriptable seats answered substantively; the kimi CLI is WALLED and its `kimi-k3:cloud` substitute ANSWERED, so that seat is COVERED, not lost.** ***\n\n**Seat sizes and tails verified individually rather than by size alone:** codex 300,614 b (streams its reasoning; the tail is substantive), grok 11,840 b, `ollama_minimax` 12,053 b, `ollama_glm` 9,695 b, `ollama_kimi` 7,601 b, `ollama_deepseek` 4,219 b.  **`kimi.txt` is 350 b ending `See log: ...kimi-code.log` -- the CLI failure signature, NOT a considered non-answer.**\n\n**CONSENSUS ON THE PRODUCT CODE: NEITHER FIX IS WRONG.**  glm -- *'#524 is architecturally correct and its scope is what was overstated'*; minimax -- *'Both are correct, minimal, and correctly scoped'*; `kimi-k3` -- *'`addr & 0xfffffffc` per-access is provably equivalent'*; deepseek -- *'Neither fix is wrong as a local correction, but #524 is incomplete (combined handlers)'*, **independently reproducing the codex finding about `netbsd_memset`/`netbsd_memcpy`.**\n\n=== *** THE SELECTOR QUESTION GOT REAL ANSWERS, AND THEY ARE FLOOR-BREACH CANDIDATES *** ===\nI asked, importing the shape from [[sgirandomcolour]]: *what conditioning could a plausible edit introduce that the 25 and 16 rows never vary?*\n\n| round | proposed conditioning | seat | predicted |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `#525` | **`if (odata & WDCS_DRDY) odata = 0;`** | grok | **16/16 -- TOTAL ESCAPE** |\n| `#525` | `if (d->unit == 0) odata = 0;` -- a DRIVE-UNIT selector | deepseek | passes if every row uses the primary controller |\n| `#524` | `if (cpu->mem->page_size == 4096) addr &= ~3;` -- a constant property of the test map | deepseek | passes; breaks on any other page size |\n\n**grok also corrected my clause-(d) choice:** deleting `odata = 0` is NOT the discriminating mutant, *because `M_NULL_EFFECT` already scored 14/16* -- the detector sees it.  **The one that escapes keeps the assignment and gates it.**  *That is the difference between a mutant the rows catch and a mutant the rows are blind to, and I had picked the first and called it the floor test.*\n\n**PRESCRIPTION (grok):** *'Product diffs can stand.  The detectors cannot ship as the round's \"we pinned the repair\" claim until `#524` has a `+2` and a `+3` (or an anchored `& 3` mutant) and `#525` has one CORE observation with `WDCS_DRDY` clear.'*  **That is an L3 -- an undischarged obligation, where the row is the sole carrier of something the round claims.**\n\n*** AND A MEASURED-VS-PREDICTED DISCREPANCY IN MY OWN ARTIFACT, caught by grok and verified here: `PRETABLE.out:54` predicts `M_NULL_MEDIUM 4/25`; `SCORES.txt` measures **6/25**. ***  *I cited 6/25 -- the measured one -- so no record is wrong, but the PREDICTION sits in a durable artifact where a future reader can cite it as a measurement.  That is the frozen-number class with a two-row error already in the drawer.*  grok's reading of the remainder: *'PATH/ATTRIB-style controls surviving an early `return`'*.\n\n**Seats DELIBERATELY NOT FIRED, by name: agy** (reserved for measurement; it built both witnesses today), **Opus 5** (held -- same Anthropic quota the adjudication needs), **Fable 5** (held by owner sequencing until every other seat has spoken -- **this panel is that step**).\n\n*** 2026-09-11 FABLE 5 ADJUDICATION (`FABLE_ADJ_20260911`).  **SEAT ALIVE -- token AND a verbatim `CHECKPOINT.md` HEAD quote returned, and it re-ran `git log --oneline -1` itself to confirm the checkpoint is not stale.**  Fired ONLY after all seven other scriptable seats had spoken, per owner sequencing, and via a subagent rather than Opus. ***\n\n=== RULING: **HOLD for ONE MEASUREMENT, then repair IN-ROUND.  Grok MIS-GRADED this: it is not L3, it is CLAUSE (d).** ===\n\n*** IT IS THE CARRIER'S OWN WORKED EXAMPLE -- `#461`'s `m_align1`, a mask narrowed `& 3` -> `& 1`. The fix IS the mask, so a weakened mask is the canonical (d) form, and naming it (d) matters because **a (d) breach cannot be argued around as 'the round does not claim that'.** ***\n\n**CHECKED BY THE ADJUDICATOR, not inferred: every unaligned base in `agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` is `+1`** -- `:218` `0x20001`, `:281` `0x21001`, POP `0x22001`, PUSH `0x23005 -> 0x23001`.  **For every row `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3`, so `0xfffffffc -> 0xfffffffe` at both sites is 25/25 BY ARITHMETIC.**\n\n> **But it must still be BUILT AND SCORED, not asserted.**  Grok said plainly it had not built it, and this project's own rule is that *'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.*  **Build `M_MASK1` on the clone, score it, and when it escapes add a `+2` DATA row for LDM and one for STM**, re-run all legs, and `M_MASK1` must go red.\n\n**`+3` BUYS NOTHING ENUMERABLE and is DROPPED** -- rows are owed per ENUMERATED MUTANT: `~1` dies at `+2`, `~2` already dies at `+1`.  **Disclose residue 3 in the contract instead of rowing it.**  *That is the row-demand bound doing its job against my instinct to add more rows.*\n\n**Operative licence is L1** (nothing has shipped), not L2.\n\n=== *** THE `PRETABLE` 4/25-vs-6/25 GAP IS EXPLAINED, AND THE EXPLANATION IS A REAL DEFECT *** ===\nThe two extra survivors are **`ARM_STM_COLD_CLOBBER` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_CLOBBER`**, found by diffing the row list against the FAILING line.  **A 'neighbour not clobbered' row is an IDENTITY ROW under a medium that writes nothing** -- so `PRETABLE.out`'s claim that *every row genuinely observes block transfer* is **FALSE for those two.**  *The prediction was not merely two off; it rested on a wrong statement about the rows.*\n\n**Write `PRETABLE2.out` for the re-table -- do NOT append to `PRETABLE.out`**: this ledger already records an appended section destroying a PRETABLE's mtime, which is the artifact whose whole evidentiary value is its timestamp.\n\n**TWO L4s IN MY OWN COMMENT, BOTH APPLIED:**\n  1. *'DDI0100I says to ignore the low two bits'* was UNQUALIFIED.  **Verified at `ddi0100i.txt:7954-7959`: that holds for `CP15_reg1_Ubit == 0` AND alignment checking DISABLED; `Ubit == 1` faults on every non-word-aligned access.**  And the fork is internally inconsistent -- `cpu_arm.c:129` SETS `ARM_CONTROL_ALIGN` in the default control register, `:474` only PRINTS it, and **nothing consults it.**  *** THE ROW'S OWN 2026-08-29 fable5 ENTRY ALREADY SAID 'the record must NOT say unqualifiedly' -- so the comment I wrote today broke an obligation this row had already recorded. ***\n  2. *'Measured: ... `arm_writeback_probe.py` scores it 22/22'* -- **`22/22` appears in NO measurement artifact.**  Verified: it occurs only in briefs and seat answers; `probe_M_ENTRY.raw` backs the 20/25 alone.  **Restated BY CONSTRUCTION** (that probe scopes itself to the single-register path and never reaches these helpers), with the word *Measured* removed from it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLES 2 AND 3.  **THE CLAUSE-(d) BREACH IS MEASURED, CLOSED AND RE-MEASURED: M_MASK1 25/25 -> 27/29.** ***\n\n=== TABLE 2 (`killtable_20260911b/`): THE BREACH, MEASURED ===\n**`M_MASK1: 25/25` -- a TOTAL ESCAPE.**  The fix present, its mask weakened `0xfffffffc` -> `0xfffffffe`: the carrier's own `#461 m_align1` shape (`& 3` -> `& 1`), and **the canonical clause-(d) form for this round because the fix IS the mask.**\n\n*Fable predicted it by arithmetic -- every unaligned base the probe drove was `+1`, and `x+1 & ~1 == x+1 & ~3` -- and ruled that it be BUILT anyway, because this project's rule is that 'it would sweep the table' is a MEASUREMENT, never an inference.  It was right to insist: the prediction was correct, and a round that had accepted it unmeasured would have shipped a floor claim it had not tested.*\n\n*** AND TABLE 1's NUMBERS REPRODUCED EXACTLY ON THE REPAIRED DRIVER -- 7 of 7 legs match. ***  **So table 1's MEASUREMENT was sound even though its DRIVER was defective**; what [[killtablebaseline]] cost was reproducibility, not correctness.  *That is worth knowing before discarding anything from it, and it is the opposite of what a defective-driver finding usually implies.*\n\n=== TABLE 3 (`killtable_20260911c/`): THE CLOSURE, RE-MEASURED ===\n\n| leg | score | rows it fails |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` (the fix) | **29/29** | none |\n| `HEAD` | 19/29 | 10 -- the defect rows, including BOTH new `+2` rows |\n| **`M_MASK1`** | **27/29** | *** exactly 2: `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` *** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 24/29 | 5 -- **ALL FIVE WRITEBACK ROWS AND NOTHING ELSE** |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the STM-side rows |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 24/29 | 5 -- the LDM-side MIRROR of those |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/29 | 21, including BOTH control rows |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/29 | = `HEAD`, the clean-restore proof (now against GIT) |\n\n> *** EVERY LEG DIES ON A DISTINCT, SEMANTICALLY CORRECT SET, AND THAT IS THE PART WORTH MORE THAN THE SCORES. ***  `M_ENTRY` fails ONLY writeback rows; `M_POP_ONLY` and `M_PUSH_ONLY` fail mirror-image sets; `M_MASK1` fails ONLY the two rows written for it.  *Compare `wdcaltdigin`, where four legs all scored 14/16 and only the row identity distinguished them -- here the row identity and the score agree, which is what a well-separated instrument looks like.*\n\n**THE CLOSURE IS TIGHT, NOT OVER-ROWED: `M_MASK1` fails exactly TWO rows -- the minimum.**  *Rows are owed per enumerated mutant, and two mutant-killing rows plus their two PATH controls is what the enumeration demanded.*\n\n=== THE PREDICTIONS WERE RIGHT TO THE BYTE, WHICH IS THE CHANNEL RULE WORKING ===\n`PRETABLE2.out` named, before the run, the observable each new row would show under `M_MASK1`:\n  * `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x77881122`, **measured `0x77881122`**;\n  * `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2` -- predicted `0x33440000`, **measured `0x33440000`**.\n\n*The channel line requires naming the mutant a row kills AND the observable that differs under it, at writing time, before any table runs.  Both were named and both were exact.*\n\n**ORDER PROVEN BY MTIME:** `PRETABLE2.out` 12:15:13, first table run 12:16:31 -- **78 s earlier**.  Written as a SEPARATE FILE rather than appended to `PRETABLE.out`, because this ledger records an append destroying a PRETABLE's mtime once, and the mtime is the artifact's entire evidentiary value.\n\n*** A PREDICTION OF MINE THAT MISSED, recorded because it is the same class I spent today cataloguing: I predicted `BASE 30/30` and the measurement is `29/29`. ***  I derived 30 from `grep -c 'record('` -- **a CORRELATE of the scored-row count, not the count itself** (one `record()` call is not scored).  *The deriver-keys-on-a-correlate rule, in my own pre-table prediction, on the same day I recorded it against three other censuses.  The error is harmless here because predictions and measurements are in separate sections and every citation names `SCORES.txt` -- which is exactly why they are kept apart.*\n\n**AND THUMB IS COVERED AFTER ALL, which settles part of an open question:** the failing-row lists name `THUMB_POP_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_PUSH_WB_UNAL`, `THUMB_POP_COLD_DATA` and `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA` -- so the instrument does exercise the Thumb callers, and `M_ENTRY` kills the Thumb writeback rows alongside the ARM ones.  *The `#524` pass-2 seat could not verify this because `cpu_arm.c` was outside its permitted set; the table settles it from the other direction.*\n\n**FLOOR STATUS FOR `#524`: (a) HEAD red, (b) reinstatement dies, (c) null medium 8/29, (d) `M_MASK1` KILLED at 27/29.  The breach is closed in-round under L1** -- nothing had shipped, so this is pre-ship construction rather than an L2 repair.  **Residue 3 disclosed in the contract rather than rowed**, because `~1` dies at `+2` and `~2` already dies at `+1`, so a `+3` row kills no enumerated mutant.\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 4 (`killtable_20260911d/`).  **TABLE 4: THE CLASS CLOSURE WORKS AND **BASE IS RED ON ONE OF MY OWN NEW ROWS** -- ROUND HELD.** ***\n\n=== THE REPAIR-CHAIN PASS FOUND A FLOOR BREACH AND REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN ===\n*VERDICT: FLOOR BREACH FOUND; the repair correctly detects `M_MASK1` but does not close the repair chain.*  **Two more clean-sweep candidates against the 29-row composite, both STATIC DEDUCTIONS rather than measured -- which is why each became a leg:**\n  * **`M_MASK7` (`& ~7`, OVER-alignment):** every base the instrument drove -- `0x10000`, `0x2000{1,2}`, `0x2100{1,2}` -- sits in the **FIRST WORD of its 8-byte block, where `~3` and `~7` AGREE.**  *And the two-register LDM does not rescue it: it asserts only `r0` writeback, never either loaded register.*\n  * **`M_PBITCOND` (a CONDITIONAL weakening on an EXISTING dominating predicate):** mask `~1` when `p_bit`, `~3` otherwise, at the STORE site only.  ARM STM is IA (`p_bit=0`, correct mask); Thumb PUSH is DB (`p_bit=1`, weakened) **but ran at exactly ONE residue, `+1`, where `~1` and `~3` agree.**  *The predicate axis was exercised at a single point.*\n\n**TWO CONSECUTIVE PASSES FOUND MASK-WEAKENING BREACHES, so the next repair owed CLASS CLOSURE rather than a third point fix.**  Space from the helpers' own inputs: **{site} x {residue} x {word position in the 8-byte block} x {p_bit}**.  Three uncovered cells closed in ONE diff.\n\n=== AND BOTH NEW MUTANTS DIED, ON THE CELLS WRITTEN FOR THEM ===\n\n| leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|\n| `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | `ARM_LDM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`, `THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | its two P2 rows **plus both new ones** |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | the writeback rows |\n| `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 21+ rows incl. both controls |\n| `HEAD` / `RESTORED` | 19/32 | identical -- clean restore against GIT |\n\n*** BUT `BASE: 31/32`.  THE FIX ITSELF FAILS `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2` -- one of the rows I ADDED THIS PASS -- and a red BASE row means the round DOES NOT SHIP. ***\n\n**The row reports `w0=0x00000000 w1=0x00000000` on the FIXED build.**  Expected: base `0x21005` masks to `0x21004`, so the word lands in `PAGE_STM+4`.  **Nothing landed in either word.**\n\n> **AND I COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE BY REASONING.**  Under HEAD the same row reports `w1=0x00000011`, which implies the stored bytes ENDED at `0x21004` -- i.e. a base of `0x21001`, not the `0x21005` my literal sets.  That contradicts `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA`, which calibrates correctly at base `0x21001` giving `0x22334400`.  *Two readings of the same mechanism that cannot both be right.*\n\n**SO I STOPPED REASONING AND INSTRUMENTED**, which is this project's own rule when a value is surprising: a diagnostic rebuilds BASE and **dumps a WIDE WINDOW around `PAGE_STM`, so the bytes can be LOCATED rather than guessed at.**  *I had been deriving where the store went without looking at where it actually is.*\n\n**TWO POSSIBILITIES, AND WHICH ONE IS NOT YET KNOWN:** my row is mis-specified -- most likely, since every other BASE row passes including `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_P2`, which uses the same mechanism at `+2` -- or it has found something real about the store path at bit-2-set addresses.  **Recorded as open rather than resolved in either direction.**\n\n*Also fixed this pass, from the panel: the mutator now emits **`0xfffffffc`, the EXACT shipped literal**, so `BASE` measures the working-tree text rather than a semantic equivalent -- grok raised that the table had not measured what would be committed.*\n\n*** DISCLOSED: THE PATH ROWS ARE FIXTURE CHECKS, NOT MUTATION DISCRIMINATORS, AND THE REPAIR PASS WAS RIGHT ABOUT THE ONES I ADDED. ***  Every `in_tlb_*` is captured BEFORE `run_prog`, so no helper-local mutation of a mask, operand or predicate can retroactively change it.  They establish the run was cold -- which the core comparison needs -- and discriminate NO enumerated mutant.  Kept for that purpose, disclosed rather than counted toward the floor.\n\n*And my `'a +3 row buys no enumerated mutant'` was TOO BROAD as a claim about the COMPOSITE: it holds within a fixed cell, but the missing distinction is the PREDICATE/RESIDUE COMBINATION, and residue 3 is one possible witness among several rather than one that buys nothing.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 TABLE 5 (`killtable_20260911e/`).  **THE RED BASE ROW WAS A DEFECT IN THE WITNESS, NOT THE FIX -- AND IT HAD CORRUPTED 8 OF 10 LEGS.  BASE IS NOW 32/32 AND THE FLOOR IS CLEAN.** ***\n\n=== HOW IT WAS FOUND, INCLUDING THE PART I DID BADLY ===\nTable 4 reported `BASE 31/32` -- the FIX failing `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA_BIT2`.  **A red BASE row blocks the round, so it was diagnosed rather than explained away.**\n\n*** I FIRST TRIED TO REASON IT OUT FROM THE BYTE PATTERNS AND COULD NOT MAKE THE ARITHMETIC CLOSE. ***  Under HEAD the row implied the store ended at `0x21004` -- a base of `0x21001` -- while `ARM_STM_COLD_DATA` calibrates correctly at that same base.  **Two readings of one mechanism that cannot both be true**, and I spent four rounds of inference on it before instrumenting.\n\n**The dump settled it in ONE run:**\n    r0 = 0x21005                                 <- the literal loaded fine\n    0x00021000  00000000 44332211 00000000 ...   <- the store LANDED at 0x21004\n\n> **THE STORE WAS CORRECT ALL ALONG.  THE READ WAS WRONG.**\n\n=== THE INSTRUMENT DEFECT, MEASURED DIRECTLY ===\n    dump from an ALIGNED start:\n      0x00021000  00000000 44332211\n    dump from an UNALIGNED start -- what `read_mem32` actually issued:\n      0x00021000           44332211 00000000\n                  ^^^^^^^^ the leading group is BLANK-PADDED\n\n`read_mem32()` searched for the line labelled `(addr & ~0xF)` -- **which matches in BOTH cases** -- then indexed `line[12:48].split()` by `(addr & 0xF) // 4`.  **When the dump starts unaligned the leading groups are ABSENT and `.split()` collapses the padding, so every index shifts and `parts[idx]` silently returns a NEIGHBOURING WORD.**\n\n    read_mem32(0x21004) -> parts == ['44332211', '00000000'], idx 1 -> 0\n\n*This is the padded-column trap this project already records, in a new place: the label matched, so every check that looked for the right line passed, and the CONTENT came from the wrong column.*\n\n**REPAIR: dump from `(addr & ~0xF)` for 16 bytes, so all four groups are always present -- which is what the index arithmetic already assumed.  One line.**\n\n*** SCOPE, AND IT IS WIDER THAN THE ONE RED ROW: 8 OF 10 LEGS CHANGED VALUE WHEN THE INSTRUMENT WAS REPAIRED. ***\n\n| leg | table 4 | table 5 |\n|---|---|---|\n| `BASE` | 31/32 | **32/32** |\n| `HEAD` | 19/32 | **17/32** -- the pre-fix build was failing MORE rows than reported |\n| `RESTORED` | 19/32 | 17/32 (= HEAD) |\n| `M_ENTRY` | 26/32 | 27/32 |\n| `M_MASK1` | 28/32 | 29/32 |\n| `M_PBITCOND` | 30/32 | 31/32 |\n| `M_POP_ONLY` | 25/32 | 23/32 |\n| `M_PUSH_ONLY` | 25/32 | 26/32 |\n| `M_MASK7`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | unchanged | unchanged |\n\n**The defect was in the probe AS ORIGINALLY BUILT**, so it affected every `read_mem32` call at a nonzero 16-byte offset -- the STM and Thumb PUSH clobber rows -- across tables 1 through 4.  **Every number those rows produced before this table is superseded.**  *Grepped: no other probe in the tree shares the shape, so the blast radius is this round's instrument alone.*\n\n=== THE FLOOR, NOW CLEAN ===\n\n| clause | leg | score | killed on |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| -- | `BASE` | **32/32** | **nothing -- clean** |\n| (a) pre-fix red | `HEAD` | 17/32 | the defect rows |\n| (b) reinstatement | `RESTORED` | 17/32 | = HEAD exactly |\n| (c) null medium | `M_NULL_MEDIUM` | 8/32 | 24 rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK1` | 29/32 | the three `P2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_MASK7` | 30/32 | the two `BIT2` rows |\n| **(d)** | `M_PBITCOND` | 31/32 | **`THUMB_PUSH_COLD_DATA_P2` alone -- the minimum** |\n| de-escalation | `M_ENTRY` | 27/32 | **all five writeback rows, nothing else** |\n\n**Every mutant dies on exactly the cells written for it, and `M_PBITCOND` on precisely one.**  *The class closure holds under a correct instrument, which is the thing table 4 could not establish.*\n\n*A small one of mine, recorded because I spent today cataloguing this class: my own shell check for a clean BASE tested `[ -z \"$B\" ]`, but the probe prints `FAILING: none` when clean -- a NON-EMPTY string -- so the check took its else branch and printed 'BASE still red on: none'.  The score `32/32` is what settled it.  A check that reports the opposite of the truth on a green run is the same family as the ones I filed against other people's scripts today.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PROMOTED TO regress/ AND WIRED INTO gate_arm.sh -- 32/32 AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY. ***\n\n**This discharges the obligation the round could not previously claim.**  Fable's adjudication found that *neither of today's probes is under `regress/` proper, so 'shipped a detector with the fix' is not yet true of either round.*  For `#524` it is true now.\n\n=== THE PROMOTION IS A COPY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE ===\n`regress/artifacts/armbdt/agy_armbdt_witness_20260911.py` **stays in place as the AS-RUN record of tables 1-5**; `regress/arm_bdt_probe.py` is the copy that evolves and that the gate executes.  *Editing the artifact copy would falsify what those tables actually measured* -- the same reason the repaired kill-table driver was written as a new file rather than over the old one.\n\n**The promoted copy differs only by two PRINT-ONLY lines (`ARMBDT_CONTROL=`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=`, matching the gate idiom the other ARM probes use) and a header note.  Verified: the `record()` count is unchanged, so no row was added or lost in the copy.**  Its header also carries the `read_mem32` alignment warning, so the next probe copied from it does not inherit [[probereadalign]].\n\n=== MEASURED AGAINST THE GATE'S OWN BINARY, NOT THE SCRATCH CLONE ===\n`$PMAX` is `build/gxemul`.  After the propagate+rebuild, the promoted probe run directly against it returns **`SCORE 32/32`, `ARMBDT_CONTROL=OK`, `ARMBDT_RESULT=32/32`, `FAILING: none`**.  *A pre-check worth the thirty seconds: wiring the probe in BEFORE the rebuild would have run it against a pre-fix binary and produced a red gate that meant nothing about the fix.*\n\n=== THE GATE SECTION, AND WHY THE DENOMINATOR IS A LITERAL ===\nFollows `arm_writeback_probe.py`'s shape exactly: run -> `gate_skip` if no result line -> print the ok/FAIL rows -> check the control -> **`check \"bdt rows run\" \"$bwant\" 32`** -> check correct == run.\n\n> **The denominator is pinned to a LITERAL deliberately.  The probe grew 25 -> 29 -> 32 rows across three kill tables as clause-(d) mutants were closed, and a gate that trusts the probe's own denominator cannot tell a probe that LOST rows from one that never had them.**\n\nThe section also records all five measured-dead mutants with their scores and the row family that kills each (`M_ENTRY` 27/32 writeback rows, `M_MASK1` 29/32 the `+2` rows, `M_MASK7` 30/32 the bit-2 rows, `M_PBITCOND` 31/32 Thumb PUSH `+2`, `M_NULL_MEDIUM` 8/32), **so a future reader meets the evidence at the gate rather than having to find the artifacts.**\n\n*Targeted gate run in flight.  Nothing committed; the owner's hold stands until it is green.*",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:32422:    "note": "Closed in the main loop (claude-fable-5-1, author self-review disclosed) at pass 16 on 8 of 9 mandated seats answering (agy deliberately not fired, reserved for measurement): six zero-breach answers each with a denominator, two claimed breaches refuted by reading. The chain closes on the first VALID pass finding zero breaches, per the repair-chain rule."
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:39691:     "note": "[NOT A SEAT ANSWER] *** WRONG RECORD, corrected 2026-09-19: MEASURED from the subagent transcript (per-message `model' and `stop_reason'; _scratchpad/agent_models.py), the answer this entry credits to fable5 was WRITTEN BY claude-opus-4-8 -- claude-fable-5-1 ended a turn with stop_reason \"refusal\" at 00:24:32Z and the Agent tool re-ran the rest on opus-4-8 (a `thinking_drop' attachment), keeping this seat's identity line.  \"identity verified\" was a SELF-REPORT, a check that cannot fail.  Kept as an opus-4-8 READING, not a fable5 answer; the first-hand fable5 ruling is the fable5 review 2026-09-19 entry below. ***  fable5 RULED LAST on #530's pass 2 + §F2 (identity verified: claude-fable-5-1, CHECKPOINT line quoted verbatim).  R1: L2 for vc ONLY -- lane is a floor axis because data[ofs] distinguishes all four lanes; va vb ve vg vt1 vt2 are clause-(e) debt, disclosed against the ALREADY-RULED parent classes p_port10 and lcaisasize (which the new contract must cite by name).  R2: floor closure = the four C=16 WRITE lanes {frozen L3 (shipped), l0w16 L0, low16 L1, a lane-2 cell at k=0} + an explicit store_buf chunk-list assertion; {mc2, kbc1024} is REFUTED as a closure (a lane-1 exemption escapes it); kbc1024, k=16 and the continuation cell are not floor.  R3: narrowing to escape vc = laundering; correcting the overclaiming contract = required (L4).  R4: fix the exit code AND the alive-blind retry in the closure diff.  R6: the chain closes on the first VALID pass that verifies the enumeration -- it must kill the lane-1 covering escape `&& (len <= 8 || (P & 3) != 1)` and show no lane cell open.  R7: E-DEBT correctly overturned; the filed pair was the #499 error from the other side.  -> #538 is the closure round.",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:46582:   "state_note": "filed 2026-09-17 from a VOLUNTEERED grok side-finding (raised under the standing license while it was refuting a different brief), then CONFIRMED on a second firing by a full instruction-to-switch trace.  ONE ROUND, MEMBER 3 at BOTH default: arms.  *** SAVE (:559) IS NOT FIRST-HIT -- it runs on the OLD mode (SVC32, modelled) and returns; the process dies in LOAD (:606).  A LOAD-ONLY fix CREATES the Save kill, which is sibling parity and not a second row. ***  Member 1 DELETE is WRONG (leaves cpsr reserved and r[] on the previous bank -> silent wrong-bank execution, then a second kill).  Member 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE on ARM but wrong: MSR is 'Exceptions: None'.  THE DECLINE MUST SIT BEFORE SAVE, or roll cpsr back in Load -- callers write cpsr BETWEEN save and load.  RANKED ABOVE armthumbabortkill: process death vs running=0, cheaper witness, no fake-green hazard.  GATE 14's 358 checks never feed the encoding.  *** REWRITE, NOT PATCH.  THE 16-ROW SPEC PLUS EIGHT AMENDMENTS DOES NOT CLOSE, AND THE SEAT THAT WROTE THE ORIGINAL SPEC RULED AGAINST ITS OWN WORK WHEN ASKED TO BE HARDER ON IT. ***  I asked plainly whether eight amendments on a 16-row spec meant the spec should be rewritten rather than patched, and named the `#392` shape as the thing I feared.  Answer: yes.  'A builder applying them in order will get 1+6 wrong and will either treat `save 2s` as a duplicate of `kill 15` or REPLACE `kill 15` with it, losing the `== 0x14` pin.'  *** AND THE TALLY ITSELF WAS A FROZEN NUMBER: it is 14/17 -> 17/17 over THIRTEEN sessions, not 14/16 -> 16/16 over twelve.  The original 12 was already false -- 16 scored minus `enc ok` minus the identity row minus one for live/pc/armed sharing S1 is 13, and the spec wrote 12. ***  THE VEHICLE STANDS and is unchanged: `-V -A -E testarm -M 64`, `0xE321F014`, member 3 at BOTH `default:` arms, one fresh process per scored row that executes a guest instruction, mid-stream dump.  The ROW LIST, the ORACLES and the SESSION ARITHMETIC are replaced. **ANSWER TO MY INTERACTION QUESTION 1+6, AND IT REFUTES MY FRAMING.**  I asked whether, once amendment 1 makes EOF-alone the kill trigger, the `enc ok` / `no cbit` / dump-before-MSR machinery becomes THE ONLY THING between the witness and a false green.  The answer is that those three are **NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT** -- each has a smallest defeat: dump-DEST proves MRS ran, not that the MSR word was planted or stepped; `no cbit` is greened by a decoder that never writes the mode field AT ALL, which greens every kill row too; and `enc ok` walking a constructor instead of the planted literal is the BFX incident repeating.  What actually closes false-green, all four required: (1) **POST want is `m=13 sp once`, not `m=13`** -- member 3 prints `(once)`, and encoding-nop, skipped-step and a silent member-2 all print nothing, so the latch must be IN the got string or its absence is a comment rather than a FAIL; (2) **poison `DEST+4` with 0xdeadbeef** and dump DEST+4, never DEST+0, so a never-fired post-MSR store reds; (3) **one `WORDS` dict**, with every `put w` drawn from it and a per-session plant-dump of the MSR slot -- this is the load-bearing anti-false-green that amendment 6 hoped `enc ok` was; (4) **named-red tally** -- HEAD must be EXACTLY the three named reds, because 17/17 on HEAD is not success. **ANSWER TO INTERACTION 3+5, AND AMENDMENT 3 IS HALF-RIGHT AND MISREADS ITS OWN CASE.**  The original two reds were `kill 14` and `kill 15` -- TWO LOAD HOLES catching the `== 0x14` special case -- **not** Load vs Save, which is what amendment 3 assumed.  So: SCORE `save 2s`, KEEP `kill 15`, do NOT replace one with the other.  The underlying worry stands: without a scored two-write row a Load-only skip-memcpy POSTs clean and still dies on the write OUT of reserved mode (16/17, `save 2s` red on the second MSR -- that is the point of the row). **AMENDMENT 5 IS TRUE OF THE ENCODING AND INERT FOR THIS PROBE, VERIFIED LOCALLY BY ME AND NOT TAKEN FROM THE SEAT:** `#0x14` does clear I and F, and testarm does hang cons/ether/rtc off `irqc` -- but ARM IRQ dispatch sits inside `if (!single_step) {` at `cpu_dyntrans.c:206` with the ARM arm at `:208-209`, and `-V` sets single_step; **`fiq_asserted` has ZERO occurrences across all of `src/`** and `ARM_FLAG_F` zero in `cpu_dyntrans.c`, so there is no ARM FIQ dispatch to enable; and debugger interaction `timer_stop()`s so `step N` runs no host-timer callback.  **The pin is STAY ON `step`, NEVER `continue`** -- a `continue` after a LANDED `#0x14` can take `arm_exception` and run Save on the reserved current mode, which is a free-run hazard and not a step hazard.  DO NOT change the filed stimulus to `#0xD4`. **BOTH KILL ARMS CONFIRMED AT HEAD BY READING:** `cpu_arm.c:559-561` `arm_save_register_bank: unimplemented mode %i` + `exit(1)`, and `:606-608` the same for `arm_load_register_bank`.  Two arms, two kills -- which is exactly why member 3 is owed at both and why `save 2s` is independent rather than a duplicate. **DROPPED FROM THE ORIGINAL SPEC:** EOF-AND-text as the kill trigger; the 12 sessions; 'two reds because two default: arms'; POST `0xd3` on `kill 14` (member 3 refuses the MODE FIELD, so I|F stay clear at `0x13` -- record I|F, do not want them); already-USR32 as the fake-green mechanism; copy-flags-`session()`; copy-flags-`printed()` (arm_flags_probe.py has none).  Amendment 8 belongs on the ALPHA spec, not this one. **CLAUSE (e), DISCLOSED NOT ROWED:** register-form MSR (`X(msr)` copies rm into arg[0] and calls msr_imm); the 26-bit holes; the other 32-bit holes 0x16/18/19/1a/1c/1d/1e (`kill 14` + `kill 15` catch a ONE-VALUE special case, not a two-value one); SPSR-then-`MOVS pc`; free-run IRQ after a landed reserved write, pinned by stay-on-step. **BUILD FROM THE ARTIFACT, NOT FROM THIS NOTE** -- the complete 17-row table, the 13-session schedule, the CONTROL=DEAD set, the plant layouts and the tally live verbatim at `regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/WITNESSSPEC_REWRITE_grok_20260917.txt`.  Transcribing a row table into a ledger note is the frozen-number class waiting to happen. **ONE BUILD REQUIREMENT I FOUND MYSELF AND THE SPEC DOES NOT STATE:** the launch line uses `regress/arm_bfx_stub.bin`, which is on the NEVER-STAGE list and is UNTRACKED.  That is safe, and the reason matters -- **all 11 top-level committed probes referencing such a stub CREATE IT IF ABSENT** (e.g. `alpha_harness_probe.py:169-173`).  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` MUST carry the same self-create block, or it will be the one committed probe that cannot run on a fresh clone.  *** THE PROBE WAS ATTACKED BEFORE IT WAS EVER RUN, AND IT HAD THREE INDEPENDENT DEFECTS. Verdict: 'It runs.  It does not score.' ***  (1) **LINE LABELS ARE 16-BYTE ALIGNED** -- `debugger_cmds.c:709` is `addr = addr_start & ~0xf`, so `dump 0x9004` labels the line `0x00009000` AND BLANK-PADS the leading word groups, shifting every group index.  (2) **THE DUMP RENDERS LITTLE-ENDIAN BYTES, NOT A WORD** -- 0x2a prints as `2a000000`.  (3) **`save 2s` READ A SLOT NOTHING WROTE**: its plant put SP at DEST+16 via `str_sp_10` while `classify()` read DEST+8, so ` sp` could NEVER appear and **the row was ALWAYS RED, pre-fix and post-fix alike** -- not a witness of Save at all. *** THE REMEDIES FOR (1) AND (2) ALREADY EXISTED IN THIS TREE AND I DID NOT USE THEM: `arm_bdt_probe.py:123-149` records the alignment measurement from 2026-09-11 in a seven-line comment, and `arm_flags_probe.py:191-193` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- the carrier's own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; `armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed `ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and 'SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect'.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why `arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel's own byte pattern hiding the defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table's CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not 'fix' the probe down to it.  REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit first, then this.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is 'we do not run that OS here'; the gap on armmsr is 'that OS would not do this.'  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr & 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c 'case ARM_MODE_USR26'` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *'If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE'*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#<legal> ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel's SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux's `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:46663:     "note": "*** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is 'we do not run that OS here'; the gap on armmsr is 'that OS would not do this.'  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr & 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c 'case ARM_MODE_USR26'` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *'If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE'*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#<legal> ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel's SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux's `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**"
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:46901:   "id": "ip30abortkill",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:46904:   "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py -- rung 3 on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30'.  ONE INSTRUMENT, BOTH BUILDS, 21 rows: PRE-FIX PREFIX_21row_20260917.out IP30ABORT_RESULT=8/21 (13 defect rows red); POST-FIX probe_FIX531_21row_20260917.out IP30ABORT_RESULT=21/21, 0 red.  Liveness and device-signature controls green on both.  THE ORACLE-CORRECTION ORDER PROOF is a separate triple on the earlier 18-row form and is deliberately NOT re-measured: pre-fix+OLD oracle 11/18 (probe_HEAD_15row_20260917.out), pre-fix+RE-PINNED 8/18 (REPIN_prefix_20260917.out, the red), post-fix+RE-PINNED 18/18 (probe_FIX531_final_20260917.out).  An order proof is a claim about three runs of ONE instrument at one moment; re-running it on the grown form would destroy the only evidence that the re-pin preceded the code change.  The 18-row instrument is frozen at ip30_abort_probe.18row_20260917.frozen.py.  *** THE FLOOR IS NOT DISCHARGED BY 21/21: the two rows added for the L2 breach read `died' pre-fix because their seeding write aborts, which is the reproduction working and NOT a measured kill.  Kill table 1 (six mutants) is the discharge; PRETABLE.out was written before it ran and its mtime proves the order. ***",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:46908:   "state_note": "*** ONE GUEST LOAD OR STORE AT 0x0ff10020 ON THE ADVERTISED `-E sgi -e ip30` RAISES SIGABRT. AN `exit(` SWEEP CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE `abort()` CONTAINS NO `exit(`. ***  FOUR abort() SITES IN ONE FILE, all verified by reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:  (1) :127 `case 0x10020` Set ISR; (2) :135 `case 0x10028` Clear ISR; (3) :145 `case 0x10030` write arm; (4) :78 the DEVICE_TICK. REACHABILITY: `DEV_SGI_IP30_LENGTH` is 0x80000 (:44) registered at :387, the device is added at `machine_sgi.c:417` as `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000`, and the subtype is ADVERTISED at `machine_sgi.c:744` -- `machine_entry_add_subtype(me, \"IP30\", 30, \"ip30\", \"octane\", NULL)`. 0x10020/0x10028/0x10030 all fall inside the window. *** TWO FACTS THE HUNTING SEAT DID NOT STATE, FOUND BY READING THE ARM, AND BOTH RAISE THE SEVERITY: ***  (A) **`case 0x10020` HAS NO `writeflag` TEST AND EXECUTES `d->isr = idata;` FIRST.**  `idata` is initialised to 0 at :87 and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at :89-90, so a guest **LOAD** at 0x0ff10020 ZEROES THE INTERRUPT STATUS REGISTER and *then* aborts.  That is GUEST-STATE CORRUPTION AND A HOST DEATH IN THE SAME ARM, and it means the defect is not write-only as a reader would assume from the `Set ISR` comment.  `case 0x10028` shares the missing test (its `d->isr &= ~idata` is a no-op at idata==0, then aborts).  `case 0x10030` IS writeflag-tested and reads return `d->isr` correctly -- so the three arms are NOT uniform, and a detector must row them separately.  (B) **THE TICK ABORT IS ARMED BY A LEGAL WRITE TO AN IMPLEMENTED REGISTER.**  `case 0x10000` (:99-105) implements imask0 and ACCEPTS the write; the tick at :74-78 then aborts whenever `d->imask0 & (1<<50)`.  So the guest makes an accepted, unremarkable register write and the host dies LATER, from a different code path, with no access in flight.  That is the de-escalation shape at its worst: the announcing access succeeds. OPTION SET: member **7 IMPLEMENT** is the strong candidate -- every one of the four sites has the intended model sitting beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)` call, so this is a stubbed path (no predicate refusing a value), which is exactly the tell member 7 was minted for. Member **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE** is the fallback if the interrupt rewrite stays out of scope.  Member 5 FAULT is architecturally available here (MIPS dispatches on a failed device access) but is INERT as the code stands, because `abort()` fires before any `return 0`. GATE COVERAGE: NONE.  No ip30 probe exists under `regress/`.  Gate 9 may CONSTRUCT the machine, but nothing feeds 0x10020 -- the same blind spot as `armmsrmodekill`. SEVERITY HIGH: `abort()` is strictly worse than `exit(1)` (SIGABRT plus a core dump), it is on an advertised subtype, and one of the four paths corrupts guest state before dying.  Honest limit: there is NO in-tree Octane boot image, so this ranks probe-HIGH and not boot-HIGH -- the same standing as `mapledmaaddr` and `vmedefaultkill`.  Reachability is CONFIRMED BY READING and is NOT YET MEASURED; the round owes a rung-3 probe on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30` (`ld`/`sd` at 0x0ff10020), plus a separate row for the armed-tick path, before any edit. NO EXISTING ROW COVERS THIS.  Nine ledger rows mention ip30 or abort(): `exitsweep` is CLOSED and counts `exit(` sites, not `abort()`; `ctorabortclass` is HELD and is the CONSTRUCTION abort class (nine subtypes across five families, none of them ip30 -- ip30 constructs fine, which is why this one is guest-reachable rather than construction-time); `hpcabort` is CLOSED and is hpcmips construction; `asansweepblind` is DROPPED and is about gate 9's blindness to construction aborts.  `exitunowned76` counts `exit(`.  `cpusexitsweep` is src/cpus/ only.  The site was NAMED ONCE, in the 2026-08-17 `exitsweep` correction in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (\"No abort() is guest-reachable is FALSE\"), and never given an id -- so it has been a known-but-unqueued exposure for a month.  *** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED 2026-09-17.  THE DEFECT IS NO LONGER CONFIRMED-BY-READING; IT IS MEASURED. ***  `IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK`, `IP30ABORT_RESULT=6/9` on the committed build, with the three kill rows RED -- which IS the reproduction, since the house polarity is WANT = POST-FIX CORRECT.  Measured, on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30' with no `device_add' introduced to reach the site: **a guest LOAD at 0x0ff10020 kills the host** (`kill 20 rd' DEAD); so does a load at 0x10028; so does a WRITE at 0x10030 -- while a READ at 0x10030 is SERVICED (`acc 30 rd' alive).  **The three arms are provably NOT uniform**, which was a reading and is now a measurement.  The raw disposition was captured separately: `KILLED BY SIGNAL 6 (SIGABRT) after 0.6 s`, and the tree installs no SIGABRT handler. *** AND THE FIRST RUN OF THAT PROBE REPORTED `alive' FOR ALL THREE KILL ROWS -- A FALSE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH -- FOR A REASON WORTH CARRYING INTO EVERY EOF-ORACLE PROBE IN THIS TREE. ***  `rd()` RETURNS IMMEDIATELY ON EOF instead of waiting out its timeout, so the `spin` that exists to give a dying child time to die is SKIPPED EXACTLY WHEN THE CHILD DIES.  A single `waitpid(WNOHANG)` then samples ~0 s after EOF while the kernel is still writing a 6 MB core, reports 'still running', and the kill row goes GREEN.  **The fix is to POLL with a deadline, never to sample once** -- and `arm_msrmode_probe.py`, whose every kill row is an EOF oracle, MUST carry the polling form or its witness will certify the MSR defect fixed while it is live. *A second apparatus error in the same run, caught by the same disagreement: my `devsig` and `acc 18` oracles wanted truncated values, but `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS on this 64-bit R10000, so a CORRECT device reddened.  The apparatus was wrong, not the model.* ## FIX DESIGN, ADJUDICATED -- **MEMBER 3 + MEMBER 4, AND THE writeflag TEST IN THE SAME DIFF.** *** MY MEMBER-7 PREMISE WAS REFUTED BY A REPO FACT, NOT BY ARGUMENT. ***  I filed that member 7 IMPLEMENT was the strong candidate because every site carries its intended model beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)`.  **THAT API NO LONGER EXISTS.**  Verified locally: `cpu_interrupt(` occurs FIVE times in the entire tree -- one warning comment in `dev_lca.c` and the four commented-out calls here -- with no definition and no declaration; the live API is `interrupt_assert` through `struct interrupt` (`cpu_mips.c:1884`).  **Uncommenting does not compile.**  The tell was right (a stubbed path with no predicate); the inference that it was cheap was wrong. Further, `machine_sgi.c:415` for case 30 is literally `// TODO: Interrupts!` -- no INTERRUPT_CONNECT, no HEART lines -- and the serial is already routed AROUND HEART onto `cpu.2`.  The fossil literals use a DELETED numbering scheme (`8 + x`, so the tick's `cpu_interrupt(cpu, 8+1+50)` is 59), so translating them onto `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu.2)` would be a WRONG MODEL, not a restore.  A cheap HEART sharing `cpu.2` with the 16550 breaks on deassert (assert ORs, deassert ANDs off), which is exactly why CRIME exists.  **`#516` refused member 7 on `lca_interrupt_assert` after naming the same tell -- the precedent is in-tree.** **MEMBER 7 IS THE FOLLOW-UP ROW** (implement HEART the way `dev_sgi_ip32.c` implements CRIME: INTERRUPT_CONNECT to cpu.2, named child lines, a reassert function), NOT this de-fatalisation. **MEMBER 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE and WRONG.**  `memory_rw.c` turns a device `return 0` into `EXCEPTION_DBE` on MIPS, so it works -- but these offsets are NOT unmapped space: they have storage, comments, and a PROM that already talks to the neighbour at 0x10018.  `dev_unreadable.c` is the FAULT idiom and is for holes.  *Note the brief's own framing was weak here and the seat said so: `abort()` firing before any `return 0` is a description of the current kill, not a design warrant either way.* **Q3 -- THE writeflag TEST IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  This is the `asicindexcase` / `mvme187memcoob` shape: the kill is currently also the direction check.  De-fatalise 0x10020 without adding the test and **the host lives on with a guest-clobbered ISR** -- worse than today.  0x10028 shares the missing test and does not corrupt today (`&= ~0` is a no-op) but gets it anyway, so the twins stay twins.  0x10030 already has it and its reads work NOW -- an existing accept-side channel that must not break.  Owed row: a load at 0x10020 leaves ISR unchanged (seed via a write, `lw`, read back at 0x10030). **Q4 -- YOU DO NOT DECLINE A TICK; YOU STUB THE CALLBACK.**  Members 1-6 presume an access with a value to refuse, and the `:78` abort has none -- the guest already completed a LEGAL write to an IMPLEMENTED register.  No new option-set member is minted: this is member 3 applied to a callback, exactly as `#516` did for `lca_interrupt_assert`.  The tick also does not implement its own comment (no compare of 0x20000 against 0x30000), so a member-7 timer would have to ADD that, not merely delete the abort. **Q5 -- DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.**  `0x10000` is not 'maybe an accept row' -- for the tick it IS the accept row, because that write is what used to arm the host death; it owes a companion row that the host SURVIVES the next tick.  Neighbours to pin: 0x20, 0x10000, 0x10018, 0x10030 READ, 0x20000, 0x30000.  **The latch must be TWO BITS, not one** (tick vs ISR-recalc): a shared bit lets the first shape silence the second, which is LCA's assert/deassert lesson. **Q6 -- SIBLINGS, GREPPED.**  `dev_sgi_ip19.c` and `dev_sgi_ip20.c` use `debug()` + `return 1` for unknown offsets; **this file's OWN `default:` does the same**.  So the ISR arms are the file's outliers, not the house idiom -- the same upstream-residue finding that settled `sgireunmapped`. **WITNESS LIMIT, STATED:** the probe covers the three ACCESS kills and does NOT cover the TICK kill.  `tickshift` is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 cycles and a handful of `step`s cannot reach it -- that needs `continue`/free-run, which is the same step-vs-free-run distinction the carrier already records for host-timer paths.  The tick row is OWED and is not claimed.  ## *** PANEL PASS 1 -- FOUR CORRECTIONS, TWO OF THEM TO WRONG RECORDS I WROTE THIS SESSION. *** (7 of 8 scriptable transports; codex still running at write time; Fable held for last.) **(A) DISPOSITION 1 IS WRONG AS RECORDED, and filing the follow-up row on it would ship a false premise.**  I recorded *'Member 7 IMPLEMENT is NOT available.'*  **The correct statement is AVAILABLE-BUT-UNWARRANTED.**  What does not exist is `cpu_interrupt()`; the LIVE API -- `interrupt_assert`/`interrupt_deassert` through `struct interrupt` -- exists and is exactly what `dev_sgi_ip32.c` uses for CRIME.  So member 7 is not blocked by the absence of an API; it is declined because DELIVERY IS A SUBSYSTEM nobody has costed (no INTERRUPT_CONNECT for ip30, the 16550 already on `cpu.2`, and a deleted `8 + x` numbering).  *The distinction is not pedantic: 'not available' would make the filed follow-up row unbuildable by definition, when in fact it is ordinary unscheduled work.*  (glm, who noticed that my own finding names the live API two sentences after declaring the member unavailable.) **(B) DISPOSITION 4'S WORD 'SEPARATE' IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THE PACKET.**  grok and the kimi substitute reached this independently.  The missing `writeflag` test is **the store-arm of THIS de-fatalisation** -- the same three `case` labels, the same function, the same common tail -- so it is ONE DIFF either way: *if you keep a store, a load must not execute it; if you decline the store, the load is already safe.*  **Calling it 'separate' is what made my own Q4 (scope-creep?) askable at all**, so the framing manufactured the question.  It is genuinely separate only at `0x10030`, which is already writeflag-tested.  kimi's sharper form: at 0x10020/0x10028 the missing test **is the read-kill's entry condition**, not a second defect. **(C) MY Q2 WAS REFUTED BY MY OWN PACKET** -- independently by kimi and deepseek.  I asked whether keeping `d->isr = idata` is member 4 given that the store updates *'a register nothing reads for any purpose'*.  **The same packet states that `case 0x10030`'s READ returns `d->isr`.**  The register IS read, by a path the packet itself documents and the witness measures (`acc 30 rd` alive).  The question was malformed; the answer is that the store feeds a live read path, which is member 4 ACCEPT on its own terms and not member 6. **(D) TWO WARRANTS SHARPENED RATHER THAN OVERTURNED.**  Disposition 3's warrant cited a PROM that 'already talks to 0x10018' -- but 0x10018 is a NEIGHBOUR, so that sentence is surplus to the claim it was supporting (grok).  Disposition 6 holds, and its in-tree warrant is better taken from CRIME's *'one bit per site, never shared'* than from LCA's two-functions case; the hazard is concrete -- **one shared bit lets a Set-ISR write silence the timer-tick diagnostic forever** (grok, minimax, agy).  Disposition 7 holds AS A CENSUS but does NOT pick member 2 over member 3: the `fatal()` already exists, so de-fatalising KEEPS it and latches (grok). **STILL OPEN AND NOT INVENTED:** minimax says Q3 -- what a read at 0x10020 should RETURN post-fix -- **cannot be settled from the packet** and needs an IP30/HEART source, and there is no HEART manual under `_scratchpad/`.  That is an honest UNKNOWN and it outranks a confident guess; the round picks a disposition for Q3 with its reason stated, or defers the read behaviour.  **agy proposes a TICK ROW BY `step 70000`** -- `tickshift` 16 means 65536 cycles -- which would make the tick reachable WITHOUT free-run and **contradicts my 'needs free-run' statement in this row's witness limit.**  Not yet measured; carried as a named question rather than adopted.  *** THE TICK ROW IS BUILDABLE AND IS MEASURED: `step 1' KILLS THE HOST.  The codex/agy split is settled BY MEASUREMENT, and BOTH SEATS WERE HALF RIGHT WHILE THE DECISIVE FACT WAS NAMED BY NEITHER. ***  codex was right that stepping alone is insufficient -- the guard must be ARMED (measured: no store + 70000 steps -> alive; bit 49 + 70000 steps -> alive).  agy was right that free-run is NOT needed, but its stated reason -- a 65536-cycle period implying `step 70000' -- is not why the cheap row works.  **THE MECHANISM, verified by me in the source and not taken from the seat:** `machine_add_tickfunction' sets `ticks_till_next[n] = 0' (`machine.c:354') while `ticks_reset_value[n] = 1 << tickshift' (`:355'), and `machine_run()' decrements then fires while `<= 0' (`:727').  **So the FIRST `machine_run()' of any session drives it to -1 and fires EVERY tick function immediately** -- `step 1' executes the store and then runs the tick in that same `machine_run()'.  *** A THIRD FACT NEITHER SEAT NAMED, AND IT IS A FALSE-GREEN TRAP I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: **BIT 50 CANNOT BE SET BY `sw'.** ***  `case 0x10000' does `d->imask0 = idata' with `idata = memory_readmax64(cpu, data, len)', so a FOUR-BYTE store caps idata below 2^32 -- measured, `sw 0xffffffff' + 70000 steps -> ALIVE.  Arming requires **`sd' = 0xFCE80000** (readback `ld' = 0xDC220000).  **`OP_SW_T0_A3' IS THE ONLY STORE HELPER IN `ip30_abort_probe.py'**, so a tick row copy-pasted from it would arm nothing, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH.  **PERIODICITY IS NOT A STARTUP ARTIFACT** -- the boundary was measured from TWO different warm offsets, both sides: warm=10 gives alive at N=65525 and DEAD at 65526; warm=100 gives alive at 65435 and DEAD at 65436.  Both put tick #2 at `machine_run' #65536 exactly, so ticks fire at MR #1, #65536, #131072.  **A guest arming bit 50 at ANY moment dies within 65536 instructions, with no access in flight.**  *The seat's own first derivation said #65537 and its boundary run measured that false -- it recorded the measurement over its derivation, which is the right way round.*  STABILITY: run 1 and run 2 are BYTE-IDENTICAL across all 13 scored rows (9/13, same four FAILs), `IP30TICK_CONTROL=OK` both times.  CONTROLS ALL GREEN, including the two that make the positive attributable: **`tick49 s70k'** (same store, same step count, bit 49 not 50 -> alive) is the discriminator, and **`late s1'** (armed AFTER tick #1 was consumed, one step -> alive) is what proves `tick50 s1' measured THE TICK and not something else about the first instruction.  **L4 APPLIED TO MY OWN PROBE:** `ip30_abort_probe.py`'s `acc imask' row claimed \"a handful of steps cannot reach it\".  MEASURED FALSE.  The row is still safe, for two reasons it did not state (bit 18, and `sw' cannot set bit 50 anyway), and the comment now says so.  **CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ROUND:** codex's constraint is DISCHARGEABLE rather than blocking -- the four-site closure is available because the tick row exists and is measured.  Artifacts at `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/tick_20260917/`, all four carrying their completion sentinel, copied off the volatile scratch path the measuring seat itself flagged.  *** Q3 IS SETTLED BY THE DOCUMENT THE CODE ITSELF CITES, AND THE ANSWER ARRIVED WITH A NEW FINDING. ***  `dev_sgi_ip30.c` says \"according to Linux/IP30\" three times, so the reference is Linux's `arch/mips/include/asm/sgi/heart.h` -- fetched with the owner's approval on this project's vmesbcp2 / 21066 / ddi0406 precedent, then VERIFIED LOCALLY offset by offset before anything was cited.  **Every offset matches the fork exactly**, base included (0x0ff00000 vs `machine_sgi.c:417`). **THE ANSWER: `set_isr` (0x10020) and `clear_isr` (0x10028) are WRITE-SIDE registers.**  The architectural triple is set / clear / status -- write set_isr to set, write clear_isr to clear, READ `isr` for status.  A read of set_isr has NO defined meaning, so **do not mirror `d->isr` there** (that fabricates semantics, which the addition licence forbids) and **DECLINE the read** -- now a TRANSFERRED WARRANT rather than the conservative guess it was when minimax said the packet could not settle it. *** AND IT UPGRADES THE `writeflag` TEST FROM DEFENSIVE TO CORRECT: *** it was argued as a direction guard; the reference makes it ARCHITECTURE, because executing a write-side register's store arm on a READ is wrong on its own terms, independently of the host death. *** NEW FINDING, AND THE CODE ASKED FOR IT IN ITS OWN COMMENT: heart.h documents `isr` (0x10030) as READ-ONLY, and the fork implements a WRITE arm -- `/* Clear-on-write (TODO: is this correct?) */  d->isr &= ~idata;`.  The cited document answers that TODO: NO. ***  Clearing goes through `clear_isr` at 0x10028, which this same file already implements, so the model has TWO clear paths where the architecture has ONE, and the fabricated one is the one carrying the self-doubting comment.  IN SCOPE for this round (same case labels, same function, same detector); it is a member 6 BACKED ACCEPT standing WITHOUT a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit.  Disposition deliberately NOT decided here. **HONEST LIMIT, STATED:** a Linux header is a driver author's model, NOT SGI's specification. It is strong evidence about what the registers ARE and about what this emulator MEANT -- because this emulator cites it -- and it is not a hardware contract.  No claim above depends on the difference: declining a read is safe under either reading.  Preserved at regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/HEART_reference_20260917.txt.  *** FABLE 5.1 ADJUDICATION, 2026-09-17.  IDENTITY VERIFIED -- `claude-fable-5-1`, the nonce, AND the verbatim first line of CHECKPOINT.md, so prompt delivery and file access are proven rather than assumed.  Fired LAST, after all 8 scriptable transports and the Opus measurement. *** **SHIP ORDER: (c) commit the reviewed working set -> (b) run `arm_msrmode_probe.py` -> (a) the ip30 fix as its OWN round.**  Serialisation note it supplied and I had not: (b) runs the emulator and (c) runs a gate, and they contend on CPU but NOT on the tree -- so run (b) to completion, THEN (c)'s gate alone. **A2 -- Q3 IS ALREADY ANSWERED AND THE FIX DOES NOT WAIT.**  DECLINE the read at 0x10020; do not mirror `d->isr`, do not return 0-as-a-value.  *** AND IT RULED ON A SECOND DISPOSITION MY FIVE QUESTIONS NEVER REACHED: DECLINE THE 0x10030 **WRITE** ARM TOO. ***  heart.h documents `isr` as read-only and the fork's clear-on-write is a member-6 BACKED ACCEPT standing without a warrant, which the addition licence does not permit -- Linux/IP30 clears via 0x10028, which the file already implements.  Same function, same case labels, same detector family, so same round.  It owes a STATE row (write declined AND `isr` unchanged), not merely an `alive' row. **A3 -- MY FRAMING WAS WRONG AND THE CORRECTION IS THE USEFUL PART.**  These are not four defects competing to join a round under the reopening rule.  **They are ONE ABANDONED FEATURE** -- the identical `fatal(\"IP30 legacy interrupt rewrite: TODO\"); abort();` at every reachable exit of a rewrite nobody finished.  The unit of work is the FEATURE, not the site, and splitting it would de-fatalise three of four exits and leave the fourth a live host death, which is the de-escalation clause's own hazard.  *** AND IT REFUSED TO LET ME GLOSS THE COST: the 'no new oracle' criterion IS genuinely violated -- the tick needs its own periodicity oracle and its own latch bit, so THE ROUND SHIPS TWO DETECTORS, and the round record must NAME that deviation rather than let a two-probe structure read as an accident. *** **A4 -- IT FOUND MY WEAKEST CORRECTION AND IT IS RIGHT.**  My (iv) said the `acc imask' row is safe *because it writes bit 18 AND because `sw' cannot set bit 50*.  Both clauses are true and **clause (2) MISDIRECTS**: the row's value `1<<18` has no high bits at any width, so width is irrelevant to THIS row -- while 'sw cannot set bit 50' reads as a general safety invariant for imask0, and **the death IS reachable, by `sd 1<<50`**.  *** MY OWN PROBE'S COMMENT IS STRONGER AND MORE HONEST THAN MY SUMMARY OF IT: it calls that row a documented HAZARD and warns that copy-pasting it as a kill row would 'arm NOTHING, stay ALIVE, and score green on a live host death'.  My correction re-labelled a HAZARD as a SAFETY PROPERTY. ***  That is the a-correction-is-not-safer-than-what-it-corrects rule landing on me. **A4(i) -- MY WARRANT OVERCLAIMED TRANSFERABILITY, verified locally:** `dev_sgi_ip32.c` has 6 INTERRUPT_CONNECT/ASSERT sites; **`dev_sgi_ip30.c` has ZERO, and zero `struct interrupt` members.**  'CRIME uses it' proves the IDIOM exists on MIPS; it does not prove member 7 is BUILDABLE for ip30, which has no interrupt topology at all.  The correct statement is *member 7's idiom is available; its application to ip30 requires building the HEART topology and is substantial* -- which is the carrier's own 2026-09-06 lesson (check the member is available on THIS CPU; a precedent real elsewhere is not thereby transferable). **A5 -- THE QUESTION I WAS NOT ASKING, AND IT IS THE RIGHT ONE: DOES `ip30abortkill` ACTUALLY OUTRANK `armmsrmodekill`?**  Rung is a FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY orders the queue, and severity rests on real-guest reachability **that is measured for NEITHER**.  Verified locally: `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels today**; only one gate names ip30 at all, and the device's own header says it is 'just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.'  Meanwhile `armmsrmodekill` kills the PROCESS on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  **So the order between the two HIGHs was set without the measurement that would settle it** -- which is itself the argument for running the arm probe BEFORE the larger ip30 build: the cheap measurement informs the order of the expensive work.  *** THE FIX DIFF IS WRITTEN AND PRESERVED, AND IT FOUND A HOLE IN MY OWN DETECTOR. ***  `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt`.  NOT APPLIED -- the adjudicated ship order puts the ip30 round LAST, after the commit and the arm probe. The diff honours every ruling: TWO latch bits (`IP30_W_TICK` 1<<0, `IP30_W_ISR` 1<<1, 'one bit per site, never shared'), the `(once)` house marker, `break` and never an early `return` so the common tail at :180-183 still runs, the `writeflag` test at 0x10020/0x10028, and on a READ it DECLINES rather than mirroring -- *'odata stays the function's 0 initializer; that is not a modelled value'*.  It also declines the 0x10030 WRITE arm per the Fable ruling, with the comment *'the TODO answers no.  Clearing goes through clear_isr at 0x10028'*.  It carries a control-flow table showing every path lives. *** THE DETECTOR FINDING, AND IT IS FLOOR-RELEVANT: `kill 30 wr` IS SURVIVE-ONLY AND THEREFORE CANNOT SEE THIS REPAIR. ***  Post-fix the host lives on a 0x10030 write whether or not the store is applied, so an `alive` oracle is invariant under the very edit the row exists to pin.  **That row owes a STATE oracle -- write declined AND `isr` unchanged** -- which is the CHANNEL check's vacuous-by-construction shape, knowable now, before any table runs. **AND THE ISR-UNCHANGED ROW MUST COVER BOTH LOADS, with a twist at 0x10028:** `&= ~0` is a no-op today, so a row that only checks the ISR CELL cannot fail there.  Pin **`odata` is not `d->isr`** instead, seeding ISR to a distinctive value first.  Without that, the 0x10028 writeflag repair ships unpinned.  *** THE DETECTOR WAS ATTACKED BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED, AND IT HAS CLEAN-SWEEP (d) MUTANTS. Every one is L1 CONSTRUCTION -- pre-ship material still being built -- NOT an L2 breach, because nothing has been committed. ***  That is the whole point of attacking a detector while it is still a draft. **THE MISSES, each 12/12 against the current 12-row probe:** **(1) `M_tick_abort` -- keep the `if`, put `abort()` back.**  MISS.  No session sets imask0 bit 50, so nothing in the probe visits the tick guard at all.  *** THIS IS CLAUSE (b) AT SITE 4 AS WELL AS (d): a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT of the removed defect that the detector cannot see. ***  **(2) `M_tick_bit49`** -- `(1<<50)` -> `(1<<49)`.  MISS: `acc imask` deliberately writes bit 18, and `tick49 s70k` was ALREADY MEASURED alive.  **(3) `M_tick_swap`** -- `imask0` -> `reg_0x20000`, a field the body writes every tick.  MISS: `acc 20000` stores 0x1234 so bit 50 stays clear. **(4) `M_wf20_store_out` -- the `writeflag` wraps only the ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata` still runs on a LOAD.**  MISS.  **This is the `asicindexcase` (d) shape exactly: the guard is kept and the repair that stops the clobber is nulled** -- the host lives and the guest's load still zeroes the ISR.  *** AND PASS 1 ALREADY ASKED FOR THIS ROW (Q5) AND IT IS STILL NOT THERE -- which is the `#490` lesson repeating: a prescribed row lost between the pass that prescribed it and the list that was written. ***  **(5) `M_wf20_abort_wr` / `M_wf28_abort_wr`** -- `writeflag` present, `abort()` kept INSIDE the write arm.  MISS, because there is no `kill 20 wr` and no `kill 28 wr` row: the probe only ever LOADS at those two offsets. **TWO HONEST NON-BREACHES, and recording them matters as much as the misses:** `M_wf28_store_out` is **EQUIVALENT, not a breach** -- `idata` is 0 on reads (`:87-90`) so `&= ~0` is a no-op, and *a mutant that will not die is a RESULT*.  `M_one_latch_bit` (tick and ISR sharing one `warned`) is **NOT a (d) floor cell**, because the two guards live in DIFFERENT FUNCTIONS and DEPENDS-ON is walked only to the enclosing function boundary, never into callers -- so it is **L3 / contract disclosure, not L2**.  *That bound is what stops this becoming `#392`, and the seat applied it against its own finding.* **ROWS OWED BEFORE THE FIX SHIPS:** a TICK row (arm bit 50 with `sd`, step, expect the announce -- note the existing tick witness is a SEPARATE probe, so either it folds in or the contract must state the round ships TWO detectors); **`kill 20 wr` and `kill 28 wr`**; the **ISR-UNCHANGED** row (seed ISR, load at 0x10020, read back at 0x10030); and a `txt` sibling for the 0x10030 write so `M_latch_silent_30` cannot delete the diagnostic silently. **AND ONE FORWARD-LOOKING MUTANT WORTH THE CONTRACT:** `M_wf20_len8` -- `writeflag == MEM_WRITE && len == 8` -- misses today only because there is no write row at 0x10020, and **HEART registers are `u64`, so it becomes real the moment a write row is added using `sw`**.  That is the `sd`/`sw` trap from the tick measurement arriving in the detector.  *** THE OWED ROWS ARE WRITTEN (L1 CONSTRUCTION, NOT YET RUN). ***  Five mutants that scored 12/12 against the probe are now closed by construction: **`tick 50`** (closes M_tick_abort -- which is ALSO clause (b) at site 4, a VERBATIM REINSTATEMENT the probe could not see -- and M_tick_swap); **`tick 49`**, its DISCRIMINATOR, so `tick 50` cannot pass for an unrelated reason and M_tick_bit49 cannot walk free; **`kill 20 wr` / `kill 28 wr`**, because the probe only ever LOADED at those offsets so an abort() kept inside the WRITE arm was invisible; **`isr keep`**, the `asicindexcase` row that closes M_wf20_store_out -- the writeflag wrapping only the ANNOUNCE while `d->isr = idata` still runs on a load, which leaves the host ALIVE and the ISR CLOBBERED, worse than the defect being fixed; and **`kill 30 txt`**, a diagnosis sibling for the 0x10030 write arm so M_latch_silent_30 cannot delete its announce silently. *** THE TICK ROW REQUIRED A NEW OPCODE, AND THAT IS THE TRAP, NOT A DETAIL: the probe had ONLY `sw`, and a 4-byte store CANNOT SET imask0 BIT 50 because `case 0x10000` reads through `memory_readmax64`.  A tick row written with the existing helper would arm NOTHING, stay alive, and SCORE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH. ***  Added `OP_SD_T0_A3` (0xFCE80000) and `OP_LD_V0_AT` (0xDC220000), and **DECODED BOTH FROM OUTSIDE THE CODE before trusting them** -- opcode 63 = sd with base $7/rt $8, opcode 55 = ld with base $1/rt $2 -- because this project's hand-assembled-encoding trap is that a wrong constant is invisible when the probe reads the same constant. **`isr keep` READS `died` PRE-FIX AND THAT IS CORRECT**: its seeding write at 0x10020 itself aborts on the committed build.  That is part of the reproduction, not an apparatus failure, and the row comment says so. **NOT YET RUN** -- five grok seats are loading the machine and this probe's settle/spin are LOAD-SENSITIVE oracles.  The new rows are CONSTRUCTED, not MEASURED, and the pre-fix run owes a re-run with this instrument (a comparison owes ONE instrument).  *** L4 -- SIX PASS-1 SEAT CELLS CARRIED ONE STAMPED NOTE, CORRECTED 2026-09-17 FROM EACH SEAT'S OWN FILE. ***  `precommit_check.sh` section O caught it (`BOILERPLATE_FAIL`, `ip30abortkill x6`), and the defect is not untidiness.  **The six seats named FIVE different weakest dispositions** -- agy #6/#4, grok its own reframing, glm **#1**, kimi **#4**, deepseek **#5**, minimax **#6** -- **and split THREE WAYS on Q3**: agy/grok/kimi say the `0x10020` read returns **0**, deepseek says it returns **`d->isr`**, and minimax says it is **not settleable from the packet without the HEART spec**.  *A stamped note renders that as unanimity* -- the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape reached by a different road, since every seat here really did answer. **AND THE ERASED DISSENT WAS THE LOAD-BEARING ONE.**  minimax's refusal to guess on Q3 is what made fetching an IP30/HEART reference a decision for the owner instead of letting three seats' confident `0` stand as consensus; and grok's Q6 -- *\"that is not coverage debt, that is the defect left live\"* -- contradicts three seats who said the tick row may be filed. *The `ev` fields were per-seat and correct throughout, so the answers were never lost; only the notes were stamped.  That is why the repair was a read rather than a recovery -- and it is the argument for section O existing at all, because nothing else in the harness compares one seat's cell against another's.*  *** THE 15-ROW INSTRUMENT'S PRE-FIX BASELINE WAS RUN 2026-09-17, AND IT CAUGHT A ROW THAT WAS RED ON A CORRECT BUILD.  L1 construction repair, in-round. ***  **FIRST, THE COUNT WAS WRONG IN MY OWN NOTE:** the probe emits **18** rows, not the 15 a `grep -c '^\\s*row('` reported -- the probe's own `IP30ABORT_RESULT` line is the authority, and the grep was another correlate.  Baseline: **`IP30ABORT_RESULT=10/18`**, sentinel `IP30ABORT_DONE`, preserved at `probe_HEAD_preaccfix_20260917.out`. **SEVEN OF THE EIGHT FAILURES ARE THE DEFECT REPRODUCING**, at both directions of all three offsets plus the tick and the ISR clobber: `kill 20 rd`, `kill 28 rd`, `kill 30 wr`, `kill 20 wr`, `kill 28 wr`, `tick 50`, `isr keep`.  **Every control is GREEN** -- `live` returns `0x11223344`, the accept rows are serviced, and *** `tick 49` STAYS ALIVE WHERE `tick 50` DIES ***, which is the discriminating pair proving it is bit 50 rather than the tick path generally. *** THE EIGHTH FAILURE WAS NOT THE DEFECT AND NOT A SECOND ONE: `acc 20000` EXPECTED A COUNTER NOT TO COUNT. ***  It stored `0x1234` and wanted it back; it read `0x161c`.  **`0x161c - 0x1234 = 0x3e8 = 1000` EXACTLY**, which is `d->reg_0x20000 += 1000` in `DEVICE_TICK` -- one tick between the store and the load.  *The DEVICE was right and the ROW was wrong.*  The access arm alone reads like a plain r/w register; only the file's own `/*  A counter  */` comment and the tick body say otherwise. **AS WRITTEN IT MANUFACTURED A PHANTOM FAILURE ON A CORRECT BUILD, so it could never signal the defect this probe exists for** -- the vacuity class inside the round's own witness, and the reason it is L1 (pre-ship construction) rather than filed. **THE REPLACEMENT ORACLE IS STRICTLY STRONGER THAN THE ONE IT REPLACES.**  The counter is always a multiple of 1000 (it starts at 0 and only ever `+= 1000`) and `0x1234` = 4660 is NOT, so `readback - 0x1234` is an exact multiple of 1000 **iff the store landed**, and is never one if the store was lost.  The row now pins THAT THE STORE REACHED `reg_0x20000` -- which the old literal compare only did by accident on a tickless run -- while tolerating any number of ticks. *Found by doing the test-first step the round already owed: re-running the pre-fix baseline on the CURRENT instrument, because the last recorded figure (6/9) came from a retired nine-row one.  That is the same two-instrument defect found in `sgirergbroundtrip` (4/6 vs 10/10) an hour earlier, caught here BEFORE it became a record rather than after.*  *** FIXED AND MEASURED 2026-09-17 AS `#531`.  PRE-FIX 11/18 -> POST-FIX 18/18 [18-ROW INSTRUMENT, SUPERSEDED BY 8/21 -> 21/21 -- see the correction at the end of this note], with the re-pin's own red measured separately at 8/18 [the order proof, which STAYS on the 18-row form by design]. ***  All four guest-reachable `abort()` calls de-fatalised; live `abort()` 4 -> 0, `return` statements 6 -> 6 (no early return, the common tail still runs). **The disposition was settled by `heart.h`, the header this file cites three times**: `set_isr`/`clear_isr` are write-side (member 4 on WRITE, member 3 on READ) and `isr` is READ-ONLY, so `0x10030`'s clear-on-write is dropped -- *the file's own \"TODO: is this correct?\" answered NO by its own source*. **Two L1 row defects the fix exposed, both repaired in-round:** `acc 20000` expected a counter not to count (`0x161c - 0x1234 = 1000` exactly, the tick stride) and was RED ON A CORRECT BUILD; `isr keep` read the FIRST of three loads instead of the last, invisible pre-fix because its seeding write aborted. **OWED BEFORE CLOSE: panel pass 2 on the real diff.**  *** PASS 2 DID NOT RATIFY (2026-09-17, panel _scratchpad/panel_20260917_215210).  SEVEN scriptable seats answered; the kimi CLI was on its weekly 403 wall and is COVERED by its kimi-k3:cloud substitute, not lost.  opus5 STILL RUNNING; fable5 HELD by standing directive until every other seat has spoken.  So this stage stands at 7 of 9 and any ruling drawn from it is PROVISIONAL ON ITS FACE. ***  FOUR findings, each verified by me against the code rather than taken on the seat's word.  (L4) the latch comment's 'ONE BIT PER SITE' is false -- five ISR paths share one bit -- REPAIRED to state the real invariant, that latch granularity must match the marker's qualification: both messages end in an unqualified '(once)', which claims one emission per device instance, so one bit apiece is what makes the marker true.  (L1) the tick pair stopped discriminating post-fix, REPAIRED by re-aiming both rows onto the announce channel and adding 'tick 50 txt'.  (L1) 'kill 30 txt' was bound to the 'isr keep' transcript and read the 0x10020 announce -- vacuous for its own named mutant; STAGED.  (L2 FLOOR BREACH) nothing observed that d->isr &= ~idata still runs at 0x10028, a clause-(d) clean sweep four seats reached independently; STAGED as row 'isr clr', with 'isr drop 30' beside it for the re-inserted clear at 0x10030.  The three staged repairs were deliberately NOT applied while the 19-row measurement was executing -- a comparison run owes ONE instrument.  Round figures will be cited from a single 21-row instrument; the 18-row form is frozen at ip30_abort_probe.18row_20260917.frozen.py so the opus table stays attributable.  *** THESE REPAIRS OWE THEIR OWN PASS 2, per the repair-chain rule. ***  *** 21-ROW FIGURES MEASURED 2026-09-17: 8/21 pre-fix, 21/21 post-fix, on ONE instrument. The 18-row pair (11/18, 18/18) is SUPERSEDED rather than stale -- both were correct measurements of an instrument that then grew by three repairs and two floor rows, and citing a pre-fix number from one instrument beside a post-fix number from another is exactly what the one-instrument rule refuses. ***  *** THE FIGURES IN THIS NOTE ARE SUPERSEDED AND ARE CORRECTED HERE RATHER THAN REWRITTEN, so the record shows what was claimed and when.  `PRE-FIX 11/18 -> POST-FIX 18/18' was a correct measurement of the 18-ROW instrument, taken before pass 2.  Pass 2 then found four defects; repairing them added `tick 50 txt', re-bound `kill 30 txt', and added the two floor rows `isr clr' and `isr drop 30', growing the instrument to 21 rows.  THE ROUND'S FIGURES ARE 8/21 PRE-FIX (PREFIX_21row_20260917.out) AND 21/21 POST-FIX (probe_FIX531_21row_20260917.out), both on that one instrument.  Note also that this note's `OWED BEFORE CLOSE: panel pass 2' line is DISCHARGED -- the pass ran, did not ratify, and its findings are recorded in the review entries on this row. ***  *** THE opus5 PASS-2 SEAT LANDED AND DID NOT RATIFY: SIX CLEAN SWEEPS AT 19/19. ***  The stage is now 8 of 9; fable5 remains held by standing directive.  The fix is APPROVED UNCHANGED by that seat; the DETECTOR owes a CLASS CLOSURE under the #499 amendment -- two consecutive passes have now found breaches of one class (announce ATTRIBUTION), so the next repair enumerates the whole space from the function's own inputs and closes every cell in ONE diff rather than cell by cell.  THE CLASS: six announce sites, of which only a SINGLE-ACCESS session can attribute one, because all five ISR sites print one identical string behind one shared bit.  *** KILL TABLE 1: THE FLOOR IS DISCHARGED BY MEASUREMENT, NOT BY 21/21. *** Six mutants in a scratch clone; baseline NAMED (the round's post-fix working set, not HEAD), cmp'd SAME before mutant 1, rebuilt in the clone with [binary -nt source] as its own freshness proof; clone-local sentinel; restore from the named baseline, never a script backup; restore verified cmp SAME against SEC and the sentinel cleared only after the rebuild was fresh.  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE.  base 21/21 | M1_del28store 20/21 (isr clr, 0x5a5a->0x5050) | M2_reins30clear 20/21 (isr drop 30, 0x484a->0x5a5a) | M3_tickquiet 20/21 (tick 50 txt) | M4_quiet30 20/21 (kill 30 txt) | M5_bit49 19/21 (tick 50 txt AND tick 49, in OPPOSITE directions) | M6_sharedlatch 21/21 CLEAN SWEEP, ESCAPED.  EVERY REPAIR MADE IN RESPONSE TO A SEAT IS NOW MEASURED FAILABLE: M1 is the opus5 seat's severest escape (19/19 pre-repair) and the row four packet-fed seats converged on kills it; M4 is agy's cell, 19/19 on the old binding and dead on the re-bound row; M2's 0x484a matches the arithmetic derived BEFORE the run.  M5's signature is not a red but a MOVE -- the announce leaves bit 50 and appears at bit 49, which only that mutant produces.  *** M6 ESCAPED AND WAS PREDICTED IN WRITING BEFORE THE RUN: PRETABLE.out (mtime 22:58:19, against the table's 22:59:36) names it, derives it -- every session is a fresh process, so no row exercises both announce classes in one device lifetime -- and discloses it.  The pre-table block asked its first mandated application to CHECK the CHANNEL rule rather than obey it; this is the first test of 'a row's channel under its mutant is knowable at WRITING time' in this project, and it HELD. ***  CLASS CLOSURE APPLIED (21 -> 27 rows) under the #499 amendment -- two consecutive passes found breaches of ONE class, so the whole announce-attribution space is enumerated from the function's own inputs and closed in ONE diff: the two unattributed WRITE announces, the latch COLLISION, the latch DEFEAT, and the declined read's VALUE.  Two oracle shapes are new to this probe: `latch 1x' COUNTS announces (a defeated latch reads `yes' to a presence oracle either way) and `xbit' puts BOTH announce classes in ONE device lifetime, which no fresh-process row can do.  KILL TABLE 2 IS RUNNING to VERIFY THE ENUMERATION, with PRETABLE2.out written first (23:44:37 against 23:44:43).  *** KILL TABLE 2: THE CLASS CLOSURE VERIFIED, ZERO NEW BREACHES -- the #499 stop condition MET, with its denominator stated. ***  base 27/27 | M6_sharedlatch 26/27 (xbit isr, 0->1) | M7_silent20wr 25/27 (kill 20 wr txt AND xbit isr) | M8_silent28wr 26/27 (kill 28 wr txt) | M9_nolatch 26/27 (latch 1x, 2->1) | M10_mirror20 26/27 (isr 20 rd val, 0x5a5a->0x0) | M1_del28store 26/27 (isr clr, 0x5a5a->0x5050).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, binary fresh, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  *** M6 IS THE POINT: 21/21 CLEAN SWEEP BEFORE, 26/27 AFTER. ***  DENOMINATOR: six announce sites, two latch failure modes, one declined-read value.  The two axes NOT swept -- width/len (#499's own axis) and position/translation (clause (f)) -- are DISCLOSED in the probe's contract, not left absent.  *** M9 PROVES THE COUNT ORACLE WAS NECESSARY RATHER THAN DECORATIVE: latch 1x got=2 want=1.  A PRESENCE oracle reads `yes' on the fix AND on the mutant and cannot see the defect at all -- the CHANNEL vacuity arriving one level along, inside the repair for it. ***  M1 WAS RE-RUN ON PURPOSE: #462 measured that adding rows perturbs other mutants' scores, and PRETABLE2.out stated plainly that `six new independent sessions cannot perturb it' was REASONED NOT MEASURED, naming M1 as its single test.  Identical to table 1 -- the claim is now measured.  SIGNATURES DISAMBIGUATE, WITH A COST: M6 reds xbit isr ALONE; M7 reds kill 20 wr txt AND xbit isr, because the xbit session's ISR access is itself a 0x10020 write.  So xbit isr is NOT a pure collision detector, and only kill 20 wr txt separates the two causes -- the two rows are COUPLED, and the probe header now says so.  CONTRACT (clause (e)) WRITTEN INTO THE PROBE HEADER, where the floor requires it, together with the ATTRIBUTION RULE (a row can attribute an announce only from a SINGLE-ACCESS session) so a future row cannot repeat the kill-30-txt defect by accident.  The header's own stale claim -- `a WITNESS attempt, NOT a shipped detector' -- was corrected in place.  *** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST PRECISE: THE MISSING ROW WAS PRESCRIBED BY NAME, IN ONE LINE, IN A DURABLE ARTIFACT, BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED. ***  regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt:239 reads: '5. `isr 20 rd' / `isr 28 rd' -- load does not clobber ISR; load's VALUE is not the seeded ISR (don't-mirror)'.  BOTH rows, explicitly.  The round shipped `isr 20 rd val' and not its sibling, and it then cost a full extra kill table and a nine-seat adjudication pass to rediscover -- with agy REQUIRING it and grok having to point at its own earlier answer.  The opus5 seat independently reported the same pattern: grok's design pass prescribed six closing rows and items 1, 2, 4 and 5 did not ship.  *** THE STRUCTURAL GAP, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: THE CARRIER'S EXISTING RULE CANNOT CATCH THIS. ***  After #490 the rule is 'a row list rewritten from a panel's findings is DIFFED against the pre-table block's own prescribed rows, not merely extended with the new ones.'  That binds the PRETABLE.  Here the prescription NEVER ENTERED A PRETABLE -- it lived in a SEAT'S FIX-DESIGN ANSWER, and nothing diffs a row list against that.  So the #490 rule was obeyed and the row was still lost: the diff ran against the wrong source document.  AND THE PRETABLE MADE IT WORSE RATHER THAN NEUTRAL: PRETABLE.out listed BOTH read arms as 'decline, no value to pin', so the one artifact #490 DOES mandate diffing against positively asserted that neither row was owed.  *A pre-table block that mis-classifies a cell does not merely fail to catch the omission; it CERTIFIES it.*  grok's correction is one sentence: A DECLINE HAS A VALUE, AND IT IS 0.  PROPOSED REMEDY, FILED NOT BUILT (a harness change owing two negative controls): a round whose fix design was reviewed by a seat DIFFS ITS FINAL ROW LIST AGAINST THAT SEAT'S PRESCRIBED ROWS, as #490 mandates for the PRETABLE -- and the PRETABLE should CARRY those prescriptions forward so the two documents cannot disagree silently.  Cheapest mechanical form is existence-only: if a FIXDIFF_<seat>_*.txt sits beside the killtable dir, each of its numbered prescriptions must map to a shipped row or an explicit no-row sentence.  *** KILL TABLE 3: THE SIBLING-PARITY REPAIR, MEASURED. ***  base 28/28 | M11_mirror28 27/28 (`isr 28 rd val', 0x5a5a -> 0x0).  Sentinels verified; restore cmp SAME against SEC; clone removed.  PRETABLE3.out written 00:49:41 against the table's 00:50:16.  *** PRETABLE.out AND PRETABLE2.out WERE DELIBERATELY NOT EDITED TO CARRY THE CORRECTION: their mtimes ARE the order proofs for tables 1 and 2, and editing either would reset the mtime and DESTROY the property the block exists to provide.  A correction to an order-proof artifact goes in the NEXT block. ***  The contract in the probe header now names BOTH read arms for the mirroring claim; it previously named only 0x10020, which was an overclaim of exactly the kind clause (e) is meant to prevent.  *** THE §F2 ADJUDICATION PASS RAN ON 8 OF 9 (seven scriptable seats + the opus5 seat outstanding at the time of writing); FABLE REMAINS HELD BY STANDING DIRECTIVE AND SPEAKS LAST.  Any ruling here is PROVISIONAL ON ITS FACE. ***  It found ONE floor breach (`isr 28 rd val'), which is repaired and measured, and TWO wrong warrants of mine, both corrected.  The F13 split (2 REFINE / 1 UPHOLD) is RECORDED, NOT RESOLVED -- the stopping rule files it, and a disagreement must not be settled by whoever writes it up.  *** THE opus5 §F2 SEAT: EIGHT MUTANTS, ALL 27/27 -- TOTAL CLEAN SWEEPS OF THE SHIPPED INSTRUMENT -- AND SIX LIE INSIDE CELLS PRETABLE2's OWN ENUMERATION DECLARES CLOSED. ***  Scratch clone, baseline NAMED (md5 0f012a03175541c085d90331ae395abe) and cmp SAME before mutant 1, rebuilt in-clone with -nt verified, clone-local sentinel, instrument FROZEN (probe.frozen.py md5 ca309b97085608e9fb4eb0ff0be08095) for the whole comparison, every mutant dry-run-diffed to exactly one line, MUTANT-TEXT-NOT-FOUND a hard fail.  Every log ends IP30ABORT_DONE with IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK.  Artifacts PRESERVED BY THE MAIN LOOP to regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/opusF2_20260918/ because the seat is read-only and said so rather than writing into the repo.  THE EIGHT: MA_assign20wr (|= -> =, 0x10020-WR latch set, ONE CHARACTER) | MD_assigntick (same at TICK) | MB_nolatchset20wr | MF_nolatchset28rd | MG_nolatchset30wr | ME_nolatchsettick (all: delete the latch SET) | MH_nocounter (delete d->reg_0x20000 += 1000) | MC_len4 (&& len == 4 on the 0x10020 write guard).  *** IT OVERTURNED ITS OWN F17 AS UNDER-STATED, WHICH IS THE FINDING I WAS LEAST LIKELY TO REACH: 'THE LATCH IS NOT A SHARED RESOURCE.  IT IS SIX DUPLICATED STATEMENTS, ONE BESIDE EVERY ANNOUNCE -- the same duplication the announce axis was decomposed for.' ***  The real space is {6 sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} = twelve cells, plus {2 collision directions} = FOURTEEN.  The closure rowed SIX.  In its own words: 'my finding bought you a repair for the two instances I happened to build, and the packet closed the CELLS rather than the CLASS -- which is the #499 shape, and I handed you the material for it.'  WHY THE TWO NEW ROWS CLOSE LESS THAN THEY APPEAR TO, and both reasons are structural:  (a) `latch 1x' closes exactly ONE cell -- it visits 0x10020-RD then 0x10028-RD, and the FIRST access latches, so the SECOND arm's latch-set is never load-bearing.  MF_nolatchset28rd deletes the latch set in an arm that row literally traverses: 27/27.  (b) `xbit isr'/`xbit tick' closes NEITHER collision direction.  Its session runs TICK then ISR -- the OPPOSITE order from the hazard the product comment names ('a guest write to Set-ISR would silence the TIMER diagnostic forever').  Seeing that needs tick -> ISR -> tick.  MA and MD both 27/27.  *** THAT IS A CLAIM -> ROW FAILURE ON THE ROUND'S OWN LOAD-BEARING PRODUCT COMMENT, and the pre-table CHANNEL check should have caught it at WRITING time. ***  The seat noted the gap is not carelessness: the probe SINGLE-STEPS, and a second tick costs ~65536 steps under machine.c's ticks_till_next arithmetic, so the cell is open because the probe's SHAPE made it expensive.  A free-running breakpoint-and-continue session (the sh_intprio_probe.py shape) gets it cheaply.  *** AND F1's INVARIANT HAS NO ROW PINNING ITS OBSERVABLE HALF: ISRMSG/TICKMSG stop at '...not implemented' and EXCLUDE `(once)', so deleting the suppression marker from all six strings passes all 27 rows. ***  The round's central design claim -- latch granularity matches the marker's qualification -- is unobserved on the marker side.  Derived by reading, not measured.  F6: *** MEASURED FALSE AND CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `acc 20000' accepts (readback - 0x1234) % 1000 == 0 and N = 0 IS ADMISSIBLE, so with the increment deleted the readback is exactly 0x1234, residue 0, row GREEN (MH_nocounter 27/27).  Re-derived here independently before the correction.  The comment had been wrong TWICE -- first crediting `tick 49', then my correction crediting `acc 20000' -- and the CHANGELOG's 'strictly stronger' was also false: stronger for STORE REACHABILITY, weaker for COUNTER LIVENESS.  Both corrected; the real state is an L3, the de-escalation ACCEPT side of the de-fatalised TICK arm, unpinned.  D2 ANSWERED, which no other seat could: asked to name a regression the re-pinned oracle was pinned PAST, it named THE DELETION OF THE COUNTER INCREMENT and measured it.  THE TWO DISCLOSED AXES, RULED ON: WIDTH/len is NOT floor by the pcc2widthkills bound (the body never tests len) -- but it is now a MEASURED escape (MC_len4 27/27) and the seat notes the reason clause (e) excuses value-domains is ABSENT here: len takes at most four values and the probe already has guest_store64/guest_load64.  It also observed that d->isr is uint64_t while EVERY value the probe drives is <= 16 bits, so `d->isr &= ~(uint32_t)idata' -- a plausible C slip wiping the top 32 bits on every clear_isr -- is green by construction.  POSITION/TRANSLATION: the seat says my disclosure UNDERSTATES coverage, because isr keep / isr clr / isr drop 30 all write at 0x10020/0x10028 and read back at 0x10030, which IS clause (f)'s prescribed anchor (a second access path to the same cell).  What remains unanchored is cross-FIELD aliasing.  SEVERITY RANKING IT SUPPLIED, so the repair is scoped rather than chased: (1) ME_nolatchsettick is the worst -- one guest `sd' arming imask0 bit 50 yields AN ANNOUNCE PER TICK, FOREVER, which is floodclass, guest-drivable, on the arm this round de-fatalised; (2) MH_nocounter is the L3; (3) MA/MD falsify the two-bit design's stated reason; (4) MB/MF/MG are bounded per-arm floods.  *** THE CHAIN DOES NOT CLOSE, WITH ITS DENOMINATOR STATED: diff delivered and read in full; roster INCOMPLETE (fable5 held at the time); swept 4 latch-set sites x delete, 2 collision directions x |= -> =, 1 width conjunct, 1 tick accept-side = EIGHT mutant shapes across FOUR axes, EIGHT breaches, ZERO clean. ***  RECORDS ITEMS IT RAISED: (a) the witness pair 8/21 -> 21/21 is cited from an instrument that no longer ships -- the detector is 28 rows and there is no pre-fix run at that width; it warns explicitly DO NOT DERIVE 8/27 ON PAPER, which is the frozen-number class.  The main loop had recorded this as OWED in OUTSTANDING_BUGS before the seat landed, independently.  (b) ip30setisrassign in ledger.json and not OUTSTANDING_BUGS -- ALREADY DISCHARGED: the main loop entered it after the seat began, the same time-indexing effect the #507 case showed.  AND IT RECORDED AN APPARATUS FAILURE OF ITS OWN, unprompted: its first table-2 mutator was written through a bash heredoc and lost a backslash level in each direction, turning a Python string literal into a syntax error.  The table aborted at mutant 1 with the tree still cmp SAME -- the carrier's documented hazard, and the hard-fail-on-text-not-found rule is what stopped it mis-mutating quietly.  *The main loop hit the identical trap on the same file the same night and fixed it the same way, with chr(10).*  *** THE fable5 ADJUDICATION -- NINTH AND LAST SEAT, ROSTER NOW 9/9, THE §F2 RULING IS NO LONGER PROVISIONAL. ***  SEAT VERIFIED BY EVIDENCE, NOT ASSUMED, per the owner's standing requirement: it returned the liveness token FABLE_ADJ_ALIVE_20260918, the VERBATIM first line of CHECKPOINT.md ('# CHECKPOINT -- 2026-09-18 ~00:4x', which matches what the main loop wrote), and NAMED ITS OWN MODEL -- Claude Fable 5.1 (claude-fable-5-1).  It also stated it had verified the tree under review was GXEMUL-SEC/src/ rather than a 2021 copy before ruling.  *** VERDICT: RATIFY THE FIX; DO NOT RATIFY THE DETECTOR AS SHIPPED.  THE LATCH-AXIS CLOSURE IS IN-ROUND, NOT FILED. ***  Q1 WARRANT, and it is the part that settles a genuine rule collision the main loop refused to resolve by writing it up: SIX of opus5's eight escapes (MA, MB, MD, ME, MF, MG) are clause-(d) mutants of predicates THE FIX'S OWN DIFF INTRODUCED -- the `d->warned |= IP30_W_*' sets -- which is the canonical 'keep the guard, null its repair' shape, and the vacuity floor makes that L2: replace IN-ROUND however late it is found.  Independently, the shipped CONTRACT claimed to catch 'collapsing the two latch bits' and 'defeating the latch mask outright', both MEASURED FALSE -- an L4 that must be edited in-round anyway, and once the contract is being corrected and the cells are buildable the floor demands rows rather than a file-the-rest note.  Filing was REFUSED specifically because this closure was mis-declared complete once already.  ONE HONEST CARVE-OUT it allowed: a cell genuinely UNBUILDABLE with this fresh-process apparatus may be disclosed with its apparatus reason -- but only after being MEASURED unbuildable.  The single candidate is ME (the flood cell), which needs TWO forced DEVICE_TICKs.  *** Q2: IT REFUTED THE MAIN LOOP'S OWN CORRECTION, AND THE REFUTATION WAS VERIFIED HERE IN ONE LOOK. ***  The main loop had graded the unpinned counter an L3 DE-ESCALATION OBLIGATION.  DEVICE_TICK reads `d->reg_0x20000 += 1000;' UNCONDITIONALLY, two lines ABOVE the `if (d->imask0 & ((int64_t)1<<50))' guard the fix de-fatalised.  The counter runs on EVERY tick regardless of bit 50, so MH_nocounter mutates PRE-EXISTING, FIX-INDEPENDENT behaviour: COVERAGE DEBT, filed as optional, NOT a clause-(d)/L3 obligation.  And the accept side of the predicate that WAS de-fatalised is pinned, by `tick 50' plus `tick 50 txt' -- so the round's accept-side claim is TRUE and the main loop's correction impugned it wrongly.  *** THE LINEAGE IS THE LESSON: `tick 49' -> `acc 20000' -> 'L3 obligation'.  THREE CLAIMS, TWO OF THEM CORRECTIONS, AND ONLY THE L4 DELETION WAS RIGHT.  In its words: 'the correction inherited more authority than the fact, and the over-grade then propagated into the CHANGELOG.' ***  CORRECTED IN BOTH PLACES.  Q3: UPHELD opus5 -- F13, not F10, was the disposition to least trust, and the main loop's §OWED misfiled its own doubt.  Verified against the diff: the read (else) arms fired `d->warned |= IP30_W_ISR' plus the shared write-side message, so a read DID NOT GET an announce -- it CONSUMED the shared bit for all five paths, and by P3's byte-identity a single BENIGN guest READ of 0x10020 permanently silenced the genuine write-side diagnostic.  *** AND IT NAMED THE INCOHERENT CELL: axes {read announces Y/N} x {message per-offset-distinct Y/N}; rejecting F10 while keeping F13 sits at (announce Y, distinct N) -- an undistinguished FALSE message that steals the write-side bit, defensible on neither axis. ***  RULING: adopt MEMBER 2, SILENT DECLINE, for both read arms.  APPLIED, and it SHRANK the closure: ISR announce sites 5 -> 3, so the space is {4 sites} x {announce, latch} + 2 directions = TEN cells, not fourteen.  *Resolving the design question removed four cells of detector work rather than adding them.*  Q4: CONVERGING, NOT #392 -- the FIX has been stable and approved since pass 1 and every pass has been about the DETECTOR -- *** BUT CLOSURE WAS DECLARED ONE PASS EARLY AND THAT DECLARATION IS FALSIFIED. ***  Kill table 2's enumeration counted the latch mechanism as TWO cells when it is roughly eight, so 'ZERO NEW BREACHES / the closing pass VERIFIED THE ENUMERATION' is repair-chain Correction 1 exactly: A BARE ZERO AGAINST A WRONG ENUMERATION DOES NOT CLOSE A CHAIN -- and this round supplied the counter-example to its own citation of that rule.  CORRECTED IN PLACE.  *** THE PRE-COMMITTED FORK THAT MAKES IT TERMINATE: one closure diff, one verifying pass whose job is to confirm ZERO cells outside the stated enumeration with its denominator named.  If that pass finds only already-enumerated cells now killed -> THE CHAIN CLOSES.  If a fifth mutant escapes OUTSIDE the enumeration -> the fresh-process-probe-versus-shared-lifetime-latch ARCHITECTURE is the root cause, and the latch axis drops WHOLESALE to disclosed debt rather than being chased into pass six. ***  D1 UPHELD (TWO bits, one per distinct MESSAGE).  D2 PARTLY UPHELD, and it answered glm's challenge concretely: the regression the re-pinned oracle was pinned PAST is THE ENTIRE LATCH AXIS -- the green instrument was blind to MA through MG.  WHAT ALL NINE MISSED, in its words: nobody enumerated the latch failure modes PER-SITE, PER-DIRECTION until opus5, and P3's byte-identity -- cited as correct design AND as the thing that hid F3 -- is also what made the read-arm announce a false, bit-stealing diagnostic.  *** COMMIT IS HELD on five obligations.  FOUR ARE DONE: the counter regrade, the contract correction, the stop-condition correction, and the F13 silent decline.  THE FIFTH -- the latch-axis closure rows -- REMAINS.  The fix itself ships unchanged. ***  *** KILL TABLE 4 -- THE VERIFYING PASS fable5 PRE-COMMITTED.  SIX OF EIGHT SCORED AS PREDICTED IN WRITING BEFOREHAND, AND BOTH MISSES WERE MINE. ***  base 32/32 | V1_assign20wr 31/32 (xbit rev) | V3_nolatch20wr 31/32 (latch 1x) | V4_nolatch28wr 31/32 (latch 28wr) | V5_nolatch30wr 31/32 (latch 30wr) | V6_nolatchtick 30/32 (latch tick AND xbit rev -- killed by TWO rows where one was predicted) | V7_nocounter 32/32 ESCAPED AS PREDICTED | V2_assigntick 32/32 ESCAPED (predicted KILL) | V8_len4 28/32 KILLED FOUR ROWS (predicted ESCAPE).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  PRETABLE4.out 03:06:49 against the table's 03:06:58.  *** THE TWO MISSES ARE DIFFERENT KINDS OF WRONG AND CONFLATING THEM WOULD HAVE HIDDEN ONE. ***  (A) V2 -- A ROW I BUILT WRONG.  A clobber can only bite if the clobbered class's bit was ALREADY SET.  `xbit isr' runs TICK -> ISR, so when the tick assigns, the ISR bit has never been set, nothing is lost, and the row is GREEN on the mutant (derived: warned=0x0 -> tick assigns -> warned=0x1; !(0x1 & 0x2) is TRUE, so the ISR still announces, count 1).  I gave the mirror the opposite ORDER but not the opposite OPERAND-AND-COUNT.  *** THE #499 SHAPE A THIRD TIME THIS ROUND, AND IT IS THE SAME DEFECT THE opus5 SEAT DIAGNOSED IN THE ORIGINAL `xbit', REPRODUCED INSIDE THE REPAIR FOR IT. ***  REPAIRED: `xbit rev2' (ISR -> TICK -> ISR, counting ISRMSG), ADDED rather than re-aimed because `xbit isr' still carries a real claim.  (B) V8 -- A MUTANT I BUILT WRONG, AND THIS IS THE MORE DANGEROUS FAILURE BECAUSE IT LOOKS LIKE A SUCCESS.  It was anchored on the FIRST two-tab `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)' in the file, which is at :172 inside `case 0x10000' -- THE INTERRUPT MASK REGISTER, not 0x10020.  Every tick row arms bit 50 with a 64-bit `sd' (len 8), so `&& len == 4' REFUSED that store, bit 50 was never set, no tick announced, and four tick rows reddened FOR A REASON UNRELATED TO THE CELL UNDER TEST.  *** HAD I NOT CHECKED **WHICH** ROWS DIED I WOULD HAVE RECORDED THE WIDTH AXIS AS COVERED WHEN IT WAS NEVER TESTED. ***  A mutant that dies for the wrong reason misleads exactly as much as one that will not die; the carrier's three explanations apply and this is case 1, MIS-BUILT.  REPAIRED: anchored on guard #3 (:202, case 0x10020), verified by a dry-run diff that shows it landing in the right arm.  *** AND THE MIS-BUILD LEFT A REAL FINDING, KEPT RATHER THAN DISCARDED: THE DETECTOR DOES CATCH A WIDTH NARROWING AT imask0, WITH A FOUR-ROW SIGNATURE -- coverage on an arm this round never enumerated.  The tick rows collectively pin that imask0 accepts a 64-bit write.  Not a floor cell (the fix does not touch imask0), but real, and found by accident. ***  V7 ESCAPING EXACTLY AS PREDICTED IS ITSELF A RESULT: a written prediction of an ESCAPE landing is worth as much as a kill landing, because it says the disclosure is HONEST rather than a hedge.  SIGNATURES DISAMBIGUATE: V1 reds `xbit rev' ALONE; V6 reds `latch tick' AND `xbit rev'.  So `xbit rev' is not a pure collision detector and only `latch tick' separates the two causes -- the rows are COUPLED, the same shape already recorded for xbit isr / kill 20 wr txt, and a future reader pruning `redundant' rows would destroy the discrimination.  TABLE 5 IS RUNNING to re-verify V2 and V8 and to regression-check V1 (PRETABLE5.out 04:30:15 against 04:30:25).  *** KILL TABLE 5: THE CHAIN CLOSES.  EVERY PREDICTION LANDED, INCLUDING ONE PRE-REGISTERED AFTER PRETABLE5 WAS WRITTEN. ***  base 33/33 | V2_assigntick 32/33 (xbit rev2 -- THE ESCAPE CLOSED; it swept 32/32 clean before) | V8_len4 33/33 ESCAPED AS PREDICTED, now measured at the CORRECT cell | V1_assign20wr 32/33 (xbit rev ALONE).  Every log carries IP30ABORT_DONE; restore cmp SAME against SEC, sentinel cleared, clone removed.  PRETABLE5.out 04:30:15 against the table's 04:30:25.  *** THE DENOMINATOR, because a bare zero against a wrong enumeration does not close a chain -- this round supplied that counter-example itself at table 2. ***  TEN CELLS: {4 announce sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} + {2 collision directions}.  Six closed before table 4; tables 4 and 5 killed the remaining four (V1/V2 the two collision directions, V4/V5 the two remaining latch-sets, V6 the tick latch-set).  TWO cells are DISCLOSED WITH A RULING and BOTH WERE MEASURED TO ESCAPE -- the counter (fix-independent, fable5) and the width axis at 0x10020 (outside the floor by the pcc2widthkills bound).  ZERO CELLS OUTSIDE THE ENUMERATION.  *** That is the fable5 fork's FIRST branch, so the chain CLOSES and the escape hatch -- drop the latch axis wholesale to disclosed debt -- REMAINS UNSPENT. ***  A PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION THAT HELD, recorded before the result arrived: PRETABLE5 said V1 kills via `xbit rev' but did not say whether `xbit rev2' would ALSO red.  I derived beforehand that it would not -- under V1 the ISR assignment clobbers nothing because TICK was never set, the tick then sets its own bit, and the second ISR access is correctly silent.  MEASURED: `xbit rev' ALONE.  *** So the two collision rows separate BY DIRECTION, which is stronger than either row firing on both: the signature identifies WHICH clobber, not merely that one happened. ***  V1 at 32/33 (was 31/32 with one row red in table 4) also discharges the #462 regression check: adding `xbit rev2' did not perturb it.  That claim was REASONED in PRETABLE5 and is now MEASURED.  CONTRACT UPGRADED: the width disclosure now reads MEASURED TO ESCAPE rather than 'not swept'.  *** A disclosure backed by a MEASUREMENT tells a reader which edit walks free; one backed by an OMISSION only tells them nobody looked. ***  The incidental finding from the mis-built mutant is kept in the contract too: this detector DOES catch a width narrowing at imask0, with a four-row signature -- coverage on an arm the round never enumerated.",
