tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:47258:     "ev": "regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/opusF2_20260918/ (base.raw + MA..MH .raw, probe.frozen.py, baseline, both mutators)",
tools\pipeline\ledger.json.before_nul546:47259:     "note": "*** THE opus5 §F2 SEAT: EIGHT MUTANTS, ALL 27/27 -- TOTAL CLEAN SWEEPS OF THE SHIPPED INSTRUMENT -- AND SIX LIE INSIDE CELLS PRETABLE2's OWN ENUMERATION DECLARES CLOSED. ***  Scratch clone, baseline NAMED (md5 0f012a03175541c085d90331ae395abe) and cmp SAME before mutant 1, rebuilt in-clone with -nt verified, clone-local sentinel, instrument FROZEN (probe.frozen.py md5 ca309b97085608e9fb4eb0ff0be08095) for the whole comparison, every mutant dry-run-diffed to exactly one line, MUTANT-TEXT-NOT-FOUND a hard fail.  Every log ends IP30ABORT_DONE with IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK.  Artifacts PRESERVED BY THE MAIN LOOP to regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/opusF2_20260918/ because the seat is read-only and said so rather than writing into the repo.  THE EIGHT: MA_assign20wr (|= -> =, 0x10020-WR latch set, ONE CHARACTER) | MD_assigntick (same at TICK) | MB_nolatchset20wr | MF_nolatchset28rd | MG_nolatchset30wr | ME_nolatchsettick (all: delete the latch SET) | MH_nocounter (delete d->reg_0x20000 += 1000) | MC_len4 (&& len == 4 on the 0x10020 write guard).  *** IT OVERTURNED ITS OWN F17 AS UNDER-STATED, WHICH IS THE FINDING I WAS LEAST LIKELY TO REACH: 'THE LATCH IS NOT A SHARED RESOURCE.  IT IS SIX DUPLICATED STATEMENTS, ONE BESIDE EVERY ANNOUNCE -- the same duplication the announce axis was decomposed for.' ***  The real space is {6 sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} = twelve cells, plus {2 collision directions} = FOURTEEN.  The closure rowed SIX.  In its own words: 'my finding bought you a repair for the two instances I happened to build, and the packet closed the CELLS rather than the CLASS -- which is the #499 shape, and I handed you the material for it.'  WHY THE TWO NEW ROWS CLOSE LESS THAN THEY APPEAR TO, and both reasons are structural:  (a) `latch 1x' closes exactly ONE cell -- it visits 0x10020-RD then 0x10028-RD, and the FIRST access latches, so the SECOND arm's latch-set is never load-bearing.  MF_nolatchset28rd deletes the latch set in an arm that row literally traverses: 27/27.  (b) `xbit isr'/`xbit tick' closes NEITHER collision direction.  Its session runs TICK then ISR -- the OPPOSITE order from the hazard the product comment names ('a guest write to Set-ISR would silence the TIMER diagnostic forever').  Seeing that needs tick -> ISR -> tick.  MA and MD both 27/27.  *** THAT IS A CLAIM -> ROW FAILURE ON THE ROUND'S OWN LOAD-BEARING PRODUCT COMMENT, and the pre-table CHANNEL check should have caught it at WRITING time. ***  The seat noted the gap is not carelessness: the probe SINGLE-STEPS, and a second tick costs ~65536 steps under machine.c's ticks_till_next arithmetic, so the cell is open because the probe's SHAPE made it expensive.  A free-running breakpoint-and-continue session (the sh_intprio_probe.py shape) gets it cheaply.  *** AND F1's INVARIANT HAS NO ROW PINNING ITS OBSERVABLE HALF: ISRMSG/TICKMSG stop at '...not implemented' and EXCLUDE `(once)', so deleting the suppression marker from all six strings passes all 27 rows. ***  The round's central design claim -- latch granularity matches the marker's qualification -- is unobserved on the marker side.  Derived by reading, not measured.  F6: *** MEASURED FALSE AND CORRECTED IN PLACE. ***  `acc 20000' accepts (readback - 0x1234) % 1000 == 0 and N = 0 IS ADMISSIBLE, so with the increment deleted the readback is exactly 0x1234, residue 0, row GREEN (MH_nocounter 27/27).  Re-derived here independently before the correction.  The comment had been wrong TWICE -- first crediting `tick 49', then my correction crediting `acc 20000' -- and the CHANGELOG's 'strictly stronger' was also false: stronger for STORE REACHABILITY, weaker for COUNTER LIVENESS.  Both corrected; the real state is an L3, the de-escalation ACCEPT side of the de-fatalised TICK arm, unpinned.  D2 ANSWERED, which no other seat could: asked to name a regression the re-pinned oracle was pinned PAST, it named THE DELETION OF THE COUNTER INCREMENT and measured it.  THE TWO DISCLOSED AXES, RULED ON: WIDTH/len is NOT floor by the pcc2widthkills bound (the body never tests len) -- but it is now a MEASURED escape (MC_len4 27/27) and the seat notes the reason clause (e) excuses value-domains is ABSENT here: len takes at most four values and the probe already has guest_store64/guest_load64.  It also observed that d->isr is uint64_t while EVERY value the probe drives is <= 16 bits, so `d->isr &= ~(uint32_t)idata' -- a plausible C slip wiping the top 32 bits on every clear_isr -- is green by construction.  POSITION/TRANSLATION: the seat says my disclosure UNDERSTATES coverage, because isr keep / isr clr / isr drop 30 all write at 0x10020/0x10028 and read back at 0x10030, which IS clause (f)'s prescribed anchor (a second access path to the same cell).  What remains unanchored is cross-FIELD aliasing.  SEVERITY RANKING IT SUPPLIED, so the repair is scoped rather than chased: (1) ME_nolatchsettick is the worst -- one guest `sd' arming imask0 bit 50 yields AN ANNOUNCE PER TICK, FOREVER, which is floodclass, guest-drivable, on the arm this round de-fatalised; (2) MH_nocounter is the L3; (3) MA/MD falsify the two-bit design's stated reason; (4) MB/MF/MG are bounded per-arm floods.  *** THE CHAIN DOES NOT CLOSE, WITH ITS DENOMINATOR STATED: diff delivered and read in full; roster INCOMPLETE (fable5 held at the time); swept 4 latch-set sites x delete, 2 collision directions x |= -> =, 1 width conjunct, 1 tick accept-side = EIGHT mutant shapes across FOUR axes, EIGHT breaches, ZERO clean. ***  RECORDS ITEMS IT RAISED: (a) the witness pair 8/21 -> 21/21 is cited from an instrument that no longer ships -- the detector is 28 rows and there is no pre-fix run at that width; it warns explicitly DO NOT DERIVE 8/27 ON PAPER, which is the frozen-number class.  The main loop had recorded this as OWED in OUTSTANDING_BUGS before the seat landed, independently.  (b) ip30setisrassign in ledger.json and not OUTSTANDING_BUGS -- ALREADY DISCHARGED: the main loop entered it after the seat began, the same time-indexing effect the #507 case showed.  AND IT RECORDED AN APPARATUS FAILURE OF ITS OWN, unprompted: its first table-2 mutator was written through a bash heredoc and lost a backslash level in each direction, turning a Python string literal into a syntax error.  The table aborted at mutant 1 with the tree still cmp SAME -- the carrier's documented hazard, and the hard-fail-on-text-not-found rule is what stopped it mis-mutating quietly.  *The main loop hit the identical trap on the same file the same night and fixed it the same way, with chr(10).*"
