</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">multiaccesswarms</span><span class="ttl">a probe row that tests a SLOW-PATH fix with a MULTI-ACCESS instruction is vacuous from the second access onward -- the first cold access WARMS the page and every later access takes the FAST path, so the row cannot observe a slow-path mutant at all</span><span class="tip">a probe row that tests a SLOW-PATH fix with a MULTI-ACCESS instruction is vacuous from the second access onward -- the first cold access WARMS the page and every later access takes the FAST path, so the row cannot observe a slow-path mutant at all</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE CLASS IS LIVE AND ITS POPULATION IS THE FOUNDING ROW AND NOTHING ELSE.  An honest zero of NEW unlabelled vacuous rows, carried WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: 2 helpers with the shape x 3 shipped LDM/STM probes x 3 multi-access legs. ***  DO NOT CLOSE AS CENSUS-EMPTY: `ARM_LDM_MULTI_P2_R2&#x27; is still shipped and still inside gate_arm.sh&#x27;s pinned ARMBDT 43/43 -- a gate-counted always-green slot in its own denominator.  *** NOT MECHANICALLY ENUMERABLE, exactly as the deriver-keyed-on-a-correlate rule predicts: an LDM/STM/LDRD grep fails BOTH membership halves and sweeps in six non-members, including the #378 WARM/COLD pair which is the OPPOSITE polarity (a FAST-path repair).  The grep-able CANDIDATE GENERATOR -- not the class -- is a host_load/host_store test INSIDE a register loop, i.e. arm_pop and arm_push only; a per-row reading is still owed after it. ***  TWO PREMISE CORRECTIONS TO THE FILED RULE.  (1) &#x27;every later access IN THAT SESSION&#x27; overstates it -- the warm unit is DYNTRANS_PAGESIZE 4096 indexed addr&gt;&gt;12, and returning to the GXemul&gt; prompt runs invalidate_translation_caches(INVALIDATE_ALL), so it does not survive a prompt.  (2) &#x27;more assertions mean more SESSIONS&#x27; is true but NOT REQUIRED: what the mechanism demands is more FIRST-ACCESSES TO A COLD PAGE, and a prompt return already invalidates -- so two cold observations fit in ONE process.  The intra-instruction half stands: nothing splits a multi-access loop between register i and i+1.  P2 is narrower than filed -- a slow-path mutant is invisible to access 2+ only where the helper HAS a fast arm; X(swp) and PPC lmw/stmw loop memory_rw every iteration and take none.  ONE NEW FINDING, labelling debt not a floor breach: ARM_STM_PC_DB_R7&#x27;s `w0&#x27; conjunct is access 2 (fast, 0x21003) and is unlabelled, while the row&#x27;s PC half is access 1 and still kills M_PUSHPC_NONE -- so the row is NOT vacuous and this is debt on it, not a new id and not L2.  *** HONEST LIMIT THE SEAT STATED ITSELF: it did NOT re-run the mutant table; the founding kill is taken from the round record.  This census is a READING. *** — evidence: regress/artifacts/multiaccesswarms/CENSUS_grok_20260917.txt and BRIEF_multiaccess.md; gate_arm.sh:731 (pin 43) and arm_bdt_probe.py:383 BOTH VERIFIED LOCALLY before this entry  [citations re-pointed 2026-09-17 from _scratchpad/ to the durable artifact copies; the _scratchpad originals are the same bytes]</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">silencewhitelist</span><span class="ttl">silence rows keyed on a literal or a quiet grammar are whitelists with a shape to hide in</span><span class="tip">silence rows keyed on a literal or a quiet grammar are whitelists with a shape to hide in — witness: none</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">pit8253flood</span><span class="ttl">pit8253flood  dev_8253.c: bound every unconditional emission in DEVICE_ACCESS(8253) -- LATCH :204/:270/:342, DELETE :198/:200, :351/:354 are dead code</span><span class="tip">pit8253flood  dev_8253.c: bound every unconditional emission in DEVICE_ACCESS(8253) -- LATCH :204/:270/:342, DELETE :198/:200, :351/:354 are dead code — witness: none — note: pass 1 complete 2026-09-17, nine seats + Fable adjudication.  STAYS FIRST in the queue -- Fable ruled the panel&#x27;s demotion wrong, on reach, with owner sequencing breaking a severity tie.</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — PASS-1 BRIEF WRITTEN AND STAGED, NOT FIRED -- the machine is held by #530&#x27;s kill table 7 and nine CPU-heavy seats during a timed table is the contention the carrier forbids.  Written FROM tools/BRIEF_TEMPLATE.md rather than from memory; 462 lines / 22,038 bytes, under panel.sh&#x27;s ARGV_CAP of 30000 so codex and kimi get the INLINE form rather than a pointer.  Every cited path ls-checked; the frozen source copy&#x27;s md5 was verified to match what the brief transcribes.  *** TWO FINDINGS MADE WHILE WRITING IT, BOTH BEFORE ANY SEAT WAS FIRED. ***  (1) THE SIBLING CENSUS THIS ROW ASKED FOR RETURNS SEVEN SITES, NOT TWO.  The row&#x27;s own text says &#x27;a sibling census, not an assumption&#x27; and names the writing-side default: arm.  Running it over DEVICE_ACCESS(8253) finds SEVEN unlatched fatal() sites -- the two TODO arms, BOTH huh? defaults (write AND read), the BCD arm, and the unimplemented-address write and read arms.  FOUR were not anticipated by the filing.  Only the two TODO arms are established guest-drivable; the brief asks which of the other five are, because a latch at an unreachable site is the addition licence&#x27;s &#x27;cannot hurt&#x27;.  This changes the round&#x27;s SIZE and so the row title was corrected in the same edit.  (2) A NAIVE exit-grep OVER THIS FILE RETURNS 5 AND ALL FIVE ARE COMMENT TEXT -- the #223 comments that read &#x27;warn+ignore, do not exit() the host&#x27;.  THE FILE HAS ZERO LIVE exit().  I ran that grep first and it would have put a false premise -- five surviving host kills -- into a nine-seat packet, which is the class this project records as coming back CONFIRMED by seats that cannot check it.  Settled with the project&#x27;s OWN comment-aware tool, tools/pipeline/exit_census.py, which brief template section C0 names and which I found only by running C0&#x27;s third command; it reports 137 of 269 raw hits tree-wide (51%) are inside comments, and dev_8253.c is ABSENT from its live list of 26 files.  C0 CLEARANCE RUN RATHER THAN ASSUMED: last commit touching the file is af4884c (2026-08-20, #440, a different defect) and this row id appears 0 times in CHANGELOG.md, so the round has not shipped.  STATE DELIBERATELY LEFT open, NOT in work: no seat has answered, and marking a row in work on the strength of a staged brief is the gap-closes-itself shape. — evidence: _scratchpad/pit8253flood_pass1.md (462 lines, 22038 bytes); frozen source _scratchpad/dev_8253.frozen_20260917.c md5 a081564373cf81c2cf177b75ccbbc20d; tools/pipeline/exit_census.py output; recorded in _scratchpad/CHECKPOINT.md</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — ADJUDICATION.  Identity check passed: it named itself claude-fable-5-1 AND quoted CHECKPOINT.md&#x27;s first line verbatim, so prompt delivery and file access were proven rather than assumed.  *** IT OVERTURNED ONE OF MY SEVEN ACCEPTED DISPOSITIONS. ***  A5&#x27;s CONSEQUENCE -- &#x27;do not latch :342&#x27; -- is WRONG, because it confuses a BOUNDED ANNOUNCE with SILENCE.  A (once) latch announces the event on every boot that raises it; only repetition is dropped, and repetition carries no information once the defect is FILED.  Leaving :342 unlatched ends the round with a guest-drivable unbounded fatal() still in the file under a rationale that reads as principle and is not.  RULING: LATCH :342 AND REWRITE ITS TEXT, since &#x27;not yet implemented&#x27; implies a refusal when the write is ACCEPTED.  When the BCD fix later deletes the site, its row is re-pinned to ==0 under the oracle-correction ORDER -- re-pin, show red, then edit.  D1 RULED AGAINST MY LEAN, which is what I asked for: DELETE :198/:200, do not latch.  Its decisive evidence is something I had not found -- dev_pckbc.c:941-951 FAKES both downstream observables independently of the PIT, returning 0x21 with the refresh-toggle bit flipped by a function-static counter -- so there is NO guest-observable difference between modelling counters 1 and 2&#x27;s outputs and not modelling them.  That is member 4&#x27;s definition.  And the OpenBSD bell path that DOES program counter 2 has its other half, the 0x61 speaker-enable write, already ignored SILENTLY: announcing one half of an unmodelled bell is residue, not a house idiom.  D2 accepted -- it changes each row&#x27;s ORACLE rather than the row count, plus ONE combined-session row which is the only thing that catches a cross-site field typo.  *** AND IT NAMED A LIVE HAZARD I WOULD HAVE WALKED INTO: pit8253_latch_probe.py:321 keys COMPLAINT on the literal &#x27;huh? reading from counter&#x27; and row R2 wants ZERO of them after a control word, so ANY rewrite of the :270 text turns R2 into an absence row that CANNOT FAIL. ***  D3: witnessable, ORDER-constrained, not documented-only -- and it supplied the exact session order.  ORDER: pit8253flood STAYS FIRST and the demotion is NOT right.  Neither uncovered defect has a real-guest driver (codex searched the guest sources: no BCD, no counter-1 programming), severity is a tie at MED, and OWNER SEQUENCING BREAKS TIES.  SCOPE: five sites are ONE round, because two dispositions in one diff are still one fix design -- bound every unconditional emission in DEVICE_ACCESS(8253). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/; _scratchpad/pit8253_adjudication.md; _scratchpad/i8254.txt:168-172</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — Agent seat, FILE-READING and this roster&#x27;s measuring seat.  VERDICT: DO NOT SHIP AS BRIEFED.  *** ALONE AMONG NINE SEATS IT FOUND pit8253bcdbinary -- the BCD bit accepted at :310 and the count decoded as binary at :181-184, putting counter 0&#x27;s host timer ~4x off. ***  Also: :351/:354 are structurally unreachable (DEV_8253_LENGTH 4, one registration tree-wide, all four relative_addr values named); :198/:200 are member 4 ACCEPT not member 3, because nothing is declined and hz[1]/hz[2] have no consumer; member 7 IMPLEMENT is UNAVAILABLE for :200 since the tree has no audio backend, so the rejection is for availability rather than because a latch is right; FIVE separate named int fields by dev_wdc.c:93-102&#x27;s runtime-mutual-exclusivity discriminator; and clause (d) is applicable though de-escalation is not, with the smallest null-repair mutant named (d-&gt;warned_tone becomes static int warned_tone -- ONE WORD, and every emission-count row stays GREEN because a single-machine probe has one device instance). — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — GPT-6 Astra at max, FILE-READING, 1,268,922 b.  Confirmed dev_wdc.c uses separate named int fields rather than a bitfield; ruled the de-escalation clause INAPPLICABLE and corrected the queue&#x27;s own sentence (it &#x27;reverses its premise&#x27;); INDEPENDENTLY found that the LSB-only arm installs no timer; supplied the witness-ORDERING constraint -- a signature control word DESTROYS the initial-format condition :204/:270 require, because mode_byte is shared across counters; and stated its own limit honestly: it built no witness, so that lifecycle step must not be recorded as completed. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, 17,226 b, fired under PANEL_FIRE_AGY=1 per the 2026-09-15 owner directive.  VERDICT: GO-WITH-CHANGES.  Verified the frozen md5, the C0 clearance, the exit_census figures and the (once) census against source item by item.  *** IT FOUND A FALSE CLAIM IN MY BRIEF: section C2 said 26 files appear in exit_census&#x27;s live per-file list; the real figure is 44. ***  My deriver keyed on a lowercase-and-underscore filename pattern and so silently excluded EVERY filename containing a DIGIT -- dev_8253.c, lk201.c, dev_pcc2.c and fifteen more.  That is the deriver-keyed-on-a-correlate failure this project already records, committed by me inside a brief that cites the rule. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — FILE-READING, 16,579 b.  VERDICT: NOT RATIFY member 3 for all seven.  Seven is a complete grep of the function, not seven guest-drivable floods, not seven declines, not seven IMPLEMENT stubs.  Two sites have no producer in the registered map; two more die after the first control word because mode_byte is shared; and the two named TODOs ACCEPT THE STORE FIRST.  Latch the reachable announces per site, do not decline them; de-escalation inapplicable. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — TRANSPORT: kimi-k3:cloud (the PACKET-FED substitute). the kimi SUBSTITUTE transport, PACKET-FED, 9,369 b -- fired ALONGSIDE the live CLI on this panel, so the kimi seat carries one file-reading and one packet-fed answer and they are recorded BY TRANSPORT as the doctrine requires.  *** IT FOUND pit8253lsbnotimer FROM THE INLINED FUNCTION ALONE: the LSB-only path never installs or updates any timer, so a guest programming RW=01 gets a silently dead counter with no warning at all. ***  Independently corroborated by codex, a file-reading seat, by an entirely different route. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — glm-5.3:cloud, PACKET-FED, 10,525 b.  *** IT PROVED MY C1 PREMISE FROM THE PACKET ALONE, AND MORE ELEGANTLY THAN MY FILE-READ DID: the brief&#x27;s own 18-emissions-per-session figure is itself proof that fatal() does not exit, because a killing fatal() caps the count at 1 and eighteen emissions require seventeen survivals. ***  It also found a detector defect no other seat raised: the emission-counting detector is ONE-SIDED, since a site silently deleted or demoted to debug() passes any &#x27;count &lt;= 1&#x27; row; rows must assert ==1 on first drive AND +0 on immediate repeat.  And it correctly conditioned :351/:354 as UNSETTLEABLE from the packet while naming the missing fact. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — deepseek-v4-pro:cloud, PACKET-FED, 5,571 b.  Marked every claim READ / REASONED / NOT-CHECKABLE throughout, which is the honest form this doctrine asks for.  Refused to settle :351/:354 and named the missing fact precisely (the device&#x27;s registered address range).  Ruled the uniform latched-announce likely wrong for case 1, case 2 and BCD as IMPLEMENT candidates, and named the #490 latch-bit collision BY NUMBER against a single shared latch bit. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — minimax-m3:cloud, PACKET-FED, 7,636 b.  *** IT FOUND THE DERIVATION DEFECT IN MY BRIEF&#x27;S SECTION C1: the debug()-versus-fatal() early-out contrast proves only that -q cannot silence it, and does NOT exclude an exit() after va_debug() returns or inside va_debug itself. ***  Correct, and the conclusion survived only after I went and read the right evidence.  It also separated the seven-in-function claim from the seven-in-file claim, which my brief had conflated. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260917_063557/ (PANEL_COMPLETE 06:52:57, 8/8 scriptable + Opus; the two Agent seats leave no file)</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-open"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">macepciwidth</span><span class="ttl">DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci) has #529&#x27;s width class UNFIXED, 530 lines below the handler #529 fixed -- and it does not decline at all</span><span class="tip">DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci) has #529&#x27;s width class UNFIXED, 530 lines below the handler #529 fixed -- and it does not decline at all — note: filed 2026-09-17 from the repair-14 panel; owes a rung-3 ATTEMPT before any round</span></span><span class="state st-open">open</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">FILED-FROM-R14-PANEL</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;FILED-FROM-R14-PANEL&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** #529 FIXED THE WIDTH CLASS IN DEVICE_ACCESS(crime) AND LEFT THE IDENTICAL CLASS UNTOUCHED IN DEVICE_ACCESS(macepci), IN THE SAME FILE.  The sibling is WORSE: it does not decline at all. *** dev_sgi_ip32.c:728-729 is `if (len != 4) fatal(&quot;[ macepci: unimplemented len %i ]&quot;, len);&#x27; with NO goto, NO return and NO latch -- so a narrow access PRINTS (unlatched, therefore FLOODS) and then FALLS THROUGH INTO THE SWITCH WITH THE WRONG LENGTH, which is precisely the behaviour #529&#x27;s own comment enumerates as the reason its goto exists.  Verified by reading the handler, not taken from the seat. *** IT ALSO CORRECTED A RECORDS DEFECT I HAD JUST MADE WORSE. ***  OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md&#x27;s straddling-access entry cites this site in a list of `register-width devices that REJECT an unexpected length&#x27;.  macepci does NOT reject.  The citation was ALSO stale (:698, moved to :728 by #529&#x27;s own diff) and I corrected only the NUMBER first, leaving the false CLAIM standing -- *a correction is not safer than the thing it corrects*.  Both halves fixed now, and the sibling citations were grepped: dev_sgi_gbe.c:726 and dev_kn01.c:89 both still land on their guards, so the stale population was ONE of three, not three of three. SEVERITY MED, NOT HIGH, and stated honestly: NO WITNESS EXISTS.  Whether a guest driver issues a narrow access to macepci config space is UNKNOWN and #529&#x27;s witness does NOT transfer (it was at CRIME).  A round taking this owes the rung-3 ATTEMPT first.  The tree&#x27;s own answer for this exact shape is #529&#x27;s: DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with `(once)&#x27;. — evidence: GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_sgi_ip32.c; OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md tail entry; regress/artifacts/crimewidthkill/repair14_20260916/opus_pass1.md (the seat that surfaced it)</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-open"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td
