16 chars, the probe prints %-16s, the gate pattern needs two spaces.  Verified against this round&#x27;s preserved 36/36 log at got=0/want=1.  Also found comp1-x-read empty, and that `clip surv&#x27; is vacuous by construction with a measurably false note. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — CONFIRMED — DO NOT CLOSE -- MEASURED M1 (read arm qualified to component 2) and M2 (component 1 far edge absorbed by LINEAR_A) at 36/36 EACH: two TOTAL ESCAPES of a detector that had just come back green.  Also named the VALUE axis (guard reads writeflag; body reads idata) without building it, and prescribed the class-level fix: enumerate the catch-all&#x27;s five inputs once rather than patching a fourth cell. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — MANUAL — DO NOT CLOSE -- named DIRECTION as the missing dimension and, unprompted, the single fact that would DEFEAT its own finding (that comp1 loads cannot reach the catch-all).  Checked: the catch-all else is address-agnostic, so the finding stands.  Also flagged that treating BYTE as representative of all narrow widths assumes the code partitions 8 vs !=8. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — MANUAL — [NOT-FIRED]  *** RESERVED FOR MEASUREMENT by owner ruling.  NOT a seat failure and NOT agreement. *** — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — MANUAL — CLEAN in its VERDICT LINE -- and item 3 of its own BODY describes M2 exactly (`LINEAR_A&#x27;s near bound sliding down by 0x10 eats 0x16f0-0x16ff and leaves clip * green&#x27;), which the opus seat measured as a 36/36 total escape in the same panel.  A seat that FOUND a breach and then ratified closure.  Recorded as a fresh instance of the carrier&#x27;s read-the-body rule, not as agreement. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — MANUAL — DO NOT CLOSE -- found the SAME gate defect INDEPENDENTLY and by a different route, citing lib.sh&#x27;s count() implementation.  Its framing is the one kept: `the layer the diff edited (the gate list) is the layer the harness bypassed.&#x27;  Noted the tree moved mid-review (my uncommitted repair) and said so. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — MANUAL — DOES NOT CLOSE -- named the comp1 read path and supplied the argument that SETTLED it against the two CLEAN verdicts: a standard under which the 36-row detector passes would also have acquitted the 34-row detector, which was measurably breached, so it cannot be the chain&#x27;s standard. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — MANUAL — NOT CLEAN -- named the omission of access type (read vs write) for component 1. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td><td class="c legacy"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">N/A</span><span class="tip">legacy -- verdict unclassified: d = &#x27;N/A&#x27; is outside ✓ ✕ ⏸ …; read the note for the outcome — 2026-08-31 — MANUAL — CLEAN, chain closes.  MINORITY and not adopted; settled by argument rather than tally. — evidence: _scratchpad/panel_20260831_081810</span></span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c hold"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">⏸</span><span class="tip">2026-08-31 — awaiting fable5 — [HELD AWAITING SEAT: fable5]  `#499` closed at `9f666a8` and owes a REGRESS review, which this project reserves to the flagship seat.  **The question is specific, not a re-review of the round:**  **(1)** The 28-row detector is gate-run by `gate_arm.sh` with a SUMMED denominator (`rows emitted` = ok + FAIL) that separates a LOST row from a FAILING one.  Right shape for the battery, and should the two older sibling blocks in the same gate adopt it?  **(2)** 8 of the 28 rows never failed on any of 14 variants -- two are the witness ladder&#x27;s clause-(ii) controls, six are per-window accept replicas filed as coverage debt WITH the mutants that would discharge them.  Is filing right, or does the vacuity floor demand them now?  **(3)** The shipped re-pin of `other decl` hardcodes `0x1800` and `0x12345678`.  If `OUTSIDE` or `store()`&#x27;s default changes, the row goes RED rather than silently green -- the safe direction, but the coupling is real and undocumented in the row&#x27;s comment.</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">holdmarkermulti</span><span class="ttl">gen_codex_wall.py&#x27;s marker regex matches ONE seat name, so a stage held awaiting SEVEN seats is invisible to section I -- the biggest holds are the ones that escape</span><span class="tip">gen_codex_wall.py&#x27;s marker regex matches ONE seat name, so a stage held awaiting SEVEN seats is invisible to section I -- the biggest holds are the ones that escape — commits: 9abd175 — witness: harness:MEASURED against HEAD and working ledgers.  `MARKER = re.compile(r&#x27;\(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged:\s*([a-z0-9_]+)\s*\]&#x27;)` -- the class has no comma, so `(HELD-AWAITING-SEAT/defanged: opus5, codex, grok, kimi, glm, deepseek, minimax)` does not match.  Enumerated: 2 multi-seat markers present, of which `sgirekills` is a genuine live hold owing all seven scriptable seats and is counted NOWHERE.  CLOSED by 9abd175: a strict token grammar plus seat-id validation against the ledger&#x27;s own roster, controlled in both directions -- real ids render clean with empty stderr, an unknown id renders `?token` and names row, phase and token, and the real ledger is unchanged at 8 held rows with no warnings.  Controls preserved at regress/artifacts/holdmarkermulti/controls_20260830/controls.out.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span 