009/probe_commit.out</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE PROBE WAS ATTACKED BEFORE IT WAS EVER RUN, AND IT HAD THREE INDEPENDENT DEFECTS. Verdict: &#x27;It runs.  It does not score.&#x27; ***  (1) **LINE LABELS ARE 16-BYTE ALIGNED** -- `debugger_cmds.c:709` is `addr = addr_start &amp; ~0xf`, so `dump 0x9004` labels the line `0x00009000` AND BLANK-PADS the leading word groups, shifting every group index.  (2) **THE DUMP RENDERS LITTLE-ENDIAN BYTES, NOT A WORD** -- 0x2a prints as `2a000000`.  (3) **`save 2s` READ A SLOT NOTHING WROTE**: its plant put SP at DEST+16 via `str_sp_10` while `classify()` read DEST+8, so ` sp` could NEVER appear and **the row was ALWAYS RED, pre-fix and post-fix alike** -- not a witness of Save at all. *** THE REMEDIES FOR (1) AND (2) ALREADY EXISTED IN THIS TREE AND I DID NOT USE THEM: `arm_bdt_probe.py:123-149` records the alignment measurement from 2026-09-11 in a seven-line comment, and `arm_flags_probe.py:191-193` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- the carrier&#x27;s own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; `armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed `ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and &#x27;SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect&#x27;.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why `arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel&#x27;s own byte pattern hiding the defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table&#x27;s CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not &#x27;fix&#x27; the probe down to it.  REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit first, then this. — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/PROBEATTACK_grok_20260917.txt; regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">gdromcontractresidual</span><span class="ttl">gdrom contract: two same-commit L4s from the chain-closing pass were not applied -- a bare M3 label covering two different mutants, and an undated gdromdmasilent escape record</span><span class="tip">gdrom contract: two same-commit L4s from the chain-closing pass were not applied -- a bare M3 label covering two different mutants, and an undated gdromdmasilent escape record — witness: none -- documented-only.  Both items are RECORDS defects in a shipped contract block: no row, oracle, score or pin moves, which is verified rather than asserted (row-name set diffed against HEAD: identical). — note: [2026-10-02] A MEMBER OF `closeassessrows` (#557, from the census: regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/grok/grok_closedcensus.txt; confirmed regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/aconf_census/codex_aconf_census.txt).  Work still alive: the two contract corrections are NOT at HEAD (regress/gdrom_default_probe.py:56 bare M3, :119 the undated escape record) and the gdromdmasilent re-measurement is OWED (Astra, overturning grok).  The parked 2026-09-17 edit that carries them is NOT landed by #557: Astra&#x27;s review (aconf_parked, 2c) found it also adds two executable rows and tightens a third (54 -&gt; 56 row() sites; the gate pins 60 rows and the working probe makes 62), i.e. a detector change belonging to M2_gdromnodatawidth and gdromacceptdmanum, and its docstring contradicts the gdromdmasilent record in one place; it waits for a round of its own.  The row is NOT re-opened here: that decision is the class row&#x27;s work.  Filed rather than left implied.  (1) DESIGNED TO CATCH parenthesises a bare `M3&#x27; while old M3 relocates the clear past `break&#x27; and repair-B M3_gdromsuccesswidth is `if (len == 4)&#x27; at the success arm -- two mutants, one label.  (2) `gdromdmasilent&#x27; is listed as escaping every row, but arm bytetrig / nodata byte / accept byte all trigger with 0x5a and by reading would now red it; PREDICTED not measured, so DATE the escape record against the table it actually escaped rather than dropping it. *** BOTH L4s APPLIED 2026-09-17; ROW CLOSED AS A RECORDS FIX. ***  (1) the bare `M3&#x27; label is SPLIT into M3a (the original: relocates the shadow clear PAST `break&#x27;) and M3b (repair-B&#x27;s M3_gdromsuccesswidth: `if (len == 4)&#x27; at the SUCCESS arm, a WIDTH conditioning that reds `accept byte&#x27; alone at 61/62), with a note saying the MATRIX and not the bullet is the authority.  (2) the `gdromdmasilent&#x27; escape record is **DATED, NOT DROPPED** -- it escaped the table it was measured against, and the rows added since (`arm bytetrig&#x27;, `nodata byte&#x27;, `accept byte&#x27;) would BY READING red it now.  That is a PREDICTION, and dropping the line would have asserted a re-measurement nobody ran -- the frozen-number class wearing a tidy-up&#x27;s clothes.  The re-measurement is OWED and is named as such.  **VERIFIED AS RECORDS-ONLY, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS SENTENCE WAS FALSE.**  It read *&#x27;the row-name set is byte-identical to HEAD&#x27;s&#x27;*.  It is not: the diff against HEAD shows `accept byte&#x27; and `nodata byte&#x27; ADDED, because THIS SESSION&#x27;s clause-(b) repair added them and moved the gate pin 60 -&gt; 62.  The tree SHOULD differ from HEAD there. *** THE COMPARISON WAS SIMPLY THE WRONG ONE: a records-only claim has to answer &#x27;did MY EDIT move the row set?&#x27;, and I answered &#x27;does the TREE differ from HEAD?&#x27; -- a different question whose answer was always going to be yes, and whose counter-example my own guard printed while I read past it. ***  THE CORRECT GUARD, RUN: **56 `row(&#x27; call sites before and after the edit, indentation-tolerant -- unchanged**, both L4s land inside the module DOCSTRING, and the gate&#x27;s EQUALITY pin at 62 is untouched.  No kill table is perturbed. *Two further correlate slips in that same guard, recorded because they are this session&#x27;s pattern: my &#x27;row sites 54, was 56&#x27; alarm compared `^row\(&#x27; against an earlier `^\s*row\(&#x27; -- two REGEXES, not a lost row; and my &#x27;non-comment lines&#x27; count keyed on `#&#x27;, while the contract block is a docstring whose prose never starts with one.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED —  *** BOTH L4s APPLIED 2026-09-17; ROW CLOSED AS A RECORDS FIX. ***  (1) the bare `M3&#x27; label is SPLIT into M3a (the original: relocates the shadow clear PAST `break&#x27;) and M3b (repair-B&#x27;s M3_gdromsuccesswidth: `if (len == 4)&#x27; at the SUCCESS arm, a WIDTH conditioning that reds `accept byte&#x27; alone at 61/62), with a note saying the MATRIX and not the bullet is the authority.  (2) the `gdromdmasilent&#x27; escape record is **DATED, NOT DROPPED** -- it escaped the table it was measured against, and the rows added since (`arm bytetrig&#x27;, `nodata byte&#x27;, `accept byte&#x27;) would BY READING red it now.  That is a PREDICTION, and dropping the line would have asserted a re-measurement nobody ran -- the frozen-number class wearing a tidy-up&#x27;s clothes.  The re-measurement is OWED and is named as such.  **VERIFIED AS RECORDS-ONLY, AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS SENTENCE WAS FALSE.**  It read *&#x27;the row-name set is byte-identical to HEAD&#x27;s&#x27;*.  It is not: the diff against HEAD shows `accept byte&#x27; and `nodata byte&#x27; ADDED, because THIS SESSION&#x27;s clause-(b) repair added them and moved the gate pin 60 -&gt; 62.  The tree SHOULD differ from HEAD there. *** THE COMPARISON WAS SIMPLY THE WRONG ONE: a records-only claim has to answer &#x27;did MY EDIT move the row set?&#x27;, and I answered &#x27;does the TREE differ from HEAD?&#x27; -- a different question whose answer was always going to be yes, and whose counter-example my own guard printed while I read past it. ***  THE CORRECT GUARD, RUN: **56 `row(&#x27; call sites before and after the edit, indentation-tolerant -- unchanged**, both L4s land inside the module DOCSTRING, and the gate&#x27;s EQUALITY pin at 62 is untouched.  No kill table is perturbed. *Two further correlate slips in that same guard, recorded because they are this session&#x27;s pattern: my &#x27;row sites 54, was 56&#x27; alarm compared `^row\(&#x27; against an earlier `^\s*row\(&#x27; -- two REGEXES, not a lost row; and my &#x27;non-comment lines&#x27; count keyed on `#&#x27;, while the contract block is a docstring whose prose never starts with one.* — evidence: regress/gdrom_default_probe.py contract block; regress/gate_sh_rounding.sh pin unchanged at 62</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">highrankunmeasured</span><span class="ttl">the order between the two open HIGHs rests on real-guest reachability measured for neither -- ip30&#x27;s aborts are on no in-tree path, and armmsr&#x27;s probe is built but unrun</span><span class="tip">the order between the two open HIGHs rests on real-guest reachability measured for neither -- ip30&#x27;s aborts are on no in-tree path, and armmsr&#x27;s probe is built but unrun — witness: none -- documented-only.  ANSWERED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR on both sides: armmsr&#x27;s reachability by mode enumeration (7 handled, 25 unhandled, none producible by a correct in-tree kernel), and ip30&#x27;s by gate_asan_sweep.sh:593 running ip30 green.  The ruling is ip30abortkill first. — note: *** BOTH OPEN HIGHs&#x27; SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is &#x27;just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.&#x27;  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** BOTH OPEN HIGHs&#x27; SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device header says it is &#x27;just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.&#x27;  The defect is real for a guest that touches those addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and `regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently. — evidence: gate_asan_sweep.sh:593; regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py (built, unrun); ledger witness fields</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is &#x27;we do not run that OS here&#x27;; the gap on armmsr is &#x27;that OS would not do this.&#x27;  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr &amp; 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c &#x27;case ARM_MODE_USR26&#x27;` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *&#x27;If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE&#x27;*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#&lt;legal&gt; ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &amp;= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel&#x27;s SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux&#x27;s `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).** — evidence: regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/REACH_grok_20260917.txt; src/cpus/cpu_arm.c:559,606; cpu_arm.h mode names</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">probedeathoracle</span><span class="ttl">census of probes that could report `alive&#x27; for a process that already died -- 0 shipped live probes mis-score; the class was born and closed the same day</span><span class="tip">census of probes that could report `alive&#x27; for a process that already died -- 0 shipped live probes mis-score; the class was born and closed the same day — witness: none -- documented-only, and the CLOSURE IS THE RESULT: a census with its denominator (88 live launchers, 5 with WNOHANG, 1 sampling once, 0 using waitpid-once as the survive oracle) bounding a class rather than leaving it open. — note: *** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT&#x27;d, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width&#x27;s session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT &#x27;has WNOHANG&#x27;.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat&#x27;s own words -- *&#x27;a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings&#x27;* -- and this project&#x27;s addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`&#x27;s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it.</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** CENSUSED 2026-09-17 AND THE CLASS IS CLOSED WITH ITS DENOMINATOR: **0 shipped live probes still mis-score this way.**  The defect was born today and died today. ***  The founding case is `regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py`, which had the exact shape, was MEASURED reporting `alive` for a process that had SIGABRT&#x27;d, and was rewritten to poll the same hour.  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` was built with the polling form from the start. **METHOD, keyed on MECHANISM rather than on names** -- and it explicitly refused to count `artifacts/**` frozen copies, which would have inflated the class about FIFTEENFOLD by re-counting the same five files: live population is `regress/*.py` = **99 files**, minus 10 harness/selftest/census scripts and one source-only probe = **88 launchers**. **THE DEATH-AS-ORACLE FAMILIES, with counts:** EOF-as-alive (`alive = rd(...)`) **33**; sessions borrowed from those 33 **11** (pcc2_default execs pcc2_width&#x27;s session; ten SH4 probes import sh4_pcic_probe); prompt/dump still-answering **3**; `subprocess.run` wait-status **6**; **`WNOHANG` at all: 5** (4 at HEAD plus the uncommitted arm_msrmode). **OF THOSE FIVE, HOW MANY SAMPLE waitpid ONCE?**  `pcc2_width_probe.py` polls 30x0.1s and waitpid only fills `how`; `pcc2_c040_probe.py` the same; `xscale_inten_probe.py` polls 2s in `close()` and sets `self.dead` on EOF during `send()`; `arm_msrmode_probe.py` polls 12s. `luna_absentcpu_probe.py` **does sample once -- and it is NOT the alive oracle**, being called from `died()` only after `step_at` has already failed on EOF.  **ONCE-ONLY IN THE LIVE TREE: 1.  WAITPID-ONCE AS THE SURVIVE ORACLE: 0.** *** AND THE SEAT NAMED THE CONSTRUCTION RATHER THAN THE KEYWORD, which is what makes this reusable: the bug is NOT &#x27;has WNOHANG&#x27;.  It is the three-part combination -- `rd()` returns on an empty `os.read` so the spin is skipped exactly on death; that return is IGNORED; and `alive` is then taken from a single `waitpid(WNOHANG)`.  Any one of the three alone is harmless. ***  **DISPOSITION: NO TREE-WIDE EDIT.**  The seat&#x27;s own words -- *&#x27;a tree-wide rewrite would manufacture findings&#x27;* -- and this project&#x27;s addition licence agree: 33 EOF-as-alive probes consume `rd()`&#x27;s return and are correct as written.  The boundary is recorded so a future round does not rediscover the class and sweep it. — evidence: regress/artifacts/probedeathoracle_20260917/CENSUS_grok_20260917.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">grokampkill</span><span class="ttl">three grok seats lost to a trailing &amp; inside a Bash-tool wrapper -- the documented hazard, hit after nine correct launches, detected by MTIME rather than by size or process count</span><span class="tip">three grok seats lost to a trailing &amp; inside a Bash-tool wrapper -- the documented hazard, hit after nine correct launches, detected by MTIME rather than by size or process count — witness: harness:mtime on the redirect target -- 3 of 5 jobs unwritten for 338-675 s while 2 were written within 80 s, plus a partial ending mid-sentence with no verdict line. — note: *** THREE GROK SEATS WERE LOST TO THE DOCUMENTED PLAIN-`&amp;` HAZARD ON 2026-09-17, AND THE CARRIER DESCRIBES IT EXACTLY. ***  A trailing `&amp;` inside a Bash-tool invocation does not survive: the child is torn down when that invocation&#x27;s shell exits.  I used the correct form -- the grok command in the FOREGROUND of a `run_in_background` wrapper -- for the first nine jobs, then broke it on the tenth by trying to fire TWO seats from ONE wrapper with `&amp;`.  **Knowing the rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems**, which is the carrier&#x27;s own structure-versus-memory thesis arriving against me. **HOW IT WAS DETECTED, because the obvious check is the wrong one:** the files were SMALL but not empty, which the carrier warns is indistinguishable between a stall, a wall and a dead run.  `tasklist` showed 3 live grok processes against 5 jobs, which is suggestive and not decisive because grok spawns more than one process per job.  **The decisive observable was MTIME**: `allocsize` and `records` had been written 61 s and 80 s earlier (alive), while `lunadefault`, `maplebound` and `cpusweep` had not been touched for 338-675 s.  A killed job&#x27;s redirect target stops growing; a live one does not. **AND THE TAIL CONFIRMED IT**: `grok_lunadefault.txt` ended mid-sentence in streaming reasoning -- *&#x27;next I will list every exact `case` versus the board map&#x27;* -- with no verdict line.  That is a STOPPED SEAT, and the standing rule is that **a stopped seat is a SEAT FAILURE, never agreement**: re-fire once, preserve the partial rather than deleting it. All three partials are preserved as `grok_&lt;job&gt;.STOPPED_partial.txt` and all three were re-fired one per wrapper, command in the foreground. *Two jobs fired the same way SURVIVED (`allocsize`, `records`), which is worth recording because it makes the hazard INTERMITTENT rather than deterministic -- and an intermittent failure that leaves a plausible-looking partial is exactly the shape this project keeps misfiling as a seat problem rather than a launcher one.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c pend"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="g">…</span><span class="tip">2026-09-17 — CONFIRMED — *** THREE GROK SEATS WERE LOST TO THE DOCUMENTED PLAIN-`&amp;` HAZARD ON 2026-09-17, AND THE CARRIER DESCRIBES IT EXACTLY. ***  A trailing `&amp;` inside a Bash-tool invocation does not survive: the child is torn down when that invocation&#x27;s shell exits.  I used the correct form -- the grok command in the FOREGROUND of a `run_in_background` wrapper -- for the first nine jobs, then broke it on the tenth by trying to fire TWO seats from ONE wrapper with `&amp;`.  **Knowing the rule and having it fire on the right object are different problems**, which is the carrier&#x27;s own structure-versus-memory thesis arriving against me. **HOW IT WAS DETECTED, because the obvious check is the wrong one:** the files were SMALL but not empty, which the carrier warns is indistinguishable between a stall, a wall and a dead run.  `tasklist` showed 3 live grok processes against 5 jobs, which is suggestive and not decisive because grok spawns more than one process per job.  **The decisive observable was MTIME**: `allocsize` and `records` had been written 61 s and 80 s earlier (alive), while `lunadefault`, `maplebound` and `cpusweep` had not been touched for 338-675 s.  A killed job&#x27;s redirect target stops growing; a live one does not. **AND THE TAIL CONFIRMED IT**: `grok_lunadefault.txt` ended mid-sentence in streaming reasoning -- *&#x27;next I will list every exact `case` versus the board map&#x27;* -- with no verdict line.  That is a STOPPED SEAT, and the standing rule is that **a stopped seat is a SEAT FAILURE, never agreement**: re-fire once, preserve the partial rather than deleting it. All three partials are preserved as `grok_&lt;job&gt;.STOPPED_partial.txt` and all three were re-fired one per wrapper, command in the foreground. *Two jobs fired the same way SURVIVED (`allocsize`, `records`), which is worth recording because it makes the hazard INTERMITTENT rather than deterministic -- and an intermittent failure that leaves a plausible-looking partial is exactly the shape this project keeps misfiling as a seat problem rather than a launcher one.* — evidence: _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_lunadefault.STOPPED_partial.txt _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_maplebound.STOPPED_partial.txt _scratchpad/grokpush_20260917/grok_cpusweep.STOPPED_partial.txt</span></span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Research (fix proposals)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Final review (flagship adjudication)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Regression testing</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td></tr><tr class="fixhead st-closed"><th colspan="10"><span class="hline"><span class="wrap" tabindex="0"><span class="rid">scorehyphenfp</span><span class="ttl">check_score_claims.py read the ordinal pair `entries-4/8` as a score because its lookbehind excluded a digit, x and / but not a hyphen -- and its prescribed remedy would have written a false statement into a probe</span><span class="tip">check_score_claims.py read the ordinal pair `entries-4/8` as a score because its lookbehind excluded a digit, x and / but not a hyphen -- and its prescribed remedy would have written a false statement into a probe — commits: 7440e129 — witness: harness:check_score_claims.py controlled in both directions -- 8 real score claims still match, 6 non-scores rejected; SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS at 30 pinned probes — note: [2026-10-02] COMMITS FIELD FILLED by #557 from the census of closed rows with no commit (regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/grok/grok_closedcensus.txt; Astra&#x27;s git log, regress/artifacts/closeassess_20261002/aconf_census/codex_aconf_census.txt): 7440e129 -- #529&#x27;s commit; touches tools/pipeline/check_score_claims.py.  *** FIXED 2026-09-17 BY OWNER DECISION, WITH A SIBLING AUDIT RATHER THAN A ONE-INSTANCE PATCH. ***  `check_score_claims.py`&#x27;s `SCORE` regex was `(?&lt;![\dx/])(\d+)\s*/\s*(\d+)(?![\d/%])` -- a lookbehind excluding a digit, `x` and `/` **but not a hyphen** -- so the ORDINAL PAIR `entries-4/8` was reported as a stale score claim of 4/8 against a pin of 188. *** THE REASON THIS WAS NOT LEFT ALONE: THE CHECKER&#x27;S OWN PRESCRIBED REMEDY WOULD HAVE SHIPPED A FALSE STATEMENT. ***  Its message reads *&quot;if that is a deliberate historical quote, write &#x27;8-row&#x27; on the same line&quot;* -- and on a line about ARRAY ENTRIES that silences the check by writing something untrue into a probe.  **A check whose repair corrupts the thing it guards** is the shape the carrier already records for the build-tree clearance recipe, arrived at from a new direction. **THE AUDIT, with a denominator, because a correlate-keyed matcher usually has more than one victim:** 710 N/M matches across 121 `regress/` files; the hyphen-aware form keeps **706** and drops exactly **FOUR**, every one an ordinal pair and none a score -- `entries-4/8` (`crime_width_probe.py`), `len-3/5` (`lca_isalane_probe.py` comment 96), `opcode-59/63` (`ppc_halt_probe.py` comment 238), `pass 2-3 / 6-7` (`sgi_de_primop_probe.py` comment 985).  *** THREE OF THE FOUR WERE LATENT: they fire only once their probe gains a gate pin, so the class had four members and one visible face. *** **CONTROLLED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS BEFORE LANDING:** eight real score claims still match (13/13, 26/26, 8/32, `52 / 52`, 16/16, `scored 27/29`, `base 29/29`, `RESULT=6/9`); six non-scores rejected (the four hyphen pairs plus the two pre-existing hex lists).  `SCORE_CLAIMS_PASS` at 30 pinned probes. *The fix is one character in a lookbehind.  What made it worth a round is that the audit converted an anecdote into a population -- and the population was four.*</span></span><span class="state st-closed">closed</span></span></th></tr><tr class="ph st-closed"><th scope="row" class="phlabel">Assessment (initial problem)</th><td class="c none"><span class="g">·</span></td><td class="c 