tools/pipeline/ledger.json:41181:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07 from the codex reachability pass (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt), raised under the\nfound-something-else licence on a brief about something unrelated.\n\ncodex: *\"`swpctx` writes the old context into **a0**, although the supplied ABI wrapper returns **v0**.\nThis independently contradicts the handler's contract.\"*\n\nIf correct, a guest calling the PALcode `swpctx` gets its previous context pointer in the wrong register --\nthe caller reads `v0` and finds whatever was there.  That is a silent wrong value rather than a crash, which\nis the harder kind to notice.\n\n*(SUPERSEDED WITHIN THE HOUR, kept because the record should show the order.  This paragraph read:\n**\"NOT VERIFIED BY ME.  Both citations are given and the check is two file reads; it is filed rather\nthan confirmed because the Alpha queue is already three rows deep tonight and none of them has a\nwitness.  A round taking any Alpha item should settle this one first -- it is the cheapest of the four\nto check.\"**  It WAS the cheapest, it WAS two file reads, and both were done immediately below.)*\n\n*** VERIFIED BY ME 2026-09-07, AND IT IS SHARPER THAN THE SEAT STATED: THE CODE CONTRADICTS THE COMMENT\nDIRECTLY ABOVE IT. ***\n\n    /*  Return old context in v0.  */\n    cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n\nThe comment says v0; the store is to ALPHA_A0.  **ALPHA_V0 exists and is used correctly elsewhere in the same file** -- the PAL dispatch a hundred\nlines up does `cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = a3;' -- so this is not a missing symbol or a naming\nconvention; it is one wrong identifier.  And the ABI wrapper confirms the intent independently:\n`alpha_pal_swpctx' in `src/include/thirdparty/alpha_cpu.h' declares its return register as\n`__asm(\"$0\")' (v0), takes the new context in `__asm(\"$16\")' (a0), and ends `return (v0);'.\n\n**RAISED LOW -> MED and this is now the CHEAPEST CERTAIN FIX ON THE BOARD** -- cheaper than\n[[sgirergbroundtrip]], because the intent is stated on the line above the defect rather than inferred from a\nconsumer.  One token, ALPHA_V0'.\n\n**Its REACHABILITY is still bounded by the same open question as the other Alpha rows** ([[alphapfnoffset]]'s\nboot question) -- a guest must reach a context switch.  But unlike those, the DEFECT is certain without any\nwitness: a comment and its code cannot both be right.\n\n*** CLUSTER SCOPING, codex, 2026-09-08 (ev: regress/artifacts/alphatbinegsel/codex_verify_20260908.txt).\nSIX SEPARATE ROUNDS -- no merger has sufficient evidence. ***\n\n    one fix design : return the old context through the ABI-defined result register\n    one oracle     : returned v0 equals the outgoing context address\n\n**The ordering ruling matters more than the grouping:** codex put **`alphaswpctxreg` FIRST**, on the\nground that it is the only one with verified behaviour AND a decisive witness (a returned register\nvalue), and it labelled that *\"a confidence judgment, not a claim that it has the greatest potential\nseverity\"* -- which is the rung-versus-severity distinction the ladder already insists on, arrived at\nindependently.\n\n*** AND IT REFUTED A GROUPING HYPOTHESIS I HAD FLOATED: \"the claim that `alphapteprot` prevents all\nguest paging is not established by this review.\" ***  I had suggested the page-walk row might be a\nprerequisite for testing the others; that is not supported, and scheduling on it would have been\nscheduling on an assumption.  `alphafaultclaim` and `alphavmkill` may be reconsidered together only\nafter proving they share a file, one fault-handling repair and one fault-delivery oracle --\n*\"correcting a comment and preventing termination do not establish that grouping.\"*\n\n*** FIX DESIGN COMPLETE, codex 2026-09-08 -- AND IT REFUTED THE PREMISE I PUT IN THE BRIEF. ***\n\n**MY Q2 PREMISE WAS WRONG.**  I asked whether this is ONE defect or TWO, suggesting the store to\n`r[ALPHA_A0]` does two wrong things: fails to place the result where the caller reads it, AND\nclobbers the caller's `a0`.  **The clobber is not a defect.**  The wrapper is:\n\n    static __inline unsigned long\n    alpha_pal_swpctx(unsigned long ctx)\n    {\n            register unsigned long a0 __asm(\"$16\") = ctx;\n            register unsigned long v0 __asm(\"$0\");\n            __asm volatile(\"call_pal %2 # PAL_OSF1_swpctx\"\n                    : \"=r\" (a0), \"=r\" (v0)\n                    : \"i\" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx), \"0\" (a0)\n                    : \"$1\", \"$22\", \"$23\", \"$24\", \"$25\", \"memory\");\n            return (v0);\n    }\n\n**`a0` is an OUTPUT tied to an input, so its incoming value need not survive** -- the wrapper\ndiscards that output and returns `v0`.  So it is ONE defect with two register-state effects, and the\none-character edit repairs it without any separate preservation handling.\n\n*And my reading of `\"0\"` was wrong in the other direction too: it refers to output OPERAND ZERO,\nwhich here occupies `$16` (`a0`) -- it does NOT select physical `$0`.  `ALPHA_V0 = 0` and\n`ALPHA_A0 = 16` in `cpu_alpha.h`, so the two happen to look alike in a way that invites exactly this\nconfusion.*\n\n## THE FIX -- one character, and it matches FOUR in-tree siblings\n\n    -\t\tcpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n    +\t\tcpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_V0] = cpu->cd.alpha.ctx;\n\n**The house idiom was found by grepping the fork rather than by reasoning**, as the option-set rule\nrequires.  The closest sibling is `PAL_OSF1_swpipl` (case 0x35) -- *\"a0 = new ipl, v0 = return old\nipl\"* -- which writes the old state into `ALPHA_V0` and then installs the new state from the copied\nlocal `a0`, the identical shape.  `rdmces`, `rdps` and `whami` all return through `ALPHA_V0` as\nwell, as does `PROM_R_PUTS` in `alpha_prom_call()`.\n\n**Q4 -- no in-tree dependency.**  The local `a0` stays live through `cpu->cd.alpha.ctx = a0`, so the\nload-new-context step is unaffected; the emulator caller checks status and PC and never reads\n`r[ALPHA_A0]`; and `src/` contains no calls to the wrapper at all.  *An external guest relying on\nthe accidental old-context value in `a0` would observe a change -- but the wrapper provides no such\nguarantee, so that is not a contract this round breaks.*\n\n## *** THE ORACLE IS DEBUGGER-VISIBLE, WHICH MAKES THE WITNESS CHEAP ***\n\n    observable   existing code   fixed code\n    v0           P (stale)       C  (the outgoing context)\n    a0           C               N  (the incoming argument)\n\n**The oracle is `v0_after == ctx_before`**, and the debugger's `reg` command reaches the Alpha\nregister dump, which prints both registers by name -- **so guest code need not consume the return\nvalue for the two builds to be distinguishable.**  That is a materially easier witness than\n[[alphatbinegsel]]'s, which needs a cached mapping, a PTE remap and everything inside one `step N`.\n\n*** THE VACUITY CONDITION, and it is the same shape as the page-0 trap on the sibling row: the\nobservation requires `P != C` -- the pre-call `v0` must differ from the outgoing context, or a stale\n`v0` happens to look correct. ***  The probe must set `v0` to a distinctive value before the call.\n\n**Q6 -- KEEP the comment, and it owes a row.**  *\"Return old context in v0\"* asserts an observable\npostcondition (the value AND its register), so under this project's rule it is a claim that must be\npinned, not a description.  It makes no claim about preserving `a0`, which is consistent with the\nfinding above.\n\n**STATUS: ready to implement.**  One-character product change, four sibling precedents, a named\noracle with its vacuity condition, and no in-tree dependency.  Nothing has been built or run --\nthis was a source review and the seat said so.\n\n*** THE WARRANT IS STRONGER THAN THE SEAT STATED, AND I RE-DERIVED IT RATHER THAN TRANSCRIBING. ***\n\ncodex named four sibling precedents.  **THE DERIVER, so the next reader re-runs it instead of\ntrusting a literal:**\n\n    grep -n 'ALPHA_V0\\] =' src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n\nOn 2026-09-08 that printed EIGHT return-value sites in this one file -- :118 (PROM_R_PUTS),\n:204 (rdmces), :306 (sysvalue), :339 and :344 (swpipl, rdps), :356 (usp), :361 (whami), :383 (a\nsuccess code).  The same grep for ALPHA_A0 finds the swpctx line under review.\n\n> **SO swpctx IS THE LONE OUTLIER AMONG NINE RETURN-VALUE SITES IN ITS OWN FILE.**  That is a far\n> stronger warrant than \"four siblings agree\", and it is the shape the option-set rule already\n> records from sgireunmapped: *the arm under review was the file's own outlier, and one grep showed\n> it.*\n\nALPHA_V0 is 0 and ALPHA_A0 is 16 (src/include/cpu_alpha.h), so the two constants look confusable in\na way that plausibly explains the original slip -- and explains my own misreading of the \"0\" asm\nconstraint as selecting physical $0.\n\n*The count is written with its deriver rather than as a bare literal, per the counts rule: the\nrounds that consult a number here are the rounds that change it.*\n\n*** A TOOLING NOTE FROM WRITING THIS ENTRY, because it is the fifth instance in one session:\nBACKTICKS IN RECORD PROSE MUST NOT GO THROUGH `python -c`. ***  Bash performs command substitution\non them before python ever sees the string -- this attempt emitted a screenful of\n`:118: command not found` and then a SyntaxError, having also executed lines out of two unrelated\nfiles it happened to glob.  **No write occurred, because python died before reaching it.**  The\nremedy is recorded and I keep not applying it: *record prose goes in a script FILE, written with the\nWrite tool, never inlined into a shell command.*\n\n*** RE-VERIFIED 2026-09-08 WITH A CENSUS, AND THE CENSUS IS WHAT MAKES THIS UNARGUABLE: `:251` IS A\nPOPULATION OF ONE AGAINST A POPULATION OF EIGHT. ***\n\n    grep -n \"ALPHA_V0\\]\" src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n        118  204  306  339  344  356  361  383        <- EIGHT handlers return via V0\n\n    grep -n \"r\\[ALPHA_A0\\] =\" src/cpus/cpu_alpha_palcode.c\n        251                                           <- EXACTLY ONE.  This line.\n\n**So it is not a local convention, not a naming difference and not a missing symbol.**  It is one\nidentifier, in the one place the file does it differently, under a comment that says the opposite.\n\n*** AND THE ABI WRAPPER SETTLES THE INTENT INDEPENDENTLY -- IT DECLARES a0 AS THE REGISTER THE\nGUEST MUST DISCARD. ***  `alpha_cpu.h:449-459`:\n\n    register unsigned long a0 __asm(\"$16\") = ctx;\n    register unsigned long v0 __asm(\"$0\");\n    __asm volatile(\"call_pal %2 # PAL_OSF1_swpctx\"\n        : \"=r\" (a0), \"=r\" (v0)\n        : \"i\" (PAL_OSF1_swpctx), \"0\" (a0)\n        /* clobbers t0, t8..t11, a0 (above) */\n\n`v0` is `$0` and is an OUTPUT; `a0` is an IN-OUT operand the comment explicitly calls clobbered.\n**So the model writes its result into the register the caller is told to throw away, and leaves\nuntouched the one the caller reads.**  A correct guest cannot recover the value at all.\n\n**ORDERING IS CORRECT AND IS NOT PART OF THIS DEFECT** -- `:251` publishes the OLD `ctx` before\n`:255` overwrites it with the new one.  Recorded so a fix does not \"helpfully\" reorder anything.\n\n**WHAT IS STILL OPEN, and the brief asks for each rather than asserting it:** whether the handler\nshould ALSO clobber a0 for fidelity (or whether that is unobservable by construction, since no\ncorrect caller may read it); REACHABILITY -- I have NOT shown any guest this project boots calls\n`swpctx` and consumes the return, and the wrapper existing in the OS header is a different claim;\nand the witness shape, which must seed `v0` with a distinguishing value or \"the handler did nothing\nto v0\" and \"the handler wrote the right thing\" are indistinguishable.\n\n**A CROSS-ROW HAZARD CARRIED FROM `alphatbinegsel`'s panel, recorded on BOTH rows:** `:296` in this\nsame arm is `invalidate_translation_caches(cpu, 0, INVALIDATE_ALL)` under `// TODO: Don't\ninvalidate EVERYTHING!`, and glm warned that **swpctx IS the context-switch PAL, so whichever of\nthe two rows lands SECOND must re-baseline its witness with the other's fix present** or a TBIAP on\nthe switch path confuses attribution between them.  The pass-1 brief asks explicitly whether that\nTODO belongs in this round or is a separate row, under the one-site rule.\n\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxreg/PASS1_brief.md`, fired at codex.  Written in neutral vocabulary\nand checked against the `codexcyberflag` trigger list before firing -- zero hits.\n\n*** PASS 1 (codex): THE FIX IS CORRECT AND SUFFICIENT, KEEP `med`, EXCLUDE THE CACHE TODO -- AND MY\nCENSUS WAS TEXTUALLY RIGHT AND MISLEADING. ***\n\n**THE COUNT WAS INFLATED AND THE METHOD IS THE REASON.**  I reported EIGHT `ALPHA_V0` return sites.\nThree of them -- 306, 356, 383 -- are **inside `#if 0`**, and 118 is in `alpha_prom_call`, not the\nPAL handler.  **Five compiled assignments file-wide, four in `alpha_palcode`.**\n\n> *I grepped for textual presence and reported it as a population of compiled code.*  That is this\n> project own deriver rule landing on me twice in one day: **key on what makes a member a member,\n> not on a correlate of it.**  The conclusion is untouched -- the ABI wrapper is the decisive\n> evidence, and `:251` is still the only `r[ALPHA_A0] =` in the file -- but the number was wrong,\n> and a wrong number gets reused.\n\n**Q1 -- FIX CONFIRMED, AND MY FRAMING CORRECTED.**  `ALPHA_A0` -> `ALPHA_V0`, nothing more: the\nhandler captured the incoming `a0` in a local at entry, so `ctx = a0` still receives the new\ncontext.  **No additional `a0` write is required** -- the architecture marks `a0` UNPREDICTABLE and\nthat definition *explicitly permits a register to remain unchanged*; \"clobbered\" removes a\npreservation guarantee, it does not require destruction.  **And my \"unobservable by construction\"\nwas TOO STRONG:** software CAN read `a0`, it simply cannot require a particular value.  So there is\nno second fidelity defect here.\n\n**Q2 -- KEEP `med`.**  Returning stale register contents violates a defined primitive and merits\nrepair, but no demonstrated consumer, resulting corruption or isolation failure supports a raise.\n**It also corrected my severity prose in the other direction:** *\"wrong value rather than a crash\"*\ndescribes only the IMMEDIATE effect -- a consumer could crash later, and neither that nor\nharmlessness is established.\n\n**Q3 -- REACHABLE, and there is already execution evidence I did not know about.**  Route:\n`decoder -> alpha_instr_call_pal -> alpha_palcode -> swpctx arm`, permitted in kernel mode and\nrejected in user mode.  **`#507`'s own alphabook1 result records TWO COMPLETED `swpctx` CALLS with\nsuccessful PCB checks** (`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/probe_fix_20260907.out`) -- so the\nprimitive is reached and returns today; that run simply never tested `v0`.  No verified OS boot is\nknown to CONSUME the return value, and codex was explicit that this *\"does not establish\nunreachability or rule out a rung-3 witness.\"*\n\n**Q4 -- THE ORACLE NEEDS THREE DISTINGUISHABLE VALUES, and \"v0 changed\" is not enough.**  Establish\nan independently known old context `C`, a distinct incoming context `N`, and seed `v0` with a\nsentinel `S`.  **Expected `v0 = C`; the shipped code leaves `v0 = S`; an incorrect repair returning\nthe NEW context gives `v0 = N`.**  A row asserting only that v0 changed would pass the incorrect\nrepair.\n\n**Q5 -- EXCLUDE the `// TODO: Don't invalidate EVERYTHING!` at `:296`** from this round, per the\none-site rule.\n\n*** PASS 1 CLOSED: SIX SEATS, UNANIMOUS ON THE FIX, THE SEVERITY AND THE SCOPE. ***\n\n    codex     fix correct and SUFFICIENT; keep med; exclude :296\n    grok      same, and adds: a rung-3 planted-guest witness IS establishable\n    glm       defect confirmed FROM THE PACKET ALONE; sufficiency unproven from the packet\n    kimi      fix correct and sufficient; the ordering premise hides a fork\n    deepseek  fix correct and sufficient; reachability not settleable from the packet\n    minimax   real defect, correct fix; ordering premise load-bearing\n\n**Unanimous: `ALPHA_A0` -> `ALPHA_V0`, nothing more; keep `med`; do NOT add an `a0` write; and the\n`:296` cache TODO is a SEPARATE row.**\n\n*** THE PANEL CAUGHT A PREMISE I ASSERTED AND NEVER EXHIBITED, AND THREE SEATS FOUND IT\nINDEPENDENTLY. ***  My brief said *\"Ordering in the handler is correct\"* and showed only `:251` and\n`:255`.  **glm, kimi and minimax all refused to grant it**, and kimi named the fork exactly: *if\n`:255` reads the REGISTER rather than a captured local, then `:251` clobbers it and the live defect\nis \"swpctx is a silent no-op\"* -- a different and larger bug that the same one-token fix repairs.\n\n**VERIFIED BY OPENING THE FILE: `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:165` is\n`uint64_t a0 = cpu->cd.alpha.r[ALPHA_A0], a1 = ...` -- a LOCAL captured at function entry.**  So the\npremise is TRUE and the fork does not exist; but it was true by luck of my having read it earlier,\nnot by anything the brief showed.  *A brief that asserts an ordering property owes the capture.*\n\n*** MY CENSUS DEFECT WAS FOUND TWICE, INDEPENDENTLY, AND THE TWO SEATS AGREE EXACTLY. ***  codex:\nfive compiled assignments file-wide, four in `alpha_palcode`.  grok, separately: *\"Live PAL returns\nin this file are four: rdmces, swpipl, rdps, whami\"* plus `:118` in `alpha_prom_call` -- **the same\nfive.**  Three of my eight are inside `#if 0`.\n\n**glm's triangulation point is the one to keep for the record:** the verdict holds *even if both\ngreps were wrong*, because the comment, the code and the third-party ABI wrapper agree\nindependently.  The census is corroboration, not the load-bearing evidence -- which is the right\nway round, and is why an inflated count did not change the answer.\n\n**grok also sharpened the wrapper reading:** `\"0\" (a0)` ties the input to OPERAND 0, which is `$16`,\nnot `$0`.  My brief's description of a0 as an in-out operand is right; the constraint binding is\nwhat that clause expresses.\n\n*** TWO grok ANSWERS WENT UNREAD, AND ONE OF THEM GAVE THE STOPPING ANSWER THREE PASSES BEFORE I\nASKED FOR IT.  Found by a truncation census run after an unrelated seat failure. ***\n\n**THE MECHANISM, and it is a documented trap I quoted and then walked into twice.**  I copied each\npanel directory into `regress/artifacts/` while **grok was still streaming**, so the durable\nartifacts held:\n\n    pass 3   preserved 1,017 of 10,649 bytes    (10%)\n    pass 5   preserved 1,340 of 11,718 bytes    (11%)\n    pass 4   8,937 of 8,937                     complete -- grok had finished\n    pass 6   9,162 of 9,162                     complete\n\nThe carrier's own rule is *\"a preserved log is verified by its COMPLETION SENTINEL, not by its\nexistence\"*.  **And the live `_scratchpad` copies were incomplete at the moment I read them too**,\nwhich is why the verdicts never reached the record: at pass 3 grok's file was 709 bytes when I\ntallied the panel, and at pass 5 it was 1,030.\n\n*** WHAT WAS IN THEM. ***\n\n**PASS 3 -- `VERDICT: CHAIN CLOSES.  Zero floor breaches on the shipped composite.  Denominator\nbelow.  Do not open a product mutant, a third `swpctx`, or another pass to chase apparatus\nhypotheticals this binary does not emit.`**  It independently derived that clause **(d) enumerated\nthe empty set**, naming the dominating predicates as the function-entry `userMode` gate and the\n`switch (palcode)` arm -- the same conclusion grok reached at pass 2 and which I recorded then.\n\n**PASS 5 -- `VERDICT: NOT CLOSED.  The four binaries still score as advertised.  The helper does\nnot.  response_after + t.find lost coverage.`**  That is codex's pass-5 finding reached\nINDEPENDENTLY, and my receipt for that pass called the not-closed verdict *\"unanimous among the\nlanded seats\"* while grok's confirming answer sat unread on disk.\n\n> **THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT THAT PASSES 4-6 WERE WASTED -- THEY EACH BOUGHT A REAL DEFECT, AND ONE OF\n> THEM (the coloured prompt) IS SOMETHING THIS BINARY DOES EMIT.  IT IS THAT grok'S DISCRIMINATOR\n> WAS SHARPER THAN MY STOPPING QUESTION AND ARRIVED THREE PASSES EARLIER.**  *\"Apparatus\n> hypotheticals this binary does not emit\"* separates the colour case (emitted under CLICOLOR --\n> worth a pass) from the order variants (synthetic transcripts the pty never produces -- not worth\n> one).  I asked at pass 6 for a rule and had been given one at pass 3.\n\n**THIS IS THE THIRD PARTIAL-READ FAILURE OF THE DAY AND THE MECHANISM IS DIFFERENT EACH TIME:** a\nrow's `witness` FIELD unread while its prose was read; a `state_note` read to 1,500 characters and\nacted on; and now a seat answer preserved and read while still being written.  *The common shape is\nnot carelessness about one artifact -- it is treating a record as complete because it was present.*\n\n**REPAIRED:** both artifacts re-copied at full length and verified by their closing sentences, and\nboth verdicts recorded above as ANSWERED rather than left as the silent gaps they were.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:64352:  "_TENSE": "*** `state` IS PRESENT-TENSE; `closure.note` IS PAST-TENSE. *** The note is an EVENT LOG of what happened to a STAGE ('CLOSE (assess)' closes the assess stage). `state` asserts what the ROW still owes. **They are independent BY DESIGN** -- 23 rows share the string `CLOSE (assess)` across three states, and that is the ledger working correctly, not a defect: rows that experienced the same stage event legitimately differ in what else they owe. NEITHER can be derived from the other, and both attempts to do so have been measured wrong.",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:64359:  "_RETROACTIVE_OR_FORWARD_ONLY": "*** THE FIRST DRAFT SAID FORWARD-ONLY FLAT, AND THAT WAS HALF RIGHT IN THE LOAD-BEARING HALF. *** The split is by LAYER, and the block's own _TENSE ruling decides it: **the MEANING binds retroactively; only MECHANICAL CHECKS are forward-only by date.** `state` is PRESENT-TENSE, and a present-tense field has no era -- a false state is false NOW. 'Pre-adoption rows are read charitably' would preserve the exact defect this block exists to end (the es438 reader reads 'not done' off shipped, witnessed work TODAY) and would keep the ledger permanently bilingual. Forward-only enforcement follows the boilerplate-check precedent: a rule that retro-fails the archive gets disabled, and a disabled rule protects nothing. *** THE DISHONESTY WORRY DISSOLVES ONCE THE LAYERS ARE SEPARATED: `state` is a LIVE FIELD, NOT HISTORY. *** Correcting it rewrites no history -- `entries`, notes and `commits` are untouched. The migration is the warranted in-place repair already used twice on 2026-08-25: correct the state cell and attach a warrant note naming the row's OWN evidence. NO BULK REWRITE -- the round's central finding stands (state is NOT derivable; five seats agreed on only 4 of 11 rows), so every remaining row gets a substance read.",
